/* maxima_rubi_utils.mac — runner + noun forms + (later) shims and ported
 * predicates. Loaded by maxima_rubi.mac. House rules: AGENTS.md + T5 §4;
 * the measured traps of T2 §3 are the reason for %mr_b, the block-local
 * rebind in the rule shape, and the never-kill naming policy. */

rubi_verbose : false$

/* Port of ShowStepRoutines.m :3  SimplifyFlag = True; — the class-1
 * rule 1.4.1's ShowStep guard references it; the default is on, and
 * the harness never switches it. */
mr_simplify_flag : true$

/* Capture lookup on the [var = value] equation list (order unspecified,
 * measured T2 §3.6). Quoted symbol on both sides (house rule 1). */
geteqR(mm, nm) := block([],
  if length(mm) = 0 then false
    else if is(part(part(mm, 1), 1) = nm) then part(part(mm, 1), 2)
         else geteqR(rest(mm), nm))$

/* Boolean resolver (house rule 2): is()'s third value unknown is not true. */
%mr_b(e) := block([v],
  v : is(e),
  if v = true then true else false)$

/* Package noun forms (house rule 10 — distinct from the fall-through
 * integrate noun and from corpus-expected nouns). */
mr_unintegrable(f, x) := 'unintegrable[f, x]$

/* Rubi's finite Sum, concretized (2026-08-25 divergence fix). Maxima
   evaluates function arguments eagerly, so the generator hands the
   summand over in held form: a NON-identifier summand arrives as
   lambda([var], <summand>) and is applied per index (the body then
   evaluates the total package functions, e.g. %mr_coeff(Pq, x, 2*k),
   at a CONCRETE index — the eager call with k free returns 0 and
   destroyed every even/odd-split sum, leaving degenerate
   mr_sum[0,k,0,0] nouns in answers); a bare IDENTIFIER summand (the
   Rubi Module-local-u shape, 1.1.3.1 r13 family) is resolved through
   its value, which carries the index: ev(subst(i, var, ev(fun))). A
   lambda body of one bare symbol does NOT get the index bound into
   the value (measured 2026-08-25, probe mech_decisive D1) — hence the
   two paths. Numeric bounds: per-integer i over ceiling(lo)..floor(hi);
   an empty range is 0. Non-numeric bounds keep the noun. A plain
    (already-evaluated, non-lambda) expression summand — hand-written
    call sites only, never the generator's — gets the index substituted
    and re-evaluated. The lambda test must compare against the UNQUOTED
    symbol: in this build op() on a lambda form only compares true with
    `lambda` — `op(lam) = "lambda"` is false, while for ordinary
    operators the string idiom is the one that holds; symbols and
    strings never compare equal here (measured 2026-08-25, probes
    op_cmp_probe / op_typeof_probe, Maxima 5.50.0 SBCL). A dead lambda
    branch is worse than an error: subst then descends into the lambda
    and fatals replacing its parameter. */
mr_sum(fun, var, lo, hi) := block([a, b, i, s, t],
  if numberp(lo) and numberp(hi) then (
    a : ceiling(lo),
    b : floor(hi),
    if a > b then 0
    else (
      s : 0,
      for i : a thru b do (
        if atom(fun) then t : ev(subst(i, var, ev(fun)))
        else if (op(fun) = lambda or op(fun) = "lambda") then t : apply(fun, [i])
        else t : ev(subst(i, var, fun)),
        s : s + t
      ),
      s
    )
  )
  else 'mr_sum[fun, var, lo, hi])$

/* Recursion cap (T5 §1 candidate; re-tuned in Task 9). A runaway
 * replacement becomes the fall-through noun, not a stack overflow. */
%mr_max_depth : 16$

/* Kill-switch for the boolean-leak misfire check (debug). */
%mr_boolcheck : true$

/* Ordered first-match-wins whole-expression dispatch (T2 §4.2). rl is a
 * list of rule functions r(f, x) -> replacement | false. The parameter is
 * named rl, never rules (protected symbol, measured 2026-08-20). */
/* MEASURED Maxima gotcha (2026-08-20): a `return(value)` inside a `for` loop
   does NOT return from the enclosing block in this build — it is loop-level,
   so the block fell through to the trailing `false` and dispatch ALWAYS
   returned false (rubi() was a silent pass-through to integrate). The fix:
   track the firing result in `ans`, use a BARE `return()` to break the `for`
   loop, and return `ans` after the loop. */
/* A boolean (true/false) anywhere in a replacement is a port bug, not a
   result: it means a predicate's `false` sentinel (e.g.
   %mr_polyRemainder on a non-polynomial) flowed into the repl's
   arithmetic, where Maxima booleans do NOT collapse to 0/1 — 126*false
   stays 126*false (measured 2026-08-25, 5.50.0/SBCL: 1.2.2.6 r8 on
   1.2.2.6 e123, m = -2, x^m*Pq non-polynomial -> the .m rule is
   vacuous there in Mathematica — PolynomialRemainder stays a noun and
   the firing dead-ends — but the port computed d = %mr_coeff(false, x,
   0) = false and the corrupted terms reached the final answer as
   `false` factors under a deferred integrate noun). The faithful
   port behavior for a vacuous firing is a DECLINE, so any boolean in
   the replacement downgrades the fire to a misfire and lets the next
   rule (or the integrate fall-through) handle the integrand. No
   early exit: `return` inside a `for` is loop-level in this build
   (measured 2026-08-20, the %mr_dispatch ans/return() note above). */
/* The boolean test is by STRING, not by = (measured 2026-08-25,
   5.50.0/SBCL): a `false` factor inside a Times node is retrieved by
   part() as an atom whose string is "false" but which is NEITHER
   =-nor # -equal to the boolean false NOR to the symbol 'false
   (a distinct reified object — identical() is an unevaluated noun
   here, featurep misses it, is(p = false)/is(p = 'false) both
   false, is(string(p) = "false") true) — so only the name test
   catches all three presentations (literal boolean, symbol, and the
   part()-reified factor). Atoms are cheap; the non-atom arm never
   calls string(). */
/* Control structures are OPAQUE leaves (measured 2026-08-25): generated
   replacements legally CONTAIN booleans as code — e.g. the 1.4.1 r70
   repl carries an (if is(...) = true then (...) else false) that
   evaluates away when the condition decides, and stays boolean-bearing
   when it does not. Walking into if/block/lambda bodies misfired every
   such rule (1.2.1.3 e1058 verified -> unverified). A leaked boolean
   instead sits under arithmetic (+ * / ^) or a list/noun — descending
   everything EXCEPT the control heads is the right cut. */
%mr_containsBoolean(e) := block([i, hit],
  if atom(e) then is(string(e) = "true") or is(string(e) = "false")
  else if op(e) = "if" or op(e) = "block" or op(e) = "lambda"
    or op(e) = "let" or op(e) = "letf" or op(e) = "do"
    or op(e) = "for" or op(e) = "while" or op(e) = "when"
  then false
  else if op(e) = "-" then (
    /* a negation node: length() reports 2 but part(e, 2) ERRORS in
       this build (measured 2026-08-25: part(-(1/2), 2) killed the
       walk on the 1.2.2.1 r13 repl's elliptic_f(atan(x), -1/2) — the
       1.2.1.3 e1058 cascade silently derailed). Only part 1 exists. */
    %mr_containsBoolean(part(e, 1))
  )
  else (
    hit : false,
    for i : 1 thru length(e) do (
      if %mr_containsBoolean(part(e, i)) then hit : true
    ),
    hit))$

%mr_dispatch(f, x, rl, depth) := block([i, r, res, ans, leak, bc],
  ans : false,
  for i : 1 thru length(rl) do (
    r : part(rl, i),
    /* A rule that CRASHES on a binding is a misfire, not a result. Maxima's
       matcher degenerate-binds zero coefficients (a monomial read as the
       binomial 0 + c*x), and the affected rule's condition/replacement then
       divides by 0 (measured 2026-08-24: the 1_1_1_2_r34 / 1_2_1_2_r134 /
       1_2_3_5_r20 family killed the process across the full class-1 run).
       A crashing rule must decline so the next rule (or the integrate
       fall-through) handles the integrand. errcatch in this build returns
       [value] on success and [] on error (probe-errcatch-semantics). */
    res : errcatch(apply(r, [f, x])),
    if res = [] then res : false else res : part(res, 1),
    leak : false,
    if %mr_boolcheck and res # false then (
      bc : errcatch(%mr_containsBoolean(res)),
      if bc = [] then (
        if rubi_verbose then
          print("rubi: rule ", string(r), " BOOLWALK-CRASH on ", string(f)),
        leak : true
      )
      else leak : part(bc, 1)
    ),
    if leak then (
      if rubi_verbose then
        print("rubi: rule ", string(r), " misfire (boolean leaked) on ", string(f)),
      res : false
    ),
    if res # false then (
      if rubi_verbose then
        print("rubi: rule ", string(r), " fired on ", string(f)),
      ans : res,
      return()
    )
  ),
  ans)$

/* The recursion counter increments on the way in and decrements on the way
 * out, so nested mr_int calls share one depth budget. A runaway replacement
 * becomes the fall-through noun, not a stack overflow. */
depth_level : 0$

/* No-op / self-match loop guard (2026-08-24, divergence loop). A rule whose
   replacement re-dispatches the SAME integrand — e.g. the 1.4.2
   normalization rules, whose repl is Px*ExpandToSum[z]^q*ExpandToSum[u]^p,
   the identity when z,u are already a binomial/trinomial, so the term
   re-matches the rule forever — would spin to the depth cap, a full ~2705-
   rule scan per level, before the integrate fall-through. The expanded
   single-term rationals the linearity split produces for 1.1.1.3
   (a+bx)^m(c+dx)^n(e+fx)^p hit this and blew the 30s canary cap (1.1.1.3
   e1276: the 500 x^5/(3x+2)^8 term cycled 14x identically). Cut the cycle at
   the first repeat and fall through now. member/ = is the membership test
   (isequal and identical do NOT evaluate to a boolean in this build —
   measured 2026-08-24, they stay unevaluated nouns). */
%mr_seen : []$

/* The top-level entry. fb gates the two top-level fall-throughs
 * (recursion cap, no rule fired). fb = false — the default `rubi(f, x)` —
 * returns the package no-answer noun instead of calling integrate: a
 * top-level 0-firing is a port/matcher-gap signal (2026-08-24 divergence
 * loop), not a job for Maxima's integrate, which is slow on generic
 * parameters and prompts for sign assumptions (one standalone run 92 s
 * before EOF, measured 2026-08-24). fb = true restores the status-quo
 * integrate fall-through.
 *
 * The flag governs ONLY the top level. mr_int — the entry the generated
 * rule replacements call for NESTED sub-integrals — keeps the status-quo
 * integrate fall-through: a nested 0-firing is a different case, and
 * corpus entries currently verified through nested fallback must not
 * regress (2026-08-24 design decision, handoff Step 1 open question). */
/* Native-integrate fall-through design (measured 2026-08-25): the
   integrate call in the fb=true branches below is BARE — deliberately
   not errcatched. (a) Sign prompts are a designed input channel: the
   test harness (corpus_class1_driver.build_text) runs with
   batch_answers_from_file true and pre-queues prompt answers (six
   "pos" + six "no" lines; the flag must be set via -p preload, not in
   the batch) — retrieve() reads the next batch line as the answer, so
   integrate returns an assumption-conditional result the harness's
   formal diff check verifies. (b) An ERRORING integrate (a Lisp-level
   crash, or the uncatchable RETRIEVE input-EOF when the 12 queued
   answers are exhausted) must PROPAGATE: it kills the rule's
   replacement, %mr_dispatch's errcatch turns that into a misfire, and
   the next rule gets the integrand. errcatching it instead bakes the
   no-answer noun into the answer and regresses targets that the
   declining rule's successor handles (measured 2026-08-25: broad
   canary 88/32 -> 86/34, five expected/no-answer -> unverified
   flips, e.g. 1.1.4.3 e119 A/B'd against the bare call). The
   seen-guard branch below is the SAME bare call — the productive
   loop resolution, not a fall-through (see there). */
/* Normalization-aware loop guard helper. %mr_seen membership alone
   (exact match) misses the measured float/rat normalization cycle:
   (104*(3/10*x+17/10)^4)/3 and (104*(0.3*x+1.7)^4)/3 are the same
   integrand in two forms — member() = false, is(f2 = f1) = false,
   ratsimp(f2-f1) = 0 (measured 2026-08-25, /tmp/opencode/looptest).
   The r25/r7 reduction chain 1.1.1.4 e1 walks that cycle: the seen
   list then grows one drifted copy per lap and the guard never fires
   (e1 timed out at 500 s, 2.5M-line trace). The per-dispatch cost is
   O(depth) ratsimps on small polynomials — depth caps at
   %mr_max_depth = 16. */
%mr_seenp(f) := block([looped, d, i],
  looped : false,
  if member(f, %mr_seen) then return(true),
  for i : 1 thru length(%mr_seen) do
    if is(looped) = false then (
      d : errcatch(ratsimp(part(%mr_seen, i) - f)),
      /* errcatch in this build returns [value] / []
         (probe-errcatch-semantics); the %mr_dispatch idiom */
      if d # [] and is(part(d, 1) = 0) then looped : true
    ),
  looped)$

mr_top(f, x, fb) := block([ans],
  depth_level : depth_level + 1,
  if depth_level > %mr_max_depth then (
    depth_level : depth_level - 1,
    if fb then return(integrate(f, x))
    else return(mr_unintegrable(f, x))
  ),
  if %mr_seenp(f) then (
    depth_level : depth_level - 1,
    /* A re-dispatch of an integrand already on the dispatch path is
       a pure rule loop (static table, deterministic matcher -> the
       same rule re-fires; e.g. the 1.1.1.2 r12 ExpandIntegrand-
       identity repl). MEASURED 2026-08-25: the fb fall-through to
       bare integrate(f, x) is LOAD-BEARING, not a bug — targets
       whose nested sub-integrals loop (1.1.4.3 e119: ten seen-guard
       hits on its productive 1.3.3/1.4.1/1.3.2 cascade) build their
       VERIFIED answer from those integrate results (assumption-
       conditional, from the driver's queued prompt answers). The
       seen guard fires once per cycle (it returns the integrate
       result instead of re-dispatching), so there is no per-cycle
       re-entry to cut. An ERROR here instead (measured same day)
       declines the outer rule and drops those targets to unverified
       (broad canary 88/32 -> 86/34). The manual-batch prompt-EOF
       death ("RETRIEVE: End of file encountered." when no answer
       lines are queued) is inherent to bare integrate in a batch —
       vanilla Maxima integrate dies identically — and is not
       regressed or fixed here. */
    if fb then return(integrate(f, x))
    else return(mr_unintegrable(f, x))
  ),
  %mr_seen : cons(f, %mr_seen),
  ans : %mr_dispatch(f, x, mr_rule_table, depth_level),
  %mr_seen : rest(%mr_seen),
  depth_level : depth_level - 1,
   /* Pass 2 — reverse commutative factor scan (2026-08-26, W1). Maxima's
      matcher picks, for a pattern factor with a non-atomic base, the FIRST
      ^-factor of the target in the REVERSED stored-factor order, with no
      backtracking (matrun.lisp findfun): a monomial-power factor pattern
      like (c x)^m fires only when that factor happens to be stored last —
      data-dependent (see maxima_rubi_dispatch.lisp %mr_dispatch_rev for the
      full diagnosis). The rescan is TOP-LEVEL, fb=false ONLY: nested
      0-firings keep the integrate fall-through (design note above),
      and pass 1 is bit-identical, so nothing currently passing can
      regress. (2026-08-27: the 9.1 port's nested re-dispatches keep
      the compensation via the dedicated rubi_hybrid entry — see
      rules/class1/9_1.mac and the rubi_hybrid note below; lifting
      the fb gate here instead was measured to hang on large
      integrands — 1.1.2.6 e20 looped in pattern recompilation
      past 900 s.) */
   if ans = false and not fb then
     ans : %mr_dispatch_rev(f, x, mr_rule_table, depth_level),
   /* Pass 3 — implicit exponent-1 rescan (2026-08-27). Maxima strips a
      power's exponent 1 at construction and never restores it in
      matching (matcom.lisp compilematch head check; matrun.lisp
      findfun explicit-head search), so a family rule with a (...)^m
      factor 0-fires on a target factor stored bare (x, (a+b*x),
      5*x*(a+b*x)...) where the identical target integrates in Rubi.
      %mr_dispatch_i1 (maxima_rubi_implicit1.lisp) rescans the table
      with the findfun shadow active and, on a 0-firing, on the whole
      integrand wrapped in one raw (mexpt f 1) object. TOP-LEVEL,
      fb=false ONLY, after passes 1-2 0-fire: nested mr_int dispatches
      run with the gate off (verbatim original findfun control flow),
      so nothing currently passing can change. (2026-08-27: the 9.1
      port's nested re-dispatches keep the compensation via the
      dedicated rubi_hybrid entry — see rules/class1/9_1.mac and the
      rubi_hybrid note below; lifting the fb gate here instead was
      measured to hang on large integrands — 1.1.2.6 e20 looped in
      pattern recompilation past 900 s.) */
   if ans = false and not fb then
     ans : %mr_dispatch_i1(f, x, mr_rule_table, depth_level),
  if ans = false then (
    if fb then integrate(f, x)
    else mr_unintegrable(f, x)
  ) else ans)$

/* Nested sub-integral entry (generated replacements call mr_int by
 * name): the status-quo integrate fall-through, exactly what mr_int
 * did before the fallback gate. */
mr_int(f, x) := mr_top(f, x, true)$

/* Two-arg rubi is the rules-only entry (fast no-answer noun on a
 * 0-firing). The integrate fall-through is the separate explicit entry
 * rubi_fallback(f, x, fb). MEASURED (2026-08-24, this build): Maxima
 * has NO arity overloading — a second same-name definition with a
 * different arg count REPLACES the first (the 2-arg call then errors
 * "Too few arguments supplied to rubi(f, x, fb)") — and no variadic
 * syntax (`b...` is a parse error; `argc`/`arglist` have no manual
 * entries), so the two modes must have distinct names. */
rubi(f, x) := mr_top(f, x, false)$
rubi_fallback(f, x, fb) := mr_top(f, x, fb)$

/* The 9.1 re-dispatch entry (rules/class1/9_1.mac calls it from every
 * re-dispatching replacement). The .m's nested Int had the TOP LEVEL'S
 * full matcher power AND its own residue behavior; no existing entry
 * has both halves: mr_int (fb=true) has the integrate fall-through
 * (the run-5 load-bearing native answers on stuck mquot sub-integrals,
 * e.g. the 1.4.3 r19 T4-cascade x^(n-1)/(2e)) but runs passes 2-3
 * OFF (a bare-factor sub-integral loses the lift — 1.1.2.6 e20
 * regresses exactly that way); rubi (fb=false) keeps passes 2-3 but
 * yields the mr_unintegrable noun (no native). This entry mirrors
 * mr_top with the pass gates lifted and the integrate fall-through
 * on both the seen-guard hit and the 0-firing: the 9.1 re-dispatch
 * keeps whatever rescue the sub-integral had, in either nesting
 * context. A plain mr_top parameterization was rejected: the fb flag
 * couples the pass gate and the fall-through, and changing the
 * coupling for the family entries is the measured-hang configuration
 * (2026-08-27, 1.1.2.6 e20 looped in pattern recompilation past
 * 900 s with the gates lifted process-wide). */
/* mode = "alg": the full %mr_seenp (exact member + the ratsimp
 * algebraic comparison — the float/rat normalization-drift guard,
 * 891240b, load-bearing). mode = "exact": the exact member check
 * only. The exact mode is for the 9.1 COLLAPSE re-dispatches
 * (rules/class1/9_1.mac r7, r22-r26, r28, r29) whose replacement
 * is algebraically EQUAL to the calling integrand in a different
 * stored form (the perfect-discriminant quadratic collapse, the
 * proportional-linear monic rewrite, the like-term combine) — the
 * algebraic comparison false-positives as a loop (measured
 * 2026-08-27: 1.2.1.3 e839 — x^m(A+B x)/(a+b x)^2 stored expanded;
 * r28 collapses it factored; the seen guard ratsimps the difference
 * to 0 and bails to the native noun, while the .m's cascade would
 * hand the factored form to the 1.1.1.3 family). The collapse rules
 * are one-shot on their own output (the collapsed form cannot
 * re-trigger their pattern), so the exact mode cannot self-cycle;
 * the depth cap bounds any foreign cycle, and the drift-cycle guard
 * stays intact on every non-collapse entry (the measured drift
 * chain runs family rules on mr_int, never through these). */
%mr_hybrid_body(f, x, mode) := block([ans, sp],
  sp : if mode = "exact" then is(member(f, %mr_seen) # false)
       else %mr_seenp(f),
  depth_level : depth_level + 1,
  if depth_level > %mr_max_depth then (
    depth_level : depth_level - 1,
    return(integrate(f, x))
  ),
  if is(sp) then (
    depth_level : depth_level - 1,
    return(integrate(f, x))
  ),
  %mr_seen : cons(f, %mr_seen),
  ans : %mr_dispatch(f, x, mr_rule_table, depth_level),
  if ans = false then
    ans : %mr_dispatch_rev(f, x, mr_rule_table, depth_level),
  if ans = false then
    ans : %mr_dispatch_i1(f, x, mr_rule_table, depth_level),
  %mr_seen : rest(%mr_seen),
  depth_level : depth_level - 1,
  if ans = false then integrate(f, x) else ans)$
rubi_hybrid(f, x) := %mr_hybrid_body(f, x, "alg")$
rubi_hybrid_exact(f, x) := %mr_hybrid_body(f, x, "exact")$

/* ===================== predicate cluster 1 (T5) =====================
 * Ports of Rubi IntegrationUtilityFunctions.m (pinned clone, read-only)
 * under the house rules. Each cites its .m source line; deviations from
 * the source are noted per function. */

/* ---- clone-gap equality pair --------------------------------------
 * PossibleZeroQ is referenced by EqQ/NeQ (IntegrationUtilityFunctions.m
 * :365/:370) but defined NOWHERE in the pinned clone (clone-gap note,
 * measured 2026-08-20); the contract is derived from usage: "possibly
 * zero" = "could be zero" (loose — the harness derivative check catches
 * over-firing).
 * MEASURED (2026-08-20, this build): is(e # 0) DECIDES true for any
 * expression containing a bare symbol (is(a # 0) -> true, is(a+b # 0)
 * -> true) — Maxima's is() assumes symbols are generically nonzero, so
 * `not is(e # 0)` (the plan's formula) would return false for the
 * symbol a, contradicting the unit probe "possible_zero a -> true".
 * The operational contract instead: substitute 0 for every variable
 * symbol of e; the result 0 (or a diverging substitution, caught) is
 * the "possibly zero" witness. */
%mr_isVarSym(a) := block([],
  if atom(a) and not numberp(a)
    and a # '%pi and a # '%e and a # '%i and a # 'inf and a # 'minf
    and a # 'true and a # 'false and a # 'unknown
    and a # '%lambda and a # 'undefined and a # 'und
  then true else false)$

%mr_symbols(e) := block([i, lst],
  if atom(e) then (
    if %mr_isVarSym(e) then [e] else []
  )
  /* MEASURED (2026-08-23): a rational is NOT an atom, and a negative
     rational has length 2 but only ONE part (the unary-minus quirk) —
     numberp is the exact-number guard, and op "-" recurses on its
     operand before the parts loop. */
  else if numberp(e) then []
  else if op(e) = "-" then %mr_symbols(part(e, 1))
  else (
    lst : [],
    for i : 1 thru length(e) do
      lst : append(lst, %mr_symbols(part(e, i))),
    lst
  ))$

/* MEASURED (2026-08-20): the LIST form of subst is a silent NO-OP in
   this build (subst([0], [a], a+b) -> a+b) — scalar form in a loop. */
%mr_zeroSubst(e, syms) := block([i, z],
  z : e,
  for i : 1 thru length(syms) do
    z : subst(0, part(syms, i), z),
  z)$

%mr_possible_zeroQ(e) := block([syms, z],
  syms : %mr_symbols(e),
  /* errcatch: a zero-substitution that diverges (1/x at x=0) is not a
     zero-witness -> not possibly zero */
  z : errcatch(%mr_zeroSubst(e, syms)),
  if is(z = []) then false
  else if is(part(z, 1) = 0) then true else false)$

 /* EqQ is the STRICT equality guard: u-v must simplify to 0. The earlier
  * loose reading (%mr_eqQ := %mr_possible_zeroQ(u-v), the clone-gap
  * "fire when the condition could hold" philosophy) over-fired at scale:
  * %mr_possible_zeroQ zero-substitutes EVERY variable symbol, so a generic
  * parameter product like -10*a*b*c read as "possibly zero" and the EqQ
  * guards of the 1.1.1.x/1.2.x rules fired on integrands they did not
  * cover (the 2026-08-24 full class-1 run: 19,454 FAILs, many of them
  * this). The harness derivative check cannot recover a wrong answer that
  * a misfired rule already returned. NeQ stays "not provably zero" (a
  * symbolic m must let the power rule fire), which is independent of the
  * EqQ reading. */
%mr_eqQ(u, v) := %mr_b(u - v = 0)$
%mr_neQ(u, v) := block([z],
  z : is(u - v = 0),
  if z = true then false else true)$

/* ---- simp / together shims ----------------------------------------
 * simplify and together are UNBOUND in this build (measured, T2/T4;
 * probes/translation/02-support-surface.mac S4). */
%mr_together(e) := block([r],
  r : errcatch(rat(e)),
  if is(r = []) then e
  else num(part(r, 1))/denom(part(r, 1)))$

/* the zero-chain (Q3, docs/corpus-baseline.md): apply in order, return
   the last stage. Rubi's 2-arg Simp[u_,x_] (IntegrationUtilityFunctions.m
   :2265) is TimeConstrained[NormalizeSumFactors[SimpHelp[u,x]],$TimeLimit,u]
   — a value-preserving sum-factor sign/content normalization. DEVIATION:
   the port accepts the variable argument (Maxima optional-arg idiom,
   [v] collects it as a list, [] when absent — MEASURED 2026-08-25) but
   ignores it and runs the same zero-chain; value-preserving is the hard
   requirement, shape parity is not. MEASURED 2026-08-25: with a
   1-arg-only definition every 2-arg call site fatal'd
   "Too many arguments" at repl evaluation — errcatched, so the 32 rules
   carrying a 2-arg %mr_simp (39 call sites: 1.1.1.4, 1.1.2.7, 1.1.2.9,
   1.2.1.1, 1.2.1.2, 1.2.1.3, 1.2.1.5, 1.2.2.3, 1.2.2.4, 1.2.3.4, 1.4.1)
   silently DECLINED. */
%mr_simp(e, [v]) := block([a],
  a : ratsimp(e),
  a : ratsimp(expand(a)),
  a : factor(a),
  ratsimp(a))$

/* ---- sign / posQ / negQ -------------------------------------------
 * Sign (Maxima sign() is bound; it returns the five-valued
 * pos/neg/zero/pnz/pz — pz = "positive or zero", measured). posQ is the
 * STRICT reading the unit probe pins: undecidable sign -> false
 * (Rubi's PosAux falls through to True for a bare symbol; the project
 * probe deliberately differs — documented deviation). */
%mr_sign(e) := block([s],
  s : sign(e),
  if s = 'pos then 1
  elseif s = 'neg then -1
  elseif s = 'zero then 0
  else s)$
%mr_posQ(e) := block([s],
  s : sign(e),
  if s = 'pos then true else false)$
/* Rubi :642  NegQ[u] := Not[PosQ[u]] && NeQ[u, 0] */
%mr_negQ(e) := block([v],
  v : (not %mr_posQ(e)) and %mr_neQ(e, 0),
  if is(v) = true then true else false)$

/* ---- explicit-number predicates (Task 7a) ----------------------------
 * Rubi's IntegerQ/FractionQ/IntegersQ/RationalQ test EXPLICIT numbers
 * (Head === Integer / Rational — a captured symbolic expression is
 * never "explicit" no matter what it could become). Maxima keeps
 * rationals in reduced form, so the structural test for an explicit
 * rational is op = "/" with integer num/denom — plus the "-" wrapper,
 * because MEASURED (2026-08-23) a negative rational is op "-" in this
 * build (op(-1/2) = "-", num(-1/2) = -1, denom = 2). rationalp is a
 * NOUN in this build (measured, 02-support-surface S1a).
 *
 * Call shape (generate_class1.py :877-884): the generator emits a 1-arg
 * call — the scalar for one Rubi arg, a LIST for several (Rubi's
 * IntegersQ[u__]/RationalQ[u__] are variadic) — so the ports accept
 * scalar-or-list. FractionQ is NOT in the list-packing branch: its 83
 * class-1 uses are all scalar. */

/* scalar half of %mr_rationalQ — one explicit integer or fraction */
%mr_rationalQ_scalar(e) := block([],
  if integerp(e) then true
  else if not atom(e) and (op(e) = "/" or op(e) = "-")
    and integerp(num(e)) and integerp(denom(e))
    then true else false)$

/* Rubi :97  RationalQ[u__]. Task 7a update: the committed 1-arg port
 * answered false on the LIST calls the generator emits (49 class-1
 * sites — silent dead rules) and on negative rationals (the "/"-only
 * test missed the "-" wrapper); both fixed here. */
%mr_rationalQ(e) := block([i, v],
  if atom(e) then %mr_rationalQ_scalar(e)
  /* a non-list non-atom (x/2, sqrt(1/2), ...) takes the scalar test —
     the list walk must not misread a "/" node's num/denom as elements.
     MEASURED (2026-08-23): the list op is the string "[" (not "list"). */
  else if op(e) = "[" then (
    v : true,
    for i : 1 thru length(e) do
      if %mr_rationalQ_scalar(part(e, i)) = false then v : false,
    v
  )
  else %mr_rationalQ_scalar(e))$

/* Rubi :70  IntegersQ[u__] — all args explicit integers */
%mr_integersQ(u) := block([i, v],
  if atom(u) then integerp(u)
  else if op(u) = "[" then (
    if length(u) = 0 then false
    else (
      v : true,
      for i : 1 thru length(u) do
        if integerp(part(u, i)) = false then v : false,
      v)
  )
  else false)$

/* Rubi :87  FractionQ[u__] — all args explicit PROPER fractions
 * (Rational head, not Integer): a reduced rational in this build is a
 * "/" or "-" node with integer num/denom and is never an integer, so
 * the explicit-rational test minus integerp IS the proper-fraction
 * test. */
%mr_fractionQ(e) := block([],
  if integerp(e) then false
  else if not atom(e) and (op(e) = "/" or op(e) = "-")
    and integerp(num(e)) and integerp(denom(e))
    then true else false)$

/* ---- structural shape predicates (Task 7a cluster A2) ----------------
 * PolynomialQ / AtomQ / MonomialQ / LeafCount — the Mathematica builtins
 * the census tiered C (no .m source; semantics from the manual entries
 * and, for MonomialQ, the .m usage comment). */

/* PolynomialQ[u, x] — u is MANIFESTLY a polynomial in x. STRICTER than
 * %mr_polyQ (Rubi's PolyQ, the Together route): no cancellation. MEASURED
 * (2026-08-23): this build keeps (x^2+x)/(x+1) as a "/" node (no
 * auto-cancel) while rat() DOES cancel it — so the %mr_polyQ route would
 * over-accept, and %mr_polyformQ under-accepts (its else branch rejects
 * every quotient, even x/2 whose denominator is x-free). The walker
 * below differs from %mr_polyformQ in exactly that one branch: a
 * quotient is polynomial in x iff the denominator is x-free and the
 * numerator is (x/2 -> true; 1/x -> false), at any depth — Maxima does
 * not auto-combine x/2 + 1 (measured). A nonnegative-INTEGER-exponent
 * power is required, as in %mr_polyformQ (x^a with symbolic a is not a
 * polynomial). */
%mr_polystrictQ(e, x) := block([i, ok],
  if atom(e) then (
    is(e = x) or freeof(x, e)
  )
  elseif op(e) = "-" then %mr_polystrictQ(part(e, 1), x)
  elseif op(e) = "+" then (
    ok : true,
    for i : 1 thru length(e) do
      if is(%mr_polystrictQ(part(e, i), x)) = false then ok : false,
    ok
  )
  elseif op(e) = "*" then (
    ok : true,
    for i : 1 thru length(e) do
      if is(%mr_polystrictQ(part(e, i), x)) = false then ok : false,
    ok
  )
  elseif op(e) = "/" then
    freeof(x, part(e, 2)) and %mr_polystrictQ(part(e, 1), x)
  elseif op(e) = "^" then
    if integerp(part(e, 2)) and is(part(e, 2) >= 0)
      and freeof(x, part(e, 2))
    then %mr_polystrictQ(part(e, 1), x)
    else false
  else freeof(x, e))$

%mr_polynomialQ(u, x) := %mr_polystrictQ(u, x)$

/* AtomQ[e] — e has no subparts (Mathematica builtin). Maxima's atom()
 * is the direct analog (measured: atom([1,2]) = false). */
%mr_atomQ(e) := block([], atom(e))$

/* Rubi :1391  MonomialQ[u, x] — "u is of the form a*x^n where n!=0 and
 * a!=0". The .m definition is MatchQ[u, a_.*x^n_. /; FreeQ[{a,n},x]]:
 * the OPTIONAL pattern args make bare x (n -> 1, a -> empty/1) and bare
 * x^k match. The class-1 call sites (1.3.3 x2, 1.4.1) feed PolyQ-
 * confirmed polynomials, so the operational test is structural: exactly
 * one x-power (x, or x^n with n x-free and not 0 — integer for a
 * polynomial; the lone-power case is the bare-x^n reading of the usage)
 * and every other factor x-free. DEVIATION (documented): a lone x^a
 * with SYMBOLIC a returns true here, where the .m MatchQ (Power head
 * vs the Times pattern) returns false — unreachable at the call sites
 * (PolynomialQ-guarded). a = 0 needs no test: a zero product is the
 * atom 0 in this build. */
%mr_monomialQ(u, x) := block([i, f, nx, ok],
  if atom(u) then is(u = x)
  elseif op(u) = "-" then %mr_monomialQ(part(u, 1), x)
  elseif op(u) = "^" then
    is(part(u, 1) = x) and freeof(x, part(u, 2))
      and not is(part(u, 2) = 0)
  elseif op(u) = "*" then (
    /* atom-first: op() is a hard error on atoms in this build */
    nx : 0, ok : true,
    for i : 1 thru length(u) do (
      f : part(u, i),
      if atom(f) then (
        if is(f = x) then nx : nx + 1
        elseif freeof(x, f) then true
        else ok : false
      )
      elseif op(f) = "^" and is(part(f, 1) = x)
        and freeof(x, part(f, 2)) and not is(part(f, 2) = 0)
      then nx : nx + 1
      elseif freeof(x, f) then true
      else ok : false
    ),
    ok and is(nx = 1)
  )
  else false)$

/* LeafCount[e] — the number of leaves in the expression tree
 * (Mathematica builtin: a leaf is a part with no subparts — the head
 * of a non-atom is a symbol, hence a leaf; LeafCount[1+x] = 3). */
%mr_leafCount(e) := block([i, s],
  if atom(e) then 1
  else (
    s : 1,
    for i : 1 thru length(e) do
      s : s + %mr_leafCount(part(e, i)),
    s))$

/* ---- MatchQ structural matcher (Task 7a cluster A3) ------------------
 * Rubi's MatchQ[u, pattern /; cond] (Mathematica builtin). matchq is a
 * NOUN in this build (measured 2026-08-20), so the matcher is custom.
 * Contract (generate_class1.py _emit_matchq, FIX E7): the generator
 * emits the uniform 3-arg call %mr_matchQ(u, pattern, cond) with the
 * pattern UNQUOTED — its pattern variables are fresh marker atoms
 * _mr_<key>_r<n>mq<k>_<v> (mints per file/rule/occurrence/var), which
 * stay literal at call time (a bare symbol not matchdeclare'd in an
 * active defmatch pattern is just a symbol), while computed parts
 * (renamed outer captures, %mr_expon results) evaluate to values.
 *
 * Matching semantics (the operational reading of the 17 class-1 call
 * sites; DEVIATION documented where the .m MatchQ corner is
 * unmeasurable without Mathematica):
 *  - a marker atom matches ANY sub-expression and binds it;
 *  - a literal atom (x, a number, a capture value) must be =-equal
 *    (this build's is(a = b) is true/false, never unknown — RC1);
 *  - a "*" (or "+") pattern matches its expression by FLAT PARTITION:
 *    the expression's factors (a power-less single term counts as one
 *    factor) are split into consecutive groups, one per pattern
 *    factor. Times/Plus are commutative and Maxima stores each side in
 *    its own canonical factor order (measured 2026-08-23: x^m*u
 *    stores [u, x^m] — a symbolic-exponent power sorts after a bare
 *    symbol — while x^3*y stores [x^3, y]), so EVERY permutation of
 *    the pattern factors is tried (the stored order first) — the
 *    Rubi/Mathematica orderless reading;
 *  - NON-EMPTY groups are tried before the empty one (the Mathematica
 *    preference: a real factor before the Optional fallback — this is
 *    what gives the Rubi-faithful a=c, b=d, v=y binding at the 1.3.4
 *    site instead of a=0, b=1, v=c+d*y);
 *  - an EMPTY group means the pattern factor is absent (the .m
 *    optional reading, a_./u_./r_.): a marker takes the group
 *    identity (1 for "*", 0 for "+"); a power with a marker exponent
 *    records the Optional default (exponent := 1) and skips its base;
 *    a literal or literal-power factor CANNOT be absent;
 *  - a "^" pattern against a power-less (real) expression takes the
 *    expression as base with exponent 1 (Maxima drops ^1 — the
 *    optional-r_. reading), binding a marker exponent to 1;
 *  - a marker re-occurring in one pattern must bind consistently.
 * On a successful match the cond — emitted by the generator as
 * lambda([%mr_mqb], <cond with each marker rewritten to a %mr_mk(m,
 * %mr_mqb) lookup>) or bare true — is called with the binding list
 * (Maxima evaluates call arguments eagerly and this build's ev() does
 * not strip quotes, so a plain or quoted cond cannot be held —
 * measured 2026-08-23). A non-trivial cond is threaded through the
 * structural search: the match stands iff SOME structural binding
 * makes the cond true (false, an error, or a cond that is not true —
 * a pending predicate noun — fails closed, the existing pending-noun
 * contract). The backtracking continuation is carried as an explicit
 * list (["final" | "match" | "part", ...]), not a lambda: Maxima
 * lambdas in this build do not capture the local variables of the
 * function that created them (measured 2026-08-23), so a closure over
 * the search state would resolve the wrong P/E/k at call time. */

/* a pattern marker: an atom the generator minted for a MatchQ pattern
 * variable — name shape _mr_<key>_r<n>mq<k>_<v> (generate_class1.py
 * _emit_matchq). Distinguishes markers from rule captures
 * (_mr_<key>_r<n>_<v> — no "mq") and pattern literals (x, numbers,
 * computed values).
 *
 * Membership, not name-shape (measured 2026-08-25, 5.50.0/SBCL): the
 * name-shape test the scan below used (char-by-char substring probes)
 * is unsound on this build — the COMPILED substring ERRORS on
 * out-of-range bounds (interpreted substring clamps; the same
 * (:=) function's scan probes one past the end), and inside the
 * nested compiled context of a matchfix defmatch that error is
 * FATAL — Maxima's errcatch cannot catch it: 1.2.1.6 e77's batch
 * died mid-target after 15 printed "substring: unsuitable start or
 * end position" lines (no CLASS line; a shallower probe caught the
 * identical error). The generator mints the marker names, so each
 * generated rule file REGISTERS its exact markers at load time and
 * the test is a membership scan — no strings, no probes, no errors.
 * (member() in this build returns a boolean, true/false, not the
 * sublist — measured 2026-08-23 — which is what the test wants.) */
%mr_marker_registry : []$

%mr_register_markers(list) :=
  %mr_marker_registry : append(%mr_marker_registry, list)$

%mr_isMQMarker(s) := block([],
  if not atom(s) or numberp(s) then false
  else member(s, %mr_marker_registry))$

/* the util-side predicates above (linearMatchQ / quadraticMatchQ /
 * binomialMatchQ / trinomialMatchQ / trinomialQ / gbp / gbm / gtp /
 * gtm) mint their own markers inline — register them here, at load,
 * so the membership test above sees them (the generated rule files
 * register their own in the trailing %mr_register_markers line). */
%mr_register_markers([ _mr_binMq_r1mq1_a, _mr_binMq_r1mq1_b, _mr_binMq_r1mq1_n, _mr_gbm1_r1mq1_a, _mr_gbm1_r1mq1_b, _mr_gbm1_r1mq1_n, _mr_gbm1_r1mq1_q, _mr_gbp1_r1mq1_a, _mr_gbp1_r1mq1_b, _mr_gbp1_r1mq1_n, _mr_gbp1_r1mq1_q, _mr_gbp2_r1mq1_a, _mr_gbp2_r1mq1_u, _mr_gbp3_r1mq1_m, _mr_gbp3_r1mq1_u, _mr_gbp4_r1mq1_m, _mr_gbp4_r1mq1_u, _mr_gtm1_r1mq1_a, _mr_gtm1_r1mq1_b, _mr_gtm1_r1mq1_c, _mr_gtm1_r1mq1_n, _mr_gtm1_r1mq1_q, _mr_gtm1_r1mq1_r ])$
%mr_register_markers([ _mr_gtp1_r1mq1_a, _mr_gtp1_r1mq1_b, _mr_gtp1_r1mq1_c, _mr_gtp1_r1mq1_n, _mr_gtp1_r1mq1_q, _mr_gtp1_r1mq1_r, _mr_gtp2_r1mq1_a, _mr_gtp2_r1mq1_u, _mr_gtp3_r1mq1_m, _mr_gtp3_r1mq1_u, _mr_gtp4_r1mq1_m, _mr_gtp4_r1mq1_u, _mr_linMq_r1mq1_a, _mr_linMq_r1mq1_b, _mr_quadMq_r1mq1_a, _mr_quadMq_r1mq1_b, _mr_quadMq_r1mq1_c, _mr_quadMq_r2mq1_a, _mr_quadMq_r2mq1_c, _mr_triMq_r1mq1_a, _mr_triMq_r1mq1_b, _mr_triMq_r1mq1_c, _mr_triMq_r1mq1_j, _mr_triMq_r1mq1_n, _mr_triQ_r1mq1_w ])$

/* bind a marker to a value in the binding list [marker = value, ...];
 * a re-bound marker must agree. false on conflict. (No return() inside
 * loops — loop-level return landmine, task 4 F1.) */
%mr_mq_bind(B, m, v) := block([i, found],
  i : 1, found : false,
  while i <= length(B) and not found do (
    if is(part(part(B, i), 1) = m) then found : true
    else i : i + 1
  ),
  if found then is(part(part(B, i), 2) = v) and B
  else append(B, [m = v]))$

/* match two sub-patterns in sequence, threading the bindings. Success
 * values are always binding lists (a list is non-atom; false is the
 * only atom a match returns — = / # on lists never evaluate on their
 * own in this build, so success is tested with atom()). */
%mr_mq_seq(P1, E1, P2, E2, B) := block([b],
  b : %mr_mq_match(P1, E1, B),
  if atom(b) then false
  else %mr_mq_match(P2, E2, b))$

/* ordered argument-list match — function-call arguments are ordered,
 * no permutation (unlike the commutative "*" / "+" flat match). The
 * caller has already bound the head (a marker op) and checked the
 * arity; this matches the args pairwise against the stored call. */
%mr_mq_seqargs(P, E, i, B) := block([b],
  if i > length(P) then B
  else (
    b : %mr_mq_match(part(P, i), part(E, i), B),
    if atom(b) then false
    else %mr_mq_seqargs(P, E, i + 1, b)))$

/* the cond-threading variant: chain the existing "match" goal type —
 * no %mr_mq_apply_goal change. One "match" goal per remaining
 * argument, the MatchQ goal G at the end: a "match" goal applies its
 * pair, then its continuation, so the tail is built by nesting. The
 * nesting is LOAD-BEARING — a single-level ["match", part(P, i+1), ...]
 * at i = length(P) runs part() off the end, which ERRORS in this build
 * (measured 2026-08-28, 5.50.0/SBCL: "part: fell off the end."; r96's
 * 1-arg shape is exactly that position), and would silently drop
 * trailing arguments for 3+ args. */
%mr_mq_seqargs_search(P, E, i, B, G) := block([g],
  if i > length(P) then %mr_mq_apply_goal(G, B)
  else (
    g : %mr_mq_seqargs_tail(P, E, i + 1, G),
    %mr_mq_search(part(P, i), part(E, i), B, g)))$

%mr_mq_seqargs_tail(P, E, i, G) := block([],
  if i > length(P) then G
  else ["match", part(P, i), part(E, i),
        %mr_mq_seqargs_tail(P, E, i + 1, G)])$

/* match pattern factor P against an ABSENT group — the empty
 * partition group, the Optional u_. reading: the factor is simply not
 * there. NOT the powerless rule in %mr_mq_match (a real E with no
 * power, where the base must still match): here the base is skipped.
 * A marker takes the group identity (1 for "*", 0 for "+"); a literal
 * must already BE the identity; a power with a marker exponent records
 * the Optional default (exponent := 1); a nested same-op sub-pattern
 * must itself be absent factor by factor. A sub-pattern with a literal
 * part (g_*x^2) cannot be absent — the literal has no default. */
%mr_mq_absent(P, opname, B) := block([id],
  id : if opname = "*" then 1 else 0,
  if atom(P) then (
    if %mr_isMQMarker(P) then %mr_mq_bind(B, P, id)
    else if is(P = id) then B else false
  )
  elseif op(P) = "^" then (
    if %mr_isMQMarker(part(P, 2)) then %mr_mq_bind(B, part(P, 2), 1)
    else false
  )
  elseif op(P) = opname then %mr_mq_absent_seq(P, 1, opname, B)
  else false)$

%mr_mq_absent_seq(P, i, opname, B) := block([b],
  if i > length(P) then B
  else (
    b : %mr_mq_absent(part(P, i), opname, B),
    if atom(b) then false
    else %mr_mq_absent_seq(P, i + 1, opname, b)))$

/* the product (or sum) of ef factors j..k-1; the empty group is the
 * group identity (1 for "*", 0 for "+") */
%mr_mq_group(ef, j, k, opname) := block([i, v],
  if k = j then (if opname = "*" then 1 else 0)
  else (
    v : part(ef, j),
    for i : j + 1 thru k - 1 do
      v : if opname = "*" then v * part(ef, i) else v + part(ef, i),
    v))$

/* insert h at position i of list L (1..length(L) + 1) */
%mr_perm_insert(h, L, i) := block([j, out],
  out : [],
  for j : 1 thru i - 1 do out : append(out, [part(L, j)]),
  out : append(out, [h]),
  for j : i thru length(L) do out : append(out, [part(L, j)]),
  out)$

/* all permutations of L, the identity (stored) order first —
 * insert first(L) at every position of each permutation of rest(L);
 * n is tiny (the class-1 patterns carry at most ~5 factors), so the
 * plain n! enumeration is fine */
%mr_perm(L) := block([P, i, j, R],
  if length(L) <= 1 then [L]
  else (
    P : %mr_perm(rest(L)),
    R : [],
    for i : 1 thru length(P) do
      for j : 1 thru length(L) do
        R : append(R, [%mr_perm_insert(first(L), part(P, i), j)]),
    R))$

/* pattern factors pf[i..] vs expression factors ef[j..]: a consecutive
 * partition, backtracking over the group sizes. Non-empty groups are
 * tried first and the empty (Optional-identity) group LAST: that is
 * the Mathematica preference (a real factor before the Optional
 * fallback), and it is what gives the Rubi-faithful binding at the
 * 1.3.4 site — a_ + b_*v_ vs c + d*y binds a=c, b=d, v=y, not
 * a=0, b=1, v=c+d*y. Success values are binding lists (non-atom);
 * false is the only atom a match returns, so the loop tests success
 * with atom() (= / # on lists never evaluate on their own in this
 * build — measured 2026-08-23). */
%mr_mq_part(pf, i, ef, j, B, opname) := block([k, b, res],
  if i > length(pf) then (if is(j = length(ef) + 1) then B else false)
  else (
    res : false, k : j + 1,
    while atom(res) and k <= length(ef) + 1 do (
      b : %mr_mq_match(part(pf, i), %mr_mq_group(ef, j, k, opname), B),
      if atom(b) then k : k + 1
      else (
        res : %mr_mq_part(pf, i + 1, ef, k, b, opname),
        if atom(res) then k : k + 1
      )),
    if atom(res) then (
      b : %mr_mq_absent(part(pf, i), opname, B),
      if atom(b) then false else %mr_mq_part(pf, i + 1, ef, j, b, opname)
    ) else res))$

/* the expression's flat factors for a "*" / "+" match. A non-flat
 * single term is one factor; the ONE extra Maxima-surface expansion
 * (cluster E, measured 2026-08-23): a unary-minus term (-x, -x^2,
 * -(a+b)) is Mathematica's Times[-1, ...], so against a TIMES pattern
 * it splits into the factors [-1, <subterm>] — that is what lets the
 * a_*x^q summand pattern bind a = -1 on a negative sum term (the
 * generic matcher otherwise sees the "-" node as one indivisible
 * factor). The "+" side is left as is: a negative SUM TERM is still
 * one sum term, and the product pattern matched against that term
 * takes the "*" branch above. */
%mr_mq_flat_ef(opname, E) := block([i, ef],
  ef : [],
  if opname = "*" then (
    if not atom(E) and is(op(E) = "-") then (
      ef : append(ef, [-1]),
      ef : append(ef, [part(E, 1)])
    )
    else if atom(E) or not is(op(E) = "*") then ef : append(ef, [E])
    else for i : 1 thru length(E) do
      ef : append(ef, [part(E, i)])
  )
  else (
    if atom(E) or not is(op(E) = "+") then ef : append(ef, [E])
    else for i : 1 thru length(E) do
      ef : append(ef, [part(E, i)])
  ),
  ef)$

/* a power-shaped expression as [base, exponent]: a "^" node as is, or
 * the collapsed sqrt node (Maxima stores x^(1/2) as 'sqrt(x) — the
 * op is the SYMBOL 'sqrt, measured in the cluster-B note) as exponent
 * 1/2. false otherwise. This is what lets a x^q_ pattern term match
 * the stored sqrt(x) shape (cluster E: the generalized-binomial
 * fractional-exponent probes). */
%mr_mq_power_exp(E) := block([],
  if not atom(E) then (
    if is(op(E) = "^") then [part(E, 1), part(E, 2)]
    else if is(op(E) = 'sqrt) then [part(E, 1), 1/2]
    else false
  )
  else false)$

/* flat "*" / "+" match: the expression's factors (a non-flat single
 * term is one factor) partitioned over the pattern's factors.
 * Times and Plus are commutative, and Maxima stores each side in its
 * own canonical factor order — measured 2026-08-23: x^m*u stores as
 * [u, x^m] (a symbolic-exponent power sorts AFTER a bare symbol)
 * while x^3*y stores as [x^3, y] — so a stored-order partition
 * depends on where the long _mr_ marker names happen to sort, and
 * misses matches the Rubi (Mathematica) orderless matcher finds
 * (x^3*y vs x^m*u must match with m=3, u=y). Try every permutation
 * of the pattern factors, the stored (identity) order first. */
%mr_mq_flat(P, E, B, opname) := block([ef, i, pf, perm, r],
  pf : [],
  for i : 1 thru length(P) do pf : append(pf, [part(P, i)]),
  ef : %mr_mq_flat_ef(opname, E),
  r : false,
  for perm in %mr_perm(pf) do
    if atom(r) then
      r : %mr_mq_part(perm, 1, ef, 1, B, opname),
  r)$

%mr_mq_match(P, E, B) := block([PE],
  if atom(P) then (
    if %mr_isMQMarker(P) then %mr_mq_bind(B, P, E)
    else if is(P = E) then B else false
  )
  elseif op(P) = "*" then %mr_mq_flat(P, E, B, "*")
  elseif op(P) = "+" then %mr_mq_flat(P, E, B, "+")
  elseif op(P) = "^" then (
    PE : %mr_mq_power_exp(E),
    if not atom(PE) then
      %mr_mq_seq(part(P, 1), part(PE, 1), part(P, 2), part(PE, 2), B)
    else (
      /* power-less E = exponent 1 (Maxima drops ^1): a literal 1
         exponent or a marker exponent (which binds 1 — the .m
         optional r_. reading) */
      if is(part(P, 2) = 1) then
        %mr_mq_match(part(P, 1), E, B)
      elseif %mr_isMQMarker(part(P, 2)) then
        block([b],
          b : %mr_mq_bind(B, part(P, 2), 1),
          if atom(b) then false
          else %mr_mq_match(part(P, 1), E, b))
      else false
    )
  )
  elseif %mr_isMQMarker(op(P)) then
    /* marker-as-head (r96, the first ported rule with a marker in HEAD
       position — this build's defmatch rejects pattern variables in
       head position, so the case lives here, not in Maxima's matcher):
       the op of a non-atomic P is a registered marker. Bind it to the
       op of a non-atomic E with STRICT arity; the stored call's op
       must be atomic (Maxima stored calls have atomic ops — probed
       2026-08-28); the arguments are ordered (seqargs). Binding a
       commutative or power op as a head is the .m-faithful reading
       and is allowed. */
    if atom(E) or not atom(op(E)) then false
    else if not is(length(P) = length(E)) then false
    else block([b],
      b : %mr_mq_bind(B, op(P), op(E)),
      if atom(b) then false
      else %mr_mq_seqargs(P, E, 1, b))
  else false)$

/* lookup a marker's bound value in the binding list
 * [m1 = v1, m2 = v2, ...]; false if absent (a cond that references
 * an unmatched marker fails closed) */
%mr_mk(m, B) := block([i, r, found],
  i : 1, r : false, found : false,
  while i <= length(B) and not found do
    if is(part(part(B, i), 1) = m) then block([],
      r : part(part(B, i), 2), found : true)
    else i : i + 1,
  r)$

/* Rubi's MatchQ[u, pattern /; cond] — the public 3-arg form the
 * generator emits (FIX E7). Maxima evaluates call arguments eagerly,
 * so the generator emits a non-empty cond as a LAMBDA taking the
 * binding list: lambda([%mr_mqb], <cond with each marker m rewritten
 * to %mr_mk(m, %mr_mqb)>); an empty cond is bare true. The lambda
 * body is held until called (measured 2026-08-23, task 7a A3: an
 * unquoted cond evaluates its marker-dependent parts — integerp(m),
 * is(m > 1), freeof(x, m) — on the unbound marker symbols before the
 * match; and this build's ev() does not strip quotes, so the
 * quote-based variant is dead). The cond's references to the OUTER
 * rule captures (a, b, n, ...) resolve through Maxima's ordinary
 * variable resolution at call time. On each COMPLETE structural match
 * the closure is called with the binding list; while it answers false
 * (or errors), the search continues to the next structural binding.
 * false on no match, on a closure error, or a cond that is not true
 * (unknown / a pending predicate noun fails closed — the existing
 * pending-noun contract). No marker ordering is coupled between
 * generator and matcher: every marker reference is a named lookup,
 * not a positional argument.
 *
 * The cond = true guard is measured (2026-08-23): true = true
 * evaluates to true, while a lambda = true stays a pending relation,
 * which Maxima's if treats as false — so the bare-true and closure
 * cases split without is() (is(lambda = true) would risk unknown). */
%mr_matchQ(u, pattern, cond) := block([b],
  if cond = true then (
    b : %mr_mq_match(pattern, u, []),
    if atom(b) then false else true
  )
  else %mr_mq_search(pattern, u, [], ["final", cond]))$

/* the MatchQ cond, evaluated on a complete binding list. The cond is
 * the generator's lambda over the binding list; a cond error, a false
 * answer, or a pending noun all fail closed. */
%mr_cond_ok(cond, B) := block([r],
  r : errcatch(cond(B)),
  if r = [] then false
  else if is(first(r)) = true then true
  else false)$

/* apply a search continuation to a binding list. The continuation is
 * an explicit list (no lambda — see the matcher note):
 *   ["final", cond]        — the top level: test the MatchQ cond
 *   ["match", P, E, G]     — match sub-pattern P against E, then G
 *   ["part", pf, i, ef, j, opname, G]
 *                           — resume the flat partition at pf[i..]
 */
%mr_mq_apply_goal(G, B) := block([t],
  t : part(G, 1),
  if is(t = "final") then %mr_cond_ok(part(G, 2), B)
  else if is(t = "match") then
    %mr_mq_search(part(G, 2), part(G, 3), B, part(G, 4))
  else if is(t = "part") then
    %mr_mq_part_search(part(G, 2), part(G, 3), part(G, 4),
      part(G, 5), B, part(G, 6), part(G, 7))
  else false)$

/* the cond-threading structural search: true iff SOME structural
 * binding of P against E (extending B) makes the continuation G
 * succeed. The first-success %mr_mq_match family above is kept for the
 * cond = true fast path; this is the Rubi MatchQ[u, pat /; cond]
 * semantics where the cond participates in the backtracking. */
%mr_mq_search(P, E, B, G) := block([PE],
  if atom(P) then (
    if %mr_isMQMarker(P) then
      block([b],
        b : %mr_mq_bind(B, P, E),
        if atom(b) then false else %mr_mq_apply_goal(G, b)
      )
    else if is(P = E) then %mr_mq_apply_goal(G, B)
    else false
  )
  elseif op(P) = "*" then %mr_mq_flat_search(P, E, B, "*", G)
  elseif op(P) = "+" then %mr_mq_flat_search(P, E, B, "+", G)
  elseif op(P) = "^" then (
    PE : %mr_mq_power_exp(E),
    if not atom(PE) then
      %mr_mq_search(part(P, 1), part(PE, 1), B,
        ["match", part(P, 2), part(PE, 2), G]
      )
    else (
      if is(part(P, 2) = 1) then
        %mr_mq_search(part(P, 1), E, B, G)
      elseif %mr_isMQMarker(part(P, 2)) then
        block([b],
          b : %mr_mq_bind(B, part(P, 2), 1),
          if atom(b) then false
          else %mr_mq_search(part(P, 1), E, b, G)
        )
      else false
    )
  )
  elseif %mr_isMQMarker(op(P)) then
    /* marker-as-head, the cond-threading twin of the case above in
       %mr_mq_match (r96 has a cond, so THIS is the load-bearing path):
       same binding/arity semantics, the search variant continues
       through the existing "match" goal type. */
    if atom(E) or not atom(op(E)) then false
    else if not is(length(P) = length(E)) then false
    else block([b],
      b : %mr_mq_bind(B, op(P), op(E)),
       if atom(b) then false
       else %mr_mq_seqargs_search(P, E, 1, b, G))
  else false)$

%mr_mq_flat_search(P, E, B, opname, G) := block([ef, i, pf, perm, r],
  pf : [],
  for i : 1 thru length(P) do pf : append(pf, [part(P, i)]),
  ef : %mr_mq_flat_ef(opname, E),
  r : false,
  for perm in %mr_perm(pf) do
    if not r then
      r : %mr_mq_part_search(perm, 1, ef, 1, B, opname, G),
  r)$

%mr_mq_part_search(pf, i, ef, j, B, opname, G) := block([k, b, res],
  if i > length(pf) then (
    if is(j = length(ef) + 1) then %mr_mq_apply_goal(G, B) else false
  )
  else (
    res : false, k : j + 1,
    while not res and k <= length(ef) + 1 do (
      if %mr_mq_search(part(pf, i), %mr_mq_group(ef, j, k, opname), B,
            ["part", pf, i + 1, ef, k, opname, G])
      then res : true
      else k : k + 1
    ),
    if not res then (
      b : %mr_mq_absent(part(pf, i), opname, B),
      if not atom(b) and
         %mr_mq_part_search(pf, i + 1, ef, j, b, opname, G)
      then res : true
    ),
    res))$

/* ---- int-family + binomial predicates (Task 7a cluster B) -----------
 * Rubi's integrability guards (IntLinearQ / IntQuadraticQ /
 * IntBinomialQ — True iff the product integrates in terms of
 * non-hypergeometric / non-Appell functions) and the BinomialQ shape
 * test (a + b*x^n). Line-ports of the pinned Rubi definitions
 * (provenance in each comment); the .m IntegerQ / IGtQ / ILtQ /
 * RationalQ / IntegersQ / EqQ map onto the A1 ports — IntegerQ is
 * integerp on an exact value (Maxima auto-simplifies exact rational
 * arithmetic, MEASURED 2026-08-23: 1/2 + 1/2 -> 1, so IntegerQ[m+n]
 * is integerp(m+n)). Arity (class-1 census 2026-08-23): binomialQ
 * 2/3 and intBinomialQ 7/8/10 are the two multi-arity names — the Lisp
 * dispatchers in maxima_rubi_dispatch.lisp re-dispatch onto the
 * fixed-arity bodies here, named <name><arity>; intLinearQ (7) and
 * intQuadraticQ (8) are fixed-arity and need no dispatcher. */

/* Rubi :70/:379/:384 — IntegerQ, IGtQ[u,n] := IntegerQ[u] && u>n,
 * ILtQ[u,n] := IntegerQ[u] && u<n. Exact integers only (floats and
 * symbols fail, as in Mathematica). */
%mr_integerQ(e) := integerp(e)$
%mr_IGtQ(u, n) := %mr_integerQ(u) and is(u > n)$
%mr_ILtQ(u, n) := %mr_integerQ(u) and is(u < n)$

/* the distinct x-exponents of a CONFIRMED polynomial (callers gate on
 * %mr_polynomialQ), ascending — MEASURED 2026-08-23: sort() orders
 * exact rationals, member() on numbers returns a boolean, and op()
 * hard-errors on ATOMS (symbols included) in this build, so the
 * single-term case is gated on atom() first (expand(x) = x). */
%mr_exp_list(u, x) := block([e, i, p, L],
  e : expand(u),
  L : [],
  if atom(e) then (
    p : %mr_term_xexp(e, x),
    if p # false then L : append(L, [p])
  )
  else if op(e) = "+" then (
    for i : 1 thru length(e) do
      (
        p : %mr_term_xexp(part(e, i), x),
        if p # false and not member(p, L) then L : append(L, [p])
      )
  )
  else (
    p : %mr_term_xexp(e, x),
    if p # false then L : append(L, [p])),
  sort(L))$

/* Rubi :851  BinomialParts[u, x] — [a, b, n] iff u is equivalent to
 * a + b*x^n (n, b nonzero), else false. LINE port; the branches follow
 * the .m If-chain (polynomial / power / product / sum; First/Rest
 * split as first factor-or-term over the rest). DEVIATIONS, both
 * forced by Maxima's surface, documented:
 *  - 1/x stores as a "/" node [1, x], NOT a power x^(-1) (MEASURED
 *    2026-08-23), so the power branch also accepts the num = 1
 *    reciprocal shape (1/x -> [0, 1, -1]; 1/x^k -> [0, 1, -k]);
 *  - c/x (Mathematica's Times[c, x^(-1)] — the .m ProductQ branch)
 *    stores as ONE "/" node [c, x] and is NOT accepted; no class-1
 *    call site passes that shape (the factors of the (cx)^m (a+bx^n)^p
 *    products are powers and binomials, never bare reciprocals). */
%mr_binomial_parts(u, x) := block([d, L, i, f1, r1, L1, L2],
  if %mr_polynomialQ(u, x) then (
    d : %mr_degree(u, x),
    if d = 0 then false
    else (
      L : %mr_exp_list(u, x),
      if length(L) = 1 then [0, %mr_coeff(u, x, d), d]
      else if length(L) = 2 and is(part(L, 1) = 0)
      then [%mr_coeff(u, x, 0), %mr_coeff(u, x, d), d]
      else false
    )
  )
  elseif op(u) = "^" and is(part(u, 1) = x) and freeof(x, part(u, 2))
  then [0, 1, part(u, 2)]
  elseif op(u) = 'sqrt and is(part(u, 1) = x)
  /* Mathematica stores Sqrt[x] AS x^(1/2) (PowerQ is true); this
     Maxima build collapses exactly the 1/2 power to a sqrt node
     (MEASURED 2026-08-23: op(x^(1/2)) = 'sqrt, op(x^(3/2)) = "^") —
     accept the collapsed shape with the same reading (b = 1, n = 1/2).
     NOTE: op() in this build returns the SYMBOL 'sqrt for the sqrt
     head (is(op(sqrt(x)) = 'sqrt) is true; the string "sqrt" never
     matches) while special heads (^, +, *, /, -, [) stay strings. */
  then [0, 1, 1/2]
   elseif op(u) = "/" and is(num(u) = 1) then (
     if is(denom(u) = x) then [0, 1, -1]
     else if op(denom(u)) = "^" and is(part(denom(u), 1) = x)
       and freeof(x, part(denom(u), 2))
     then [0, 1, -part(denom(u), 2)]
     else false
   )
   /* unary minus: Maxima stores -x^(1/2) / -2*(1+x^(1/2)) as a "-"
      node (the .m's Times[-1, ...] goes through the ProductQ branch
      with the -1 factor: {-1*a, -1*b, n}). Polynomial minus terms
      (-x^2) never reach this branch — the polynomial branch above
      handles them. Cluster D dependency: %mr_trinomial_parts sum
      branch passes unary-minus terms (1 - x^(1/2) + ...). */
   elseif op(u) = "-" then (
     L : %mr_binomial_parts(part(u, 1), x),
     if atom(L) then false else [-part(L, 1), -part(L, 2), part(L, 3)]
   )
   elseif op(u) = "*" then (
    f1 : part(u, 1),
    r1 : part(u, 2),
    for i : 3 thru length(u) do r1 : r1 * part(u, i),
    if freeof(x, f1) then (
      L2 : %mr_binomial_parts(r1, x),
      if atom(L2) then false
      else [f1*part(L2, 1), f1*part(L2, 2), part(L2, 3)]
    )
    else if freeof(x, r1) then (
      L1 : %mr_binomial_parts(f1, x),
      if atom(L1) then false
      else [r1*part(L1, 1), r1*part(L1, 2), part(L1, 3)]
    )
    else (
      L1 : %mr_binomial_parts(f1, x),
      if atom(L1) then false
      else (
        L2 : %mr_binomial_parts(r1, x),
        if atom(L2) then false
        else if %mr_eqQ(part(L1, 1), 0) then (
          if %mr_eqQ(part(L2, 1), 0)
          then [0, part(L1, 2)*part(L2, 2), part(L1, 3) + part(L2, 3)]
          else if %mr_eqQ(part(L1, 3) + part(L2, 3), 0)
          then [part(L1, 2)*part(L2, 2), part(L1, 2)*part(L2, 1), part(L1, 3)]
          else false
        )
        else if %mr_eqQ(part(L2, 1), 0) then (
          if %mr_eqQ(part(L1, 3) + part(L2, 3), 0)
          then [part(L1, 2)*part(L2, 2), part(L1, 1)*part(L2, 2), part(L2, 3)]
          else false
        )
        else if is(part(L1, 3) = part(L2, 3))
          and %mr_eqQ(part(L1, 1)*part(L2, 2) + part(L1, 2)*part(L2, 1), 0)
        then [part(L1, 1)*part(L2, 1), part(L1, 2)*part(L2, 2), 2*part(L1, 3)]
        else false
      )
    )
  )
  elseif op(u) = "+" then (
    f1 : part(u, 1),
    r1 : part(u, 2),
    for i : 3 thru length(u) do r1 : r1 + part(u, i),
    if freeof(x, f1) then (
      L2 : %mr_binomial_parts(r1, x),
      if atom(L2) then false
      else [f1 + part(L2, 1), part(L2, 2), part(L2, 3)]
    )
    else if freeof(x, r1) then (
      L1 : %mr_binomial_parts(f1, x),
      if atom(L1) then false
      else [r1 + part(L1, 1), part(L1, 2), part(L1, 3)]
    )
    else (
      L1 : %mr_binomial_parts(f1, x),
      if atom(L1) then false
      else (
        L2 : %mr_binomial_parts(r1, x),
        if atom(L2) then false
        else if is(part(L1, 3) = part(L2, 3))
        then [part(L1, 1) + part(L2, 1), part(L1, 2) + part(L2, 2), part(L1, 3)]
        else false
      )
    )
  )
  else false)$

/* Rubi :549/:555  BinomialQ[u, x] / BinomialQ[u, x, n] — the 2/3-arg
 * dispatch targets (maxima_rubi_dispatch.lisp); the list-first-arg arm
 * is the .m ListQ[u] branch (the generator's list-packing). */
%mr_binomialQ2(u, x) := block([i, v],
  if not atom(u) and is(op(u) = "[") then (
    v : true,
    for i : 1 thru length(u) do
      if not %mr_binomialQ2(part(u, i), x) then v : false,
    v
  )
  else not atom(%mr_binomial_parts(u, x)))$

%mr_binomialQ3(u, x, n) := block([L, i, v],
  if not atom(u) and is(op(u) = "[") then (
    v : true,
    for i : 1 thru length(u) do
      if not %mr_binomialQ3(part(u, i), x, n) then v : false,
    v
  )
  else (
    L : %mr_binomial_parts(u, x),
    if atom(L) then false else is(part(L, 3) = n)))$

/* Rubi 1.1.1.2:44  IntLinearQ[a, b, c, d, m, n, x] — True iff
 * (a+b*x)^m (c+d*x)^n integrates in non-hypergeometric terms.
 * Fixed 7-arg (census: 4 call sites, all 7-arg); a-d unused by the
 * body, as in the .m. */
%mr_intLinearQ(a, b, c, d, m, n, x) :=
  %mr_IGtQ(m, 0) or %mr_IGtQ(n, 0)
  or %mr_integersQ([3*m, 3*n]) or %mr_integersQ([4*m, 4*n])
  or %mr_integersQ([2*m, 6*n]) or %mr_integersQ([6*m, 2*n])
  or %mr_ILtQ(m + n, -1)
  or %mr_integerQ(m + n) and %mr_rationalQ(m)$

/* Rubi 1.2.1.2:151  IntQuadraticQ[a, b, c, d, e, m, p, x] — True iff
 * (d+e*x)^m (a+b*x+c*x^2)^p integrates in non-Appell terms.
 * Fixed 8-arg (census: 21 call sites, all 8-arg). */
%mr_intQuadraticQ(a, b, c, d, e, m, p, x) :=
  %mr_integerQ(p) or %mr_IGtQ(m, 0)
  or %mr_integersQ([2*m, 2*p]) or %mr_integersQ([m, 4*p])
  or %mr_integersQ([m, p + 1/3])
     and (%mr_eqQ(c^2*d^2 - b*c*d*e + b^2*e^2 - 3*a*c*e^2, 0)
          or %mr_eqQ(c^2*d^2 - b*c*d*e - 2*b^2*e^2 + 9*a*c*e^2, 0))$

/* Rubi 1.1.3.2:118  IntBinomialQ[a, b, c, n, m, p, x] — True iff
 * (c*x)^m (a+b*x^n)^p integrates in non-hypergeometric terms. The
 * 7-arg dispatch target. The (m+1)/n disjunct is guarded on n # 0:
 * Maxima hard-errors 3/0 ("expt: undefined: 0 to a negative exponent",
 * MEASURED 2026-08-23) where Mathematica's Infinity answers
 * IntegerQ -> false. */
%mr_intBinomialQ7(a, b, c, n, m, p, x) :=
  %mr_IGtQ(p, 0)
  or (%mr_rationalQ(m) and %mr_integersQ([n, 2*p]))
  or is(n # 0) and %mr_integerQ((m + 1)/n + p)
  or (is(n = 2) or is(n = 4)) and %mr_integersQ([2*m, 4*p])
  or is(n = 2) and %mr_integerQ(6*p)
     and (%mr_integerQ(m) or %mr_integerQ(m - p))$

/* Rubi 1.1.3.3:73  IntBinomialQ[a, b, c, d, n, p, q, x] — True iff
 * (a+b*x^n)^p (c+d*x^n)^q integrates in non-Appell terms.
 * The 8-arg dispatch target. */
%mr_intBinomialQ8(a, b, c, d, n, p, q, x) :=
  %mr_integersQ([p, q])
  or %mr_IGtQ(p, 0) or %mr_IGtQ(q, 0)
  or (is(n = 2) or is(n = 4))
     and (%mr_integersQ([p, 4*q]) or %mr_integersQ([4*p, q]))
  or is(n = 2)
     and (%mr_integersQ([2*p, 2*q])
          or (%mr_integersQ([3*p, q]) and %mr_eqQ(b*c + 3*a*d, 0))
          or (%mr_integersQ([p, 3*q]) and %mr_eqQ(3*b*c + a*d, 0)))
  or is(n = 3) and (%mr_integersQ([p + 1/3, q]) or %mr_integersQ([q + 1/3, p]))
  or is(n = 3) and (%mr_integersQ([p + 2/3, q]) or %mr_integersQ([q + 2/3, p]))
     and %mr_eqQ(b*c + a*d, 0)$

/* Rubi 1.1.3.4:89  IntBinomialQ[a, b, c, d, e, m, n, p, q, x] —
 * True iff (e*x)^m (a+b*x^n)^p (c+d*x^n)^q integrates in non-Appell
 * terms. The 10-arg dispatch target. */
%mr_intBinomialQ10(a, b, c, d, e, m, n, p, q, x) :=
  %mr_integersQ([p, q])
  or %mr_IGtQ(p, 0) or %mr_IGtQ(q, 0)
  or is(n = 2)
     and (%mr_integersQ([m, 2*p, 2*q])
          or %mr_integersQ([2*m, p, 2*q])
          or %mr_integersQ([2*m, 2*p, q]))
  or is(n = 4) and (%mr_integersQ([m, p, 2*q]) or %mr_integersQ([m, 2*p, q]))
  or is(n = 2) and %mr_integersQ([m/2, p + 1/3, q])
     and (%mr_eqQ(b*c + 3*a*d, 0) or %mr_eqQ(b*c - 9*a*d, 0))
  or is(n = 2) and %mr_integersQ([m/2, q + 1/3, p])
     and (%mr_eqQ(a*d + 3*b*c, 0) or %mr_eqQ(a*d - 9*b*c, 0))
  or is(n = 3) and %mr_integersQ([(m - 1)/3, q, p - 1/2])
     and (%mr_eqQ(b*c - 4*a*d, 0) or %mr_eqQ(b*c + 8*a*d, 0)
          or %mr_eqQ(b^2*c^2 - 20*a*b*c*d - 8*a^2*d^2, 0))
  or is(n = 3) and %mr_integersQ([(m - 1)/3, p, q - 1/2])
     and (%mr_eqQ(4*b*c - a*d, 0) or %mr_eqQ(8*b*c + a*d, 0)
          or %mr_eqQ(8*b^2*c^2 + 20*a*b*c*d - a^2*d^2, 0))
  or is(n = 3)
     and (%mr_integersQ([m, q, 3*p]) or %mr_integersQ([m, p, 3*q]))
     and %mr_eqQ(b*c + a*d, 0)
   or is(n = 3)
      and (%mr_integersQ([(m + 2)/3, p + 2/3, q])
           or %mr_integersQ([m/3, q + 1/3, p]))$

 /* ---- sum / simpler predicates (Task 7a cluster C) -------------------
  * SumQ (:176) · SumSimplerQ (:774) + SumSimplerAuxQ (:784, the three
  * .m definitions: split-v, split-u, base) · SimplerQ (:677) ·
  * SimplerSqrtQ (:732) · NiceSqrtQ (:647) · FractionalPowerFactorQ
  * (:658 — the NiceSqrtQ dependency, pulled forward from cluster F) ·
  * NumericFactor (:1096) / NonnumericFactors (:1125) + the rest-of-sum
  * helper the SumSimplerAuxQ splits need.
  *
  * 5.50.0 representation traps pinned here (measured 2026-08-23):
  *  - is() returns the SYMBOL unknown for an undecidable relation and
  *    `if <unknown>` stays unevaluated — every relation is therefore
  *    `is(a op b) = true` (the bare `(a op b) = true` is NOT evaluated:
  *    it stays an equation);
  *  - complexp is a NOUN; the only atomic complex value is %i (-%i is
  *    a unary "-" node, not an atom) — the "Head === Complex" test is
  *    is(u = %i) plus its unary-minus form;
  *  - numberp covers exactly the real explicit numbers (integers,
  *    rationals — 1/2 is a "/" node and numberp is TRUE — and floats);
  *    %i / %pi / E / %inf are not numberp, and neither is a symbol;
  *  - sqrt normalizes aggressively where Mathematica holds:
  *    sqrt(x^2) -> abs(x) (Rubi: Sqrt[x^2] stays a power — the
  *    FractionalPowerFactorQ/niceSqrtQ x^2 probes pin the divergence),
  *    sqrt(2*x) -> sqrt(2)*sqrt(x), sqrt(1/(2*x)) ->
  *    1/(sqrt(2)*sqrt(x)), x^(1/2) -> a 'sqrt node, x^(-1/2) ->
  *    1/sqrt(x). Hence FractionalPowerFactorQ carries 'sqrt, "/" and
  *    unary "-" branches (the .m PowerQ branch alone cannot see the
  *    Maxima shapes);
  *  - Map[f, expr] keeps a non-list head in Mathematica — the
  *    NumericFactor/NonnumericFactors product branches are for-loops
  *    over the parts;
  *  - SimplerSqrtQ's LtQ sign tests stay pending on symbolic args in
  *    Rubi (the whole If declines, the rule does not fire) — the port
  *    reproduces the decline by answering false (never true) when
  *    either sign is unknown;
  *  - NumericFactor/NonnumericFactors sum branches take the .m's GCD
  *    fallback path; the LeafCount<50 ContentFactor kludge is omitted
  *    (a sum only reaches them through a product-of-sums inside a
  *    captured exponent — no class-1 corpus site has one), and the
  *    rational-denominator corner of the power branch (Maxima stores
  *    (1/2)^(1/2) as 1/sqrt(2), a "/" node the .m sees as the power
  *    2^(-1/2)) is unreachable at the 36 call sites (v is always an
  *    integer constant, u a symbol/number/monomial/integer-power).
  */

 /* Rubi :176  SumQ[_Plus] = True; SumQ[_] = False. A Maxima "+" node
    always has >= 2 parts (Mathematica's Plus flattens single terms). */
 %mr_sumQ(u) := block([],
   if atom(u) then false else is(op(u) = "+"))$

 /* NumberQ (Mathematica builtin) — explicit numbers only. numberp
    covers the reals (integer/rational/float, incl. the non-atom "/"
    node); %pi/E/symbols are not numbers (NumberQ[Pi] is false in
    Mathematica too); the complex forms are %i, -%i, a + b*i with both
    parts explicit reals. */
 %mr_numberQ(u) := block([],
   if numberp(u) then true
   else if is(u = %i) then true
   else if atom(u) then false
   else if is(op(u) = "-") then is(part(u, 1) = %i)
   else if is(op(u) = "+")
    then numberp(realpart(u)) and numberp(imagpart(u))
     and is(imagpart(u) # 0)
   else false)$

 /* ComplexNumberQ (Mathematica builtin) — an explicit complex with a
    nonzero imaginary part. complexp is a noun in this build. */
 %mr_complexNumberQ(u) := block([],
   if is(u = %i) then true
   else if atom(u) then false
   else if is(op(u) = "-") then is(part(u, 1) = %i)
   else if numberp(u) then is(imagpart(u) # 0) = true
   else if is(op(u) = "+")
    then numberp(realpart(u)) and numberp(imagpart(u))
     and is(imagpart(u) # 0) = true
   else false)$

  /* OrderedQ[{u, v}] — u and v in canonical order. Maxima has no
     canonical-order predicate; sort() is a total canonical order here
     (numbers first, %pi before bare symbols, x before 2*x — measured
     2026-08-23). UPSTREAM-BUG NOTE (measured 2026-08-25): OrderedQ is
     never defined anywhere in the pinned Rubi 4 clone (whole-clone and
     whole-git-history grep; its only uses are the SimplerQ :710/:716/:727
     tie-breaks and SimplerSqrtQ :764 of IntegrationUtilityFunctions.m),
     so in the pinned Rubi those tie-breaks are unevaluated nouns and
     rules like 1.1.1.4's Not[SimplerQ[...]]-guarded rule decline on
     every symbolic binding. The acceptance corpus
     (MaximaSyntaxTestSuite) was generated by a Rubi build with a
     WORKING OrderedQ: e.g. 1.1.1.7 e4's expected elliptic answer
     exists only if the 1.1.1.4 rule 1/((a+b x) Sqrt[c+d x] Sqrt[e+f x]
     Sqrt[g+h x]) /; Not[SimplerQ[e+f x, c+d x]] && Not[SimplerQ[g+h x,
     c+d x]] FIRES on the sub-integral (it is the rule's binding
     guard — the .m derivation assumes the (c+d x) slot holds the
     first sqrt factor; the guard declines the swapped binding, which
     would make the Subst repl algebraically wrong). The sort() total
     order is the corpus-target reading, not the pinned-Rubi reading —
     the corpus is the acceptance target. sort() failure
     (errcatch -> []) fails closed. */
  %mr_orderedQ(u, v) := block([s],
    s : errcatch(sort([u, v])),
    if is(s = []) then false
    else is(part(s, 1) = [u, v]))$

 /* Rest[u] for a sum — Maxima has no Rest; the sum of parts 2..n. A
    one-part "sum" cannot occur ("+" nodes have >= 2 parts). */
 %mr_rest_sum(u) := block([i, n, lst],
   n : length(u),
   if is(n = 1) then part(u, 1)
   else (
     lst : [],
     for i : 2 thru n do lst : append(lst, [part(u, i)]),
     apply("+", lst)))$

 /* Rubi :1096  NumericFactor[u] — the real numeric factor. */
 %mr_numericFactor(u) := block([i, n, r, b, ex],
   if %mr_numberQ(u) then (
     if is(imagpart(u) = 0) then u
     else if is(realpart(u) = 0) then imagpart(u)
     else 1
   )
   /* a non-numeric atom (a symbol) has no numeric factor — the .m's
      final else */
   else if atom(u) then 1
   else if is(op(u) = "^") or is(op(u) = 'sqrt) then (
     b : part(u, 1),
     ex : if is(op(u) = 'sqrt) then 1/2 else part(u, 2),
     if %mr_rationalQ(b) and %mr_fractionQ(ex)
     then (
       if is(ex > 0) = true then 1/denom(b) else 1/denom(1/b)
     )
     else 1
   )
   else if is(op(u) = "*") then (
     n : length(u),
     r : 1,
     for i : 1 thru n do r : r * %mr_numericFactor(part(u, i)),
     r
   )
   /* a unary minus wraps the (non-numeric) product: the .m reads it as
      a product with a -1 factor, whose NumericFactor is -1 */
   else if is(op(u) = "-") then -%mr_numericFactor(part(u, 1))
   else if %mr_sumQ(u) then
     block([m, nst],
       m : %mr_numericFactor(part(u, 1)),
       nst : %mr_numericFactor(%mr_rest_sum(u)),
       if is(m < 0) = true and is(nst < 0) = true
       then -gcd(-m, -nst) else gcd(m, nst))
   else 1)$

 /* Rubi :1125  NonnumericFactors[u] — the product of the factors that
    are not real numbers. The .m's GCD-path sum shape keeps the Plus
    head (a sum of term/NF[u] — Map preserves non-list heads). */
 %mr_nonnumericFactors(u) := block([i, n, r, d, lst],
   if %mr_numberQ(u) then (
     if is(imagpart(u) = 0) then 1
     else if is(realpart(u) = 0) then %i
     else u
   )
   /* a non-numeric atom (a symbol) is its own nonnumeric factor */
   else if atom(u) then u
   /* a unary minus wraps the product: the .m's -1 factor has
      NonnumericFactors 1, so the nonnumeric factors pass through */
   else if is(op(u) = "-") then %mr_nonnumericFactors(part(u, 1))
   else if is(op(u) = "^") or is(op(u) = 'sqrt) then (
     if %mr_rationalQ(part(u, 1))
    and %mr_fractionQ(if is(op(u) = 'sqrt) then 1/2 else part(u, 2))
     then u / %mr_numericFactor(u)
     else u
   )
   else if is(op(u) = "*") then (
     n : length(u),
     r : 1,
     for i : 1 thru n do r : r * %mr_nonnumericFactors(part(u, i)),
     r
   )
   else if %mr_sumQ(u) then (
     d : %mr_numericFactor(u),
     lst : [],
     n : length(u),
     for i : 1 thru n do lst : append(lst, [part(u, i)/d]),
     apply("+", lst)
   )
   else u)$

 /* Rubi :658  FractionalPowerFactorQ — a factor is a complex constant
    or a fractional power. The 'sqrt / "/" / "-" branches are the Maxima
    shape adaptations (see the section note). */
 %mr_fractionalPowerFactorQ(u) := block([i, n, r],
   if atom(u) then is(u = %i)
   else if is(op(u) = "^") then %mr_fractionQ(part(u, 2))
   else if is(op(u) = 'sqrt) then true
   else if is(op(u) = "*") then (
     n : length(u),
     i : 0,
     r : false,
     while is(r = false) and i < n do (
       i : i + 1,
       r : %mr_fractionalPowerFactorQ(part(u, i))),
     r
   )
   else if is(op(u) = "/")
    then %mr_fractionalPowerFactorQ(num(u))
     or %mr_fractionalPowerFactorQ(denom(u))
   else if is(op(u) = "-") then %mr_fractionalPowerFactorQ(part(u, 1))
   else false)$

 /* Rubi :647  NiceSqrtQ — positive rational, or a squareroot without
    fractional-power / complex factors. Rt[u, 2] is sqrt(u) here;
    sqrt(x^2) -> abs(x) is the documented divergence corner (Rubi says
    false for x^2, the port says true — unreachable at the class-1
    discriminant sites). */
 %mr_niceSqrtQ(u) := block([],
   /* u is an explicit rational here — decidable, clean boolean */
   if %mr_rationalQ(u) then is(u > 0)
   else not(%mr_fractionalPowerFactorQ(sqrt(u))))$

 /* the SimplerQ part scan — Catch[Do[If[eq, Null, Throw[SimplerQ]]];
    False]: SimplerQ of the FIRST differing part pair, false if none
    (a return()/break() is loop-scoped in this build — the Task 4 trap —
    hence the found-flag while loop). */
 %mr_simplerQ_scan(u, v) := block([i, n, r, found],
   n : length(u),
   i : 0,
   r : false,
   found : false,
   while is(found = false) and i < n do (
     i : i + 1,
     if is(part(u, i) = part(v, i)) = false then (
       r : %mr_simplerQ(part(u, i), part(v, i)),
       found : true)),
   r)$

 /* Rubi :677  SimplerQ[u, v] — the structural simplicity order. The
    Re/Im, ComplexNumberQ, NumberQ, OrderedQ readings are the Maxima
    stand-ins pinned in the section note. */
 %mr_simplerQ(u, v) := block([],
   if integerp(u) then (
     if integerp(v) then (
       if is(u = v) then false
       /* value position: u, v are concrete integers, so the comparison
          is decidable — return the clean boolean (is(X) = true would
          leak the equation false = true on a false answer). */
       else if is(u = -v) then is(v < 0)
       else is(abs(u) < abs(v))
     )
     else true
   )
   else if integerp(v) then false
   else if %mr_fractionQ(u) then (
     if %mr_fractionQ(v) then (
       if is(denom(u) = denom(v))
       then %mr_simplerQ(num(u), num(v))
       else is(denom(u) < denom(v))
     )
     else true
   )
   else if %mr_fractionQ(v) then false
   else if is(realpart(u) = 0) and is(realpart(v) = 0)
    then %mr_simplerQ(imagpart(u), imagpart(v))
   else if %mr_complexNumberQ(u) then (
     if %mr_complexNumberQ(v) then (
       if is(realpart(u) = realpart(v))
       then %mr_simplerQ(imagpart(u), imagpart(v))
       else %mr_simplerQ(realpart(u), realpart(v))
     )
     else false
   )
   else if %mr_numberQ(u) then (
     if %mr_numberQ(v) then %mr_orderedQ(u, v) else true
   )
   else if %mr_numberQ(v) then false
   else if atom(u) then (
     if atom(v) then %mr_orderedQ(u, v) else true
   )
   else if atom(v) then false
   else if is(op(u) = op(v)) then (
     if is(length(u) = length(v)) then (
       if is(%mr_leafCount(u) = %mr_leafCount(v))
       then %mr_simplerQ_scan(u, v)
       /* leafcounts are concrete integers — decidable */
       else is(%mr_leafCount(u) < %mr_leafCount(v))
     )
     else is(length(u) < length(v))
   )
   else (
     if is(%mr_leafCount(u) = %mr_leafCount(v))
     then not(%mr_orderedQ(v, u))
     else is(%mr_leafCount(u) < %mr_leafCount(v))))$

 /* Rubi :732  SimplerSqrtQ[u, v] — Rt[u,2] simpler than Rt[v,2]. The
    pending-sign decline (Rubi's If unevaluated on undecidable LtQ) is
    reproduced by the unknown guard: undecidable sign -> false. */
 %mr_simplerSqrtQ(u, v) := block([sqrtu, sqrtv, zu, zv],
   zu : is(u < 0),
   zv : is(v < 0),
   if is(zv = true) and is(zu = false) then true
   else if is(zu = true) and is(zv = false) then false
   else if is(zv = 'unknown) or is(zu = 'unknown) then false
   else (
     sqrtu : sqrt(u),
     sqrtv : sqrt(v),
     if integerp(sqrtu) then (
       /* concrete integers / rationals / integer leafcounts — the
          comparisons are decidable, so a clean boolean is returned
          (the .m's PosQ[u]&&PosQ[v] branch is the BARE LeafCount
          comparison: a tie answers false, no OrderedQ tiebreak) */
       if integerp(sqrtv) then is(sqrtu < sqrtv) else true
     )
     else if integerp(sqrtv) then false
     else if %mr_rationalQ(sqrtu) then (
       if %mr_rationalQ(sqrtv) then is(sqrtu < sqrtv)
       else true
     )
     else if %mr_rationalQ(sqrtv) then false
     else if %mr_posQ(u) then (
       if %mr_posQ(v)
       then is(%mr_leafCount(sqrtu) < %mr_leafCount(sqrtv))
       else true
     )
     else if %mr_posQ(v) then false
     else if is(%mr_leafCount(sqrtu) < %mr_leafCount(sqrtv)) = true
      then true
     else if is(%mr_leafCount(sqrtv) < %mr_leafCount(sqrtu)) = true
      then false
     else not(%mr_orderedQ(v, u))))$

 /* Rubi :784  SumSimplerAuxQ — the three .m definitions in dispatch
    order: v a sum -> AND over v's terms (rational or aux(u, term)); u
    a sum -> OR over u's terms; base -> v # 0, equal NonnumericFactors,
    NF[u]/NF[v] < -1/2 (or = -1/2 with NF[u] < 0). */
 %mr_sumSimplerAuxQ(u, v) := block([i, n, r, m, nf],
   if %mr_sumQ(v) then (
     r : true,
     i : 0,
     n : length(v),
     while is(r = true) and i < n do (
       i : i + 1,
       if not(%mr_rationalQ(part(v, i)))
       and not(%mr_sumSimplerAuxQ(u, part(v, i)))
       then r : false),
     r
   )
   else if %mr_sumQ(u) then (
     r : false,
     i : 0,
     n : length(u),
     while is(r = false) and i < n do (
       i : i + 1,
       if is(%mr_sumSimplerAuxQ(part(u, i), v) = true) then r : true),
     r
   )
   else (
     m : %mr_numericFactor(u),
     nf : %mr_numericFactor(v),
     if is(v # 0) = true
    and is(%mr_nonnumericFactors(u) = %mr_nonnumericFactors(v)) = true
    and (is(m/nf < -1/2) = true
    or (is(m/nf = -1/2) = true and is(m < 0) = true))
     then true else false))$

 /* Rubi :774  SumSimplerQ[u, v] — is u + v simpler than u. The 36
    class-1 sites pass (exponent-parameter, integer-constant). */
 %mr_sumSimplerQ(u, v) := block([],
   if %mr_rationalQ(u) and %mr_rationalQ(v) then (
     if is(v = 0) then false
     else if is(v > 0) = true then is(u < -1) = true
     else is(u >= -v) = true
   )
   else %mr_sumSimplerAuxQ(expand(u), expand(v)))$

 /* ---- coefficient / degree machinery --------------------------------
 * Maxima's coefficient/degree/maxexpt are NOUNS in this build (T4 §6,
 * re-measured 2026-08-20), so %mr_coeff and the degree-based %mr_polyQ
 * are implemented over the term structure of the expanded numerator
 * (Rubi's Coeff :1246 works on Together[expr] too — same reduction). */

/* exponent of x in one monomial term of an expanded expression, or
   false if the term is not a monomial in x (x inside a function, a
   sum, a fraction, or a fractional/negative power) */
/* MEASURED (2026-08-20): op() and length() are hard errors on atoms in
   this build, so every structural walk tests atom() first. */
%mr_term_xexp(t, x) := block([i, f, p, ok],
  if atom(t) then (
    if is(t = x) then 1 else 0
  )
  elseif op(t) = "-" then %mr_term_xexp(part(t, 1), x)
  elseif op(t) = "*" then (
    p : 0,
    ok : true,
    for i : 1 thru length(t) do (
      f : part(t, i),
      if freeof(x, f) then true
      elseif is(f = x) then p : p + 1
      elseif op(f) = "^" and is(part(f, 1) = x)
        and integerp(part(f, 2)) and part(f, 2) > 0 then p : p + part(f, 2)
      else ok : false
    ),
    if ok then p else false
  )
  elseif op(t) = "/" then (
    /* a rational-coefficient term: Maxima stores 3/4*x^3 as the
       QUOTIENT node 3*x^3/4 (expand(3/4*x^3) = 3*x^3/4, measured
       2026-08-25 on 5.50.0/SBCL — this is what sent %mr_degree of
       (3/4)*x^3 to -1, derailing the 1.2.1.6 r5 reduction on 1.2.1.3
       e1058). The x-exponent lives entirely in the numerator; an
       x-bearing denominator makes the term a fraction, not a
       polynomial monomial. */
    if freeof(x, part(t, 2))
    then %mr_term_xexp(part(t, 1), x)
    else false
  )
  elseif op(t) = "^" then (
    /* a bare-power term as a WHOLE term after expand. base x with a
       positive integer exponent -> that exponent; x^(-1) is 1/x (a
       fraction) and x^(1/2)/x^a are not polynomial powers (review fix
       2026-08-20, bug B). A power with an x-FREE base (d^2, c^3, ...)
       is a degree-0 monomial — returning false here DROPPED every
       symbol-power constant term from coefficient/degree walks
       (measured 2026-08-25, 5.50.0/SBCL: %mr_coeff(d^2 - a*e^2/c, x, 0)
       lost the d^2, breaking 1.2.1.6 r4 on (e*x+d)^4 — 1.2.1.2 e2202
       wrong answer, resid 3.19). An x-bearing base that is not x
       itself ((a+b*x)^2) is not a monomial -> false. */
    if is(part(t, 1) = x) then (
      if integerp(part(t, 2)) and part(t, 2) > 0 then part(t, 2) else false
    )
    else if freeof(x, part(t, 1)) then 0
         else false
  )
  else false)$

/* the x-free coefficient of x^n in one monomial term (caller ensures
   %mr_term_xexp(t, x) = n) */
%mr_term_coeff(t, x, n) := block([i, f, c],
  if atom(t) then (
    /* the atom being x itself has coefficient 1 at n = 1 (the atom
       branch of %mr_term_xexp already knows x has exponent 1 — mirror
       it here; review fix 2026-08-20, bug A) */
    if is(t = x) then (if n = 1 then 1 else 0)
    else (if n = 0 then t else 0)
  )
  elseif op(t) = "-" then -%mr_term_coeff(part(t, 1), x, n)
  elseif op(t) = "*" then (
    c : 1,
    for i : 1 thru length(t) do (
      f : part(t, i),
      if freeof(x, f) then c : c * f
      elseif is(f = x) then n : n - 1
      elseif op(f) = "^" and is(part(f, 1) = x) then n : n - part(f, 2)
    ),
    c
  )
  elseif op(t) = "/" then (
    /* quotient node (3*x^3/4 — see the %mr_term_xexp /-case note);
       the caller ensures %mr_term_xexp(t, x) = n, which for an
       x-free denominator is the numerator's x-exponent */
    if freeof(x, part(t, 2))
    then %mr_term_coeff(part(t, 1), x, n)/part(t, 2)
    else 0
  )
  elseif op(t) = "^" then (
    /* a bare-power term (the caller ensures %mr_term_xexp(t, x) = n).
       A pure x-power has coefficient 1; an x-free-base power (d^2 —
       see the %mr_term_xexp /-note sibling fix) is its own constant
       coefficient at n = 0 and nothing elsewhere. */
    if is(part(t, 1) = x) then 1
    else if freeof(x, part(t, 1)) then (if n = 0 then t else 0)
         else 0
  )
  else 0)$

/* w : expand(u) — NOT expand(num(rat(u))) (measured 2026-08-25,
   5.50.0/SBCL): rat() on a symbolic polynomial EXTRACTS the symbolic
   content into the denominator (rat((2abc e^4 + ...)*x + ...) put c^3
   below the line), so num(rat(u)) was a c^3-RESCALED copy of u and
   every coefficient came back wrong (EQ_f/EQ_g false against the
   built-in coeff; 1.2.1.6 r4 on 1.2.1.2 e2202). Rubi's Coeff is
   Coefficient[expr, x, n] directly (Rubi.m, Together only as the
   fallback when the direct and Together values disagree) — no
   content manipulation — and every %mr_coeff call site feeds a
   PolyQ-confirmed polynomial, for which expand(u) is exact. A
   non-polynomial u yields fraction terms whose %mr_term_xexp is
   false -> 0 (conservative, matches the no-monomial reading). */
%mr_coeff3(u, x, n) := block([w, i, t, c, p],
  w : expand(u),
  if atom(w) then (
    p : %mr_term_xexp(w, x),
    if p # false and p = n then %mr_term_coeff(w, x, n) else 0
  )
  elseif op(w) = "+" then (
    c : 0,
    for i : 1 thru length(w) do (
      t : part(w, i),
      p : %mr_term_xexp(t, x),
      if p # false and p = n then c : c + %mr_term_coeff(t, x, n)
    ),
    c
  )
  else (
    p : %mr_term_xexp(w, x),
    if p # false and p = n then %mr_term_coeff(w, x, n) else 0
  ))$

/* The generator's powered-form Coeff: Coeff[u, base^power, n] is the
   coefficient of x^(power*n) when base is the integration variable. */
%mr_coeff4(u, base, power, n) := %mr_coeff3(u, base, power*n)$

%mr_coeff : lambda([u, base, a, [b]],
  if length(b) = 0 then %mr_coeff3(u, base, a)
  else %mr_coeff4(u, base, a, part(b, 1)))$

/* degree in x of a (confirmed) polynomial; degree(0) := 0 by contract
   (the 3-arg PolyQ's NeQ[Coeff[...],0] guard is what rejects 0).
   TOTAL (review fix 2026-08-20, bug B): never leaks a boolean — a term
   that is not a pure x-power (fraction, fractional/symbolic power, x
   inside a function) contributes the sentinel -1, so a non-polynomial
   whole yields -1 (Mathematica Degree parity: Degree[1/x, x] -> -1) */
%mr_degree(u, x) := block([e, i, d, p],
  e : expand(u),
  if is(e = 0) then 0
  elseif freeof(x, e) then 0
  elseif atom(e) then %mr_term_xexp(e, x)
  elseif op(e) = "+" then (
    d : -1,
    for i : 1 thru length(e) do (
      p : %mr_term_xexp(part(e, i), x),
      if p # false and p > d then d : p
    ),
    d
  )
  else (
    p : %mr_term_xexp(e, x),
    if p # false then p else -1))$

/* ---- PolyQ / LinearQ ------------------------------------------------
 * Maxima has no function overloading and no ... rest args in this
 * build (both measured 2026-08-20), and a wrong-arity call is a hard
 * error (measured) — so the 2-arg form (the probed form, and Rubi's
 * PolyQ[u,x]) owns the %mr_polyQ name; the 3-arg form (Rubi's
 * PolyQ[u,x,n], :505) is the internal %mr_polyDegQ. The generator's
 * table maps both arities to %mr_polyQ (1:1 rename) — an open
 * generator/gap for the Task 6 full-class generation (82 3-arg uses in
 * class 1); see the task-5 report. */
%mr_polyformQ(e, x) := block([i, ok],
  if atom(e) then (
    is(e = x) or freeof(x, e)
  )
  elseif op(e) = "-" then %mr_polyformQ(part(e, 1), x)
  elseif op(e) = "+" then (
    ok : true,
    for i : 1 thru length(e) do
      if is(%mr_polyformQ(part(e, i), x)) = false then ok : false,
    ok
  )
  elseif op(e) = "*" then (
    ok : true,
    for i : 1 thru length(e) do
      if is(%mr_polyformQ(part(e, i), x)) = false then ok : false,
    ok
  )
  elseif op(e) = "^" then (
    /* a polynomial power needs an explicit nonnegative integer exponent
       (x^a with symbolic a is not a polynomial, as in Mathematica's
       PolynomialQ) */
    if integerp(part(e, 2)) and is(part(e, 2) >= 0)
      and freeof(x, part(e, 2))
    then %mr_polyformQ(part(e, 1), x)
    else false
  )
  else freeof(x, e))$

/* Rubi :501  PolyQ[u,x] := PolynomialQ[u,x] || PolynomialQ[Together[u],x]
   For a rational u the two disjuncts coincide (Together changes only
   the num/den split), so one together + a structural numerator check.
   The denominator-free check catches the pseudo-polynomial case the
   .m comment warns about (PolynomialQ on an uncancelled pseudo-rat). */
%mr_polyQ(u, x) := block([v],
  v : %mr_together(u),
  freeof(x, denom(v)) and %mr_polyformQ(num(v), x))$

/* Rubi :505  PolyQ[u,x,n] := PolyQ[u,x] && EqQ[Exponent[u,x],n]
                        && NeQ[Coefficient[u,x,n],0]
   (the If/With split in the source is equivalent once v = Together[u]
   is computed once) */
%mr_polyDegQ(u, x, n) := block([v],
  v : %mr_together(u),
  %mr_polyQ(v, x) and %mr_eqQ(%mr_degree(v, x), n)
    and %mr_neQ(%mr_coeff(v, x, n), 0))$

/* ---- PolyQ power forms (Task 6 Step 0) ------------------------------
 * Rubi :512/:522/:533  PolyQ[u, x^n] / PolyQ[u, x^v] / PolyQ[u, x^v, n].
 * The base is an EXPRESSION B (x, or a capture value such as a + b*x —
 * the class-1 uses PolyQ[Pq, v^n] pass the capture v as base): u is a
 * polynomial in B^v iff, with t a fresh symbol,
 *   r = expand(subst(t, B, u))
 * has no t in any denominator and every term is a t-monomial whose t-
 * exponent k is a non-negative multiple of v. For B = x this is exactly
 * the brief's contract (no x in a denominator, every term's x-exponent
 * a non-negative multiple of v). The degree form matches Rubi :533
 * exactly: EqQ[Expon[u,x^v],n] is max k/v = n (strict). The
 * NeQ[Coeff[u,x^v,n],0] clause is subsumed — when d = n some term has
 * k/v = n, and its coefficient is structurally nonzero after expand
 * (a zero coefficient would drop the term). u = 0 is rejected by
 * guard (Rubi's Expon[0,x^v] is -Infinity, so no degree form holds). */
%mr_polyPowerQ(u, B, v) := block([t, r, i, term, k, ok],
  t : gensym("mrp"),
  r : expand(subst(t, B, u)),
  if is(freeof(t, denom(r))) = false then false
  else if not numberp(v) or v < 0 then false
  else (
    if v = 0 then is(freeof(t, r))
    else (
      /* %mr_term_xexp returns false (never negative) on a term that is
         not a pure non-negative power of the variable, so k >= 0 holds
         whenever k # false */
      ok : true,
      if atom(r) then (
        k : %mr_term_xexp(r, t),
        if k = false or not integerp(k/v) then ok : false
      )
      else (
        for i : 1 thru length(r) do (
          term : part(r, i),
          k : %mr_term_xexp(term, t),
          if k = false or not integerp(k/v) then ok : false
        )
      ),
      ok
    )
  ))$

%mr_polyDegPowerQ(u, B, v, n) := block([t, r, i, term, k, d],
  if is(%mr_polyPowerQ(u, B, v)) = false then false
  else (
    /* recompute r identically to %mr_polyPowerQ and take the maximum
       k/v (the degree in B^v); a non-monomial term cannot occur here
       (polyPowerQ true), so k # false for every term */
     t : gensym("mrp"),
     r : expand(subst(t, B, u)),
     /* u = 0: Rubi's Expon[0, x^v] is -Infinity, so no degree form
        holds (the atom-0 term would read as a degree-0 monomial and
        over-fire at n = 0) */
     if is(r = 0) = true then false
     else (
       d : 0,
       if atom(r) then (
         k : %mr_term_xexp(r, t),
         if k # false and k/v > d then d : k/v
       )
       else (
         for i : 1 thru length(r) do (
           term : part(r, i),
           k : %mr_term_xexp(term, t),
           if k # false and k/v > d then d : k/v
         )
       ),
       is(d = n)
     )
   ))$

/* Rubi :1373  LinearQ[u,x] := PolyQ[u,x,1] (list form dropped — no
   list-valued integrands in the emitted conds) */
%mr_linearQ(u, x) := %mr_polyDegQ(u, x, 1)$

/* ---- trinomial/quadratic/binomial-degree predicates (Task 7a
 * cluster D) ----------------------------------------------------------
 * The shape predicates of the 1.3/1.4 normalization rule families:
 * QuadraticQ, TrinomialQ (+ TrinomialParts), BinomialDegree, and the
 * four *MatchQ "simple shape" tests. Line-ports of the pinned Rubi
 * definitions (provenance per function); the .m ListQ[u] arm is the
 * generator's list-packing. The *MatchQ ports call the cluster-A3
 * matcher with fresh marker atoms (the %mr_mq_ family binds by
 * identity); the cond is the lambda-over-bindings-list form the
 * matcher contract requires.
 *
 * The *MatchQ conds are threaded through the matcher's structural
 * backtracking (the ["final"]/["match"]/["part"] continuation search
 * in %mr_mq_search), so a unit-coefficient term (a bare x^n / x^(2n)
 * summand) no longer traps the search on the coefficient-absorbing
 * binding: the freeof cond fails on that binding and the search
 * continues to the b := 1 / real-exponent binding. REMAINING DIVERGENCE
 * (the matcher's absent rule, not the cond search): a product/sum
 * factor that contains a literal (b_*x, c_*x^2) cannot be treated as
 * ABSENT as a whole, so the .m corner where the optional coefficient
 * defaults to 0 and deletes the whole literal-base term still answers
 * false here. Those corners are unreachable at the class-1 call sites
 * (the LinearQ / QuadraticQ / TrinomialQ guards guarantee a real
 * literal-base term). */

/* Rubi :846  BinomialDegree[u,x] := BinomialParts[u,x][[3]]. The .m
   False[[3]] hard-errors on a non-binomial; the port fails closed
   with false (every class-1 call site gates on BinomialQ[u,x] first). */
%mr_binomialDegree(u, x) := block([L],
  L : %mr_binomial_parts(u, x),
  if atom(L) then false else part(L, 3))$

/* Rubi :1380  QuadraticQ[u,x] — a degree-2 polynomial that is not the
   a*x^2 monomial. The .m ===0 (IdentityQ) maps onto is(c = 0): this
   build's is(a = b) is true/false, never unknown (RC1), and
   is(e = 0) = true iff e simplifies to the structural 0. */
%mr_quadraticQ(u, x) := block([i, v],
  if not atom(u) and is(op(u) = "[") then (
    v : true,
    for i : 1 thru length(u) do
      if not %mr_quadraticQ(part(u, i), x) then v : false,
    v
  )
  else %mr_polyDegQ(u, x, 2)
       and not (is(%mr_coeff(u, x, 0) = 0)
                and is(%mr_coeff(u, x, 1) = 0)))$

/* Rubi :929  TrinomialParts[u,x] — [a, b, c, n] iff u is equivalent
   to a + b*x^n + c*x^(2*n) (n, b, c nonzero), else false. LINE port
   of the .m If-chain (polynomial / power / product / sum; First/Rest
   split as first term-or-factor over the rest). CoefficientList is
   the [c0..cd] walk over %mr_coeff (degree-gated; %mr_coeff is exact
   on a confirmed polynomial). DEVIATIONS, documented:
    - the .m's Drop chain Drop[Drop[lst,{m}],1],-1 (m = (Length+1)/2)
     scans coefficients x^(n+2)..x^(2n-1) ONLY — the coefficients
     x^(n-1) and x^(n+1) (the ones adjacent to the middle term) are
     UNCHECKED; the port reproduces the scan exactly (the 1+x^2+x^3+x^4
     probe pins the Rubi-faithful laxness);
   - the .m's :994 b-slot (lst3[[2]] where the reconstruction says
     lst2[[2]] — a typo in the pinned clone, the branch reachable only
     through a binomial-first/trinomial-rest sum split that
     auto-combining makes unreachable) is ported as written. */
%mr_trinomial_parts(u, x) := block([d, L, i, m, ok, f1, r1, L1, L2, L3, L4],
  if %mr_polynomialQ(u, x) then (
    d : %mr_degree(u, x),
    L : [],
    for i : 0 thru d do L : append(L, [%mr_coeff(u, x, i)]),
    if is(length(L) < 3) or evenp(length(L))
    or %mr_eqQ(part(L, (length(L) + 1)/2), 0)
    then false
    else (
      m : (length(L) + 1)/2,
      ok : true,
      for i : m + 2 thru 2*m - 2 do
        if %mr_eqQ(part(L, i), 0) then ok : false,
      if ok
      then [part(L, 1), part(L, m), part(L, length(L)), (length(L) - 1)/2]
      else false
    )
  )
  elseif not atom(u) and op(u) = "^" then (
    /* PowerQ[u] && EqQ[u[[2]], 2]: the square of a binomial */
    if %mr_eqQ(part(u, 2), 2) then (
      L1 : %mr_binomial_parts(part(u, 1), x),
      if atom(L1) or %mr_eqQ(part(L1, 1), 0) then false
      else [part(L1, 1)^2, 2*part(L1, 1)*part(L1, 2), part(L1, 2)^2, part(L1, 3)]
    )
    else false
  )
  elseif op(u) = "-" then (
    /* the .m's Times[-1, u] goes through the ProductQ branch with
       the -1 factor: {-1*a, -1*b, -1*c, n} */
    L : %mr_trinomial_parts(part(u, 1), x),
    if atom(L) then false
    else [-part(L, 1), -part(L, 2), -part(L, 3), part(L, 4)]
  )
  elseif not atom(u) and op(u) = "*" then (
    f1 : part(u, 1),
    r1 : part(u, 2),
    for i : 3 thru length(u) do r1 : r1 * part(u, i),
    if freeof(x, f1) then (
      L2 : %mr_trinomial_parts(r1, x),
      if atom(L2) then false
      else [f1*part(L2, 1), f1*part(L2, 2), f1*part(L2, 3), part(L2, 4)]
    )
    else if freeof(x, r1) then (
      L1 : %mr_trinomial_parts(f1, x),
      if atom(L1) then false
      else [r1*part(L1, 1), r1*part(L1, 2), r1*part(L1, 3), part(L1, 4)]
    )
    else (
      L1 : %mr_binomial_parts(f1, x),
      if atom(L1) then false
      else (
        L2 : %mr_binomial_parts(r1, x),
        if atom(L2) then false
        else if %mr_eqQ(part(L1, 3), part(L2, 3))
          and %mr_neQ(part(L1, 1)*part(L2, 2) + part(L1, 2)*part(L2, 1), 0)
        then [part(L1, 1)*part(L2, 1),
              part(L1, 1)*part(L2, 2) + part(L1, 2)*part(L2, 1),
              part(L1, 2)*part(L2, 2), part(L1, 3)]
        else false
      )
    )
  )
  elseif %mr_sumQ(u) then (
    f1 : part(u, 1),
    r1 : %mr_rest_sum(u),
    if freeof(x, f1) then (
      L2 : %mr_trinomial_parts(r1, x),
      if atom(L2) then false
      else [f1 + part(L2, 1), part(L2, 2), part(L2, 3), part(L2, 4)]
    )
    else if freeof(x, r1) then (
      L1 : %mr_trinomial_parts(f1, x),
      if atom(L1) then false
      else [r1 + part(L1, 1), part(L1, 2), part(L1, 3), part(L1, 4)]
    )
    else (
      L1 : %mr_trinomial_parts(f1, x),
      if atom(L1) then (
        L3 : %mr_binomial_parts(f1, x),
        if atom(L3) then false
        else (
          L2 : %mr_trinomial_parts(r1, x),
          if atom(L2) then (
            L4 : %mr_binomial_parts(r1, x),
            if atom(L4) then false
            else if %mr_eqQ(part(L3, 3), 2*part(L4, 3))
              then [part(L3, 1) + part(L4, 1), part(L4, 2),
                    part(L3, 2), part(L4, 3)]
              else if %mr_eqQ(part(L4, 3), 2*part(L3, 3))
              then [part(L3, 1) + part(L4, 1), part(L3, 2),
                    part(L4, 2), part(L3, 3)]
              else false
          )
          else if %mr_eqQ(part(L3, 3), part(L2, 4))
            and %mr_neQ(part(L3, 2) + part(L2, 2), 0)
          then [part(L3, 1) + part(L2, 1), part(L3, 2) + part(L2, 2),
                part(L2, 3), part(L2, 4)]
          /* the .m :994 b-slot as written (see the DEVIATIONS note) */
          else if %mr_eqQ(part(L3, 3), 2*part(L2, 4))
            and %mr_neQ(part(L3, 2) + part(L2, 3), 0)
          then [part(L3, 1) + part(L2, 1), part(L3, 2),
                part(L3, 2) + part(L2, 3), part(L2, 4)]
          else false
        )
      )
      else (
        L2 : %mr_trinomial_parts(r1, x),
        if atom(L2) then (
          L4 : %mr_binomial_parts(r1, x),
          if atom(L4) then false
          else if %mr_eqQ(part(L4, 3), part(L1, 4))
            and %mr_neQ(part(L1, 2) + part(L4, 2), 0)
          then [part(L1, 1) + part(L4, 1), part(L1, 2) + part(L4, 2),
                part(L1, 3), part(L1, 4)]
          else if %mr_eqQ(part(L4, 3), 2*part(L1, 4))
            and %mr_neQ(part(L1, 3) + part(L4, 2), 0)
          then [part(L1, 1) + part(L4, 1), part(L1, 2),
                part(L1, 3) + part(L4, 2), part(L1, 4)]
          else false
        )
        else if %mr_eqQ(part(L1, 4), part(L2, 4))
          and %mr_neQ(part(L1, 2) + part(L2, 2), 0)
          and %mr_neQ(part(L1, 3) + part(L2, 3), 0)
        then [part(L1, 1) + part(L2, 1), part(L1, 2) + part(L2, 2),
              part(L1, 3) + part(L2, 3), part(L1, 4)]
        else false
      )
    )
  )
  else false)$

/* Rubi :568  TrinomialQ[u,x] — ListQ[TrinomialParts[u,x]] &&
   Not[QuadraticQ[u,x]] && Not[MatchQ[u, w_^2 /; BinomialQ[w,x]]]: a
   trinomial that is neither a quadratic (the n = 1 corner) nor the
   square of a binomial (the perfect-square corner the Chebyshev
   rules handle separately). */
%mr_trinomialQ(u, x) := block([i, v, L],
  if not atom(u) and is(op(u) = "[") then (
    v : true,
    for i : 1 thru length(u) do
      if not %mr_trinomialQ(part(u, i), x) then v : false,
    v
  )
  else (
    L : %mr_trinomial_parts(u, x),
    not atom(L) and not %mr_quadraticQ(u, x)
    and not %mr_matchQ(u, _mr_triQ_r1mq1_w^2,
      lambda([%mr_mqb],
        %mr_binomialQ2(%mr_mk(_mr_triQ_r1mq1_w, %mr_mqb), x)))))$

/* Rubi :1420  LinearMatchQ[u,x] — MatchQ[u, a_.+b_.*x /; FreeQ[{a,b},x]].
   The x literal in the product factor cannot be absent (see the
   section note for the corner where the WHOLE b_*x term is absent —
   unreachable at the call sites, LinearQ-guarded). */
%mr_linearMatchQ(u, x) := block([i, v],
  if not atom(u) and is(op(u) = "[") then (
    v : true,
    for i : 1 thru length(u) do
      if not %mr_linearMatchQ(part(u, i), x) then v : false,
    v
  )
  else %mr_matchQ(u, _mr_linMq_r1mq1_a + _mr_linMq_r1mq1_b * x,
    lambda([%mr_mqb],
      freeof(x, %mr_mk(_mr_linMq_r1mq1_a, %mr_mqb))
      and freeof(x, %mr_mk(_mr_linMq_r1mq1_b, %mr_mqb)))))$

/* Rubi :1427  QuadraticMatchQ[u,x] — MatchQ[u, a_.+b_.*x+c_.*x^2 /;
   FreeQ[{a,b,c},x]] || MatchQ[u, a_.+c_.*x^2 /; FreeQ[{a,c},x]]. The
   literal x / x^2 powers cannot be absent (the a + b*x corner is the
   2nd pattern's absent x^2 term — unreachable at the call sites,
   QuadraticQ-guarded). */
%mr_quadraticMatchQ(u, x) := block([i, v],
  if not atom(u) and is(op(u) = "[") then (
    v : true,
    for i : 1 thru length(u) do
      if not %mr_quadraticMatchQ(part(u, i), x) then v : false,
    v
  )
  else %mr_matchQ(u,
    _mr_quadMq_r1mq1_a + _mr_quadMq_r1mq1_b * x + _mr_quadMq_r1mq1_c * x^2,
    lambda([%mr_mqb],
      freeof(x, %mr_mk(_mr_quadMq_r1mq1_a, %mr_mqb))
      and freeof(x, %mr_mk(_mr_quadMq_r1mq1_b, %mr_mqb))
      and freeof(x, %mr_mk(_mr_quadMq_r1mq1_c, %mr_mqb))))
  or %mr_matchQ(u, _mr_quadMq_r2mq1_a + _mr_quadMq_r2mq1_c * x^2,
    lambda([%mr_mqb],
      freeof(x, %mr_mk(_mr_quadMq_r2mq1_a, %mr_mqb))
      and freeof(x, %mr_mk(_mr_quadMq_r2mq1_c, %mr_mqb)))))$

/* Rubi :1444  BinomialMatchQ[u,x] — MatchQ[u, a_.+b_.*x^n_. /;
   FreeQ[{a,b,n},x]]. The orderless partition search tries every
   stored order of the pattern factors, and the freeof cond is
   threaded through the search, so a bare x^n term binds b = 1 and
   the real n (the coefficient-absorbing binding fails freeof and is
   backtracked away). */
%mr_binomialMatchQ(u, x) := block([i, v],
  if not atom(u) and is(op(u) = "[") then (
    v : true,
    for i : 1 thru length(u) do
      if not %mr_binomialMatchQ(part(u, i), x) then v : false,
    v
  )
  else %mr_matchQ(u, _mr_binMq_r1mq1_a + _mr_binMq_r1mq1_b * x^_mr_binMq_r1mq1_n,
    lambda([%mr_mqb],
      freeof(x, %mr_mk(_mr_binMq_r1mq1_a, %mr_mqb))
      and freeof(x, %mr_mk(_mr_binMq_r1mq1_b, %mr_mqb))
      and freeof(x, %mr_mk(_mr_binMq_r1mq1_n, %mr_mqb)))))$

/* Rubi :1458  TrinomialMatchQ[u,x] — MatchQ[u, a_.+b_.*x^n_.+c_.*x^j_.
   /; FreeQ[{a,b,c,n},x] && EqQ[j-2*n,0]] (j is not FreeQ'd: j = 2n
   and n x-free implies it). The orderless partition search tries
   every stored order of the three pattern factors, and the j = 2*n
   cond is threaded through the search, so the Rubi-faithful n/j
   assignment is found even when Maxima's stored order presents the
   other assignment first (and a unit-coefficient term no longer
   traps the search on the coefficient-absorbing binding). */
%mr_trinomialMatchQ(u, x) := block([i, v],
   if not atom(u) and is(op(u) = "[") then (
    v : true,
    for i : 1 thru length(u) do
      if not %mr_trinomialMatchQ(part(u, i), x) then v : false,
    v
  )
  else %mr_matchQ(u,
    _mr_triMq_r1mq1_a
    + _mr_triMq_r1mq1_b * x^_mr_triMq_r1mq1_n
    + _mr_triMq_r1mq1_c * x^_mr_triMq_r1mq1_j,
    lambda([%mr_mqb],
      freeof(x, %mr_mk(_mr_triMq_r1mq1_a, %mr_mqb))
      and freeof(x, %mr_mk(_mr_triMq_r1mq1_b, %mr_mqb))
      and freeof(x, %mr_mk(_mr_triMq_r1mq1_c, %mr_mqb))
      and freeof(x, %mr_mk(_mr_triMq_r1mq1_n, %mr_mqb))
      and %mr_eqQ(%mr_mk(_mr_triMq_r1mq1_j, %mr_mqb)
            - 2*%mr_mk(_mr_triMq_r1mq1_n, %mr_mqb), 0))))$

/* ---- generalized binomial/trinomial predicates (Task 7a cluster E) --
 * The six Generalized* shapes of the 1.4.2 normalization rules:
 * GeneralizedBinomialQ/MatchQ/Degree and GeneralizedTrinomialQ/MatchQ/
 * Degree (pinned Rubi :579-593, :1016-1088, :1450-1468). The .m Parts
 * definitions are pattern rules, so the ports run the cluster-A3
 * matcher with fresh marker atoms and read the binding back. The
 * binding capture is global-state based (the %mr_mq_capture family):
 * the Maxima lambda the matcher calls cannot return the binding list
 * to the caller (its body is a fixed expression), so the cond lambda
 * stashes the winning binding in %mr_mq_capture and answers true.
 *
 * The product cases (a_*u_, x^m_*u_) are guarded against the trivial
 * no-progress binding (a := 1 / u := self, x^m := x^1 absent / u :=
 * self) with the %mr_mq_capture_self check: without it the .m's
 * optional-factor reading recurses on the unchanged u.
 *
 * GeneralizedTrinomialParts pins the POSITIVE orientation (n - q > 0)
 * in the sum-pattern cond: the .m's EqQ[r, 2*n-q] is satisfied by
 * either endpoint as q, and Maxima's stored (descending) term order
 * otherwise traps the search on the negative-gap binding, while the
 * GeneralizedTrinomialDegree use at the 1.4.2 call sites (EqQ against
 * a BinomialDegree) can only see the positive gap. The boolean
 * GeneralizedTrinomialQ / MatchQ answers are unchanged (a 3-term
 * progression always has a positive orientation). */

/* capture the binding list of the first structural match whose cond
   is true (the cond is a lambda over the binding list, as in
   %mr_matchQ). %mr_mq_capture_cond / %mr_mq_capture_self are globals
   because the fixed capture lambda below cannot close over locals
   (the Maxima lambda-scope limitation, matcher section note). */
%mr_mq_capture : false$
%mr_mq_capture_cond : false$
%mr_mq_capture_self : false$

%mr_mq_capture_lambda : lambda([%mr_mqb],
  block([r],
    r : errcatch(%mr_mq_capture_cond(%mr_mqb)),
    if r = [] then false
    else if is(first(r)) = true then (
      %mr_mq_capture : %mr_mqb,
      true
    )
    else false))$

%mr_mq_capture_binding(u, pattern, cond) := block([r],
  %mr_mq_capture : false,
  %mr_mq_capture_cond : cond,
  r : %mr_matchQ(u, pattern, %mr_mq_capture_lambda),
  if r then %mr_mq_capture else false)$

/* Rubi :1022-1045  GeneralizedBinomialParts[u,x] — [a, b, n, q] iff
   u ~ a*x^q + b*x^n with n - q > 0 (the .m's PosQ[n-q]), else false.
   Branch order: unary minus / sum pattern / a_*u_ (scale) /
   x^m_*u_ over a generalized binomial (shift) / x^m_*u_ over an
   ordinary binomial (the q := m shift of a + b*x^n). */
%mr_generalizedBinomial_parts(u, x) := block([r, B, A, U, L, M],
  if not atom(u) and is(op(u) = "-") then (
    L : %mr_generalizedBinomial_parts(part(u, 1), x),
    if atom(L) then false
    else [-part(L, 1), -part(L, 2), part(L, 3), part(L, 4)]
  )
  else (
    r : false,
    if not atom(u) and is(op(u) = "+") then (
      B : %mr_mq_capture_binding(
        u,
        _mr_gbp1_r1mq1_a * x^_mr_gbp1_r1mq1_q
        + _mr_gbp1_r1mq1_b * x^_mr_gbp1_r1mq1_n,
        lambda([%mr_mqb],
          freeof(x, %mr_mk(_mr_gbp1_r1mq1_a, %mr_mqb))
          and freeof(x, %mr_mk(_mr_gbp1_r1mq1_b, %mr_mqb))
          and freeof(x, %mr_mk(_mr_gbp1_r1mq1_n, %mr_mqb))
          and freeof(x, %mr_mk(_mr_gbp1_r1mq1_q, %mr_mqb))
          and %mr_posQ(
            %mr_mk(_mr_gbp1_r1mq1_n, %mr_mqb)
            - %mr_mk(_mr_gbp1_r1mq1_q, %mr_mqb)))
      ),
      if not atom(B) then r : [
        %mr_mk(_mr_gbp1_r1mq1_a, B),
        %mr_mk(_mr_gbp1_r1mq1_b, B),
        %mr_mk(_mr_gbp1_r1mq1_n, B),
        %mr_mk(_mr_gbp1_r1mq1_q, B)
      ]
    ),
    if atom(r) and not atom(u) and is(op(u) = "*") then (
      %mr_mq_capture_self : u,
      B : %mr_mq_capture_binding(
        u,
        _mr_gbp2_r1mq1_a * _mr_gbp2_r1mq1_u,
        lambda([%mr_mqb],
          freeof(x, %mr_mk(_mr_gbp2_r1mq1_a, %mr_mqb))
          and not is(%mr_mk(_mr_gbp2_r1mq1_a, %mr_mqb) = 1)
          and not is(
            %mr_mk(_mr_gbp2_r1mq1_u, %mr_mqb) = %mr_mq_capture_self))
      ),
      if not atom(B) then (
        A : %mr_mk(_mr_gbp2_r1mq1_a, B),
        U : %mr_mk(_mr_gbp2_r1mq1_u, B),
        L : %mr_generalizedBinomial_parts(U, x),
        if atom(L) then false
        else r : [A*part(L, 1), A*part(L, 2), part(L, 3), part(L, 4)]
      )
    ),
    if atom(r) and not atom(u) and is(op(u) = "*") then (
      %mr_mq_capture_self : u,
      B : %mr_mq_capture_binding(
        u,
        x^_mr_gbp3_r1mq1_m * _mr_gbp3_r1mq1_u,
        lambda([%mr_mqb],
          freeof(x, %mr_mk(_mr_gbp3_r1mq1_m, %mr_mqb))
          and not is(
            %mr_mk(_mr_gbp3_r1mq1_u, %mr_mqb) = %mr_mq_capture_self))
      ),
      if not atom(B) then (
        M : %mr_mk(_mr_gbp3_r1mq1_m, B),
        U : %mr_mk(_mr_gbp3_r1mq1_u, B),
        L : %mr_generalizedBinomial_parts(U, x),
        if atom(L) then false
        else if %mr_neQ(M + part(L, 3), 0)
          and %mr_neQ(M + part(L, 4), 0)
        then r : [part(L, 1), part(L, 2),
                  M + part(L, 3), M + part(L, 4)]
      )
    ),
    if atom(r) and not atom(u) and is(op(u) = "*") then (
      %mr_mq_capture_self : u,
      B : %mr_mq_capture_binding(
        u,
        x^_mr_gbp4_r1mq1_m * _mr_gbp4_r1mq1_u,
        lambda([%mr_mqb],
          freeof(x, %mr_mk(_mr_gbp4_r1mq1_m, %mr_mqb))
          and not is(
            %mr_mk(_mr_gbp4_r1mq1_u, %mr_mqb) = %mr_mq_capture_self))
      ),
      if not atom(B) then (
        M : %mr_mk(_mr_gbp4_r1mq1_m, B),
        U : %mr_mk(_mr_gbp4_r1mq1_u, B),
        L : %mr_binomial_parts(U, x),
        if atom(L) then false
        else if %mr_neQ(M + part(L, 3), 0)
        then r : [part(L, 1), part(L, 2), M + part(L, 3), M]
      )
    ),
    if atom(r) then false else r
  ))$

/* Rubi :579  GeneralizedBinomialQ[u,x] — ListQ[GeneralizedBinomialParts]
   (the ListQ[u] arm is the generator's list-packing). */
%mr_generalizedBinomialQ(u, x) := block([i, v],
  if not atom(u) and is(op(u) = "[") then (
    v : true,
    for i : 1 thru length(u) do
      if not %mr_generalizedBinomialQ(part(u, i), x) then v : false,
    v
  )
  else not atom(%mr_generalizedBinomial_parts(u, x)))$

/* Rubi :1017  GeneralizedBinomialDegree[u,x] := Parts[[3]] - Parts[[4]]
   (n - q). The .m False[[3]] hard-errors on a non-generalized-binomial;
   the port fails closed with false (every class-1 call site gates on
   GeneralizedBinomialQ first). */
%mr_generalizedBinomialDegree(u, x) := block([L],
  L : %mr_generalizedBinomial_parts(u, x),
  if atom(L) then false else part(L, 3) - part(L, 4))$

/* Rubi :1451  GeneralizedBinomialMatchQ[u,x] — MatchQ[u,
   a_.*x^q_.+b_.*x^n_. /; FreeQ[{a,b,n,q},x]]: the SURFACE two-power
   sum, no PosQ gate and no product/PolyQ recursion. */
%mr_generalizedBinomialMatchQ(u, x) := block([i, v],
  if not atom(u) and is(op(u) = "[") then (
    v : true,
    for i : 1 thru length(u) do
      if not %mr_generalizedBinomialMatchQ(part(u, i), x) then v : false,
    v
  )
  else %mr_matchQ(
    u,
    _mr_gbm1_r1mq1_a * x^_mr_gbm1_r1mq1_q
    + _mr_gbm1_r1mq1_b * x^_mr_gbm1_r1mq1_n,
    lambda([%mr_mqb],
      freeof(x, %mr_mk(_mr_gbm1_r1mq1_a, %mr_mqb))
      and freeof(x, %mr_mk(_mr_gbm1_r1mq1_b, %mr_mqb))
      and freeof(x, %mr_mk(_mr_gbm1_r1mq1_n, %mr_mqb))
      and freeof(x, %mr_mk(_mr_gbm1_r1mq1_q, %mr_mqb)))))$

/* Rubi :1058-1088  GeneralizedTrinomialParts[u,x] — [a, b, c, n, q]
   iff u ~ a*x^q + b*x^n + c*x^(2*n-q), else false. Branch order:
   unary minus / sum pattern (positive orientation, section note) /
    a_*u_ (scale) / the PolyQ+Expon List case (the .m's polynomial
    fallback; NeQ[q,0] — the q := 0 form is the ORDINARY trinomial,
    NOT generalized) / x^m_*u_ over a generalized trinomial (shift) /
    x^m_*u_ over an ordinary trinomial (the q := m shift of
    a + b*x^n + c*x^(2n), which is what generalizes the q := 0 form). */
%mr_generalizedTrinomial_parts(u, x) := block([r, B, A, U, L, M, EL],
  if not atom(u) and is(op(u) = "-") then (
    L : %mr_generalizedTrinomial_parts(part(u, 1), x),
    if atom(L) then false
    else [-part(L, 1), -part(L, 2), -part(L, 3),
          part(L, 4), part(L, 5)]
  )
  else (
    r : false,
    if not atom(u) and is(op(u) = "+") then (
      B : %mr_mq_capture_binding(
        u,
        _mr_gtp1_r1mq1_a * x^_mr_gtp1_r1mq1_q
        + _mr_gtp1_r1mq1_b * x^_mr_gtp1_r1mq1_n
        + _mr_gtp1_r1mq1_c * x^_mr_gtp1_r1mq1_r,
        lambda([%mr_mqb],
          freeof(x, %mr_mk(_mr_gtp1_r1mq1_a, %mr_mqb))
          and freeof(x, %mr_mk(_mr_gtp1_r1mq1_b, %mr_mqb))
          and freeof(x, %mr_mk(_mr_gtp1_r1mq1_c, %mr_mqb))
          and freeof(x, %mr_mk(_mr_gtp1_r1mq1_n, %mr_mqb))
          and freeof(x, %mr_mk(_mr_gtp1_r1mq1_q, %mr_mqb))
          and %mr_posQ(
            %mr_mk(_mr_gtp1_r1mq1_n, %mr_mqb)
            - %mr_mk(_mr_gtp1_r1mq1_q, %mr_mqb))
          and %mr_eqQ(
            %mr_mk(_mr_gtp1_r1mq1_r, %mr_mqb)
            - (2*%mr_mk(_mr_gtp1_r1mq1_n, %mr_mqb)
               - %mr_mk(_mr_gtp1_r1mq1_q, %mr_mqb)),
            0))
      ),
      if not atom(B) then r : [
        %mr_mk(_mr_gtp1_r1mq1_a, B),
        %mr_mk(_mr_gtp1_r1mq1_b, B),
        %mr_mk(_mr_gtp1_r1mq1_c, B),
        %mr_mk(_mr_gtp1_r1mq1_n, B),
        %mr_mk(_mr_gtp1_r1mq1_q, B)
      ]
    ),
    if atom(r) and not atom(u) and is(op(u) = "*") then (
      %mr_mq_capture_self : u,
      B : %mr_mq_capture_binding(
        u,
        _mr_gtp2_r1mq1_a * _mr_gtp2_r1mq1_u,
        lambda([%mr_mqb],
          freeof(x, %mr_mk(_mr_gtp2_r1mq1_a, %mr_mqb))
          and not is(%mr_mk(_mr_gtp2_r1mq1_a, %mr_mqb) = 1)
          and not is(
            %mr_mk(_mr_gtp2_r1mq1_u, %mr_mqb) = %mr_mq_capture_self))
      ),
      if not atom(B) then (
        A : %mr_mk(_mr_gtp2_r1mq1_a, B),
        U : %mr_mk(_mr_gtp2_r1mq1_u, B),
        L : %mr_generalizedTrinomial_parts(U, x),
        if atom(L) then false
        else r : [A*part(L, 1), A*part(L, 2), A*part(L, 3),
                  part(L, 4), part(L, 5)]
      )
    ),
    if atom(r) and %mr_polynomialQ(u, x) then (
      EL : %mr_exp_list(u, x),
      if length(EL) = 3
        and %mr_neQ(part(EL, 1), 0)
        and %mr_eqQ(
          part(EL, 3),
          2*part(EL, 2) - part(EL, 1))
      then r : [
        %mr_coeff(u, x, part(EL, 1)),
        %mr_coeff(u, x, part(EL, 2)),
        %mr_coeff(u, x, part(EL, 3)),
        part(EL, 2),
        part(EL, 1)
      ]
    ),
    if atom(r) and not atom(u) and is(op(u) = "*") then (
      %mr_mq_capture_self : u,
      B : %mr_mq_capture_binding(
        u,
        x^_mr_gtp3_r1mq1_m * _mr_gtp3_r1mq1_u,
        lambda([%mr_mqb],
          freeof(x, %mr_mk(_mr_gtp3_r1mq1_m, %mr_mqb))
          and not is(
            %mr_mk(_mr_gtp3_r1mq1_u, %mr_mqb) = %mr_mq_capture_self))
      ),
      if not atom(B) then (
        M : %mr_mk(_mr_gtp3_r1mq1_m, B),
        U : %mr_mk(_mr_gtp3_r1mq1_u, B),
        L : %mr_generalizedTrinomial_parts(U, x),
        if atom(L) then false
        else if %mr_neQ(M + part(L, 4), 0)
          and %mr_neQ(M + part(L, 5), 0)
        then r : [part(L, 1), part(L, 2), part(L, 3),
                  M + part(L, 4), M + part(L, 5)]
      )
    ),
    if atom(r) and not atom(u) and is(op(u) = "*") then (
      %mr_mq_capture_self : u,
      B : %mr_mq_capture_binding(
        u,
        x^_mr_gtp4_r1mq1_m * _mr_gtp4_r1mq1_u,
        lambda([%mr_mqb],
          freeof(x, %mr_mk(_mr_gtp4_r1mq1_m, %mr_mqb))
          and not is(
            %mr_mk(_mr_gtp4_r1mq1_u, %mr_mqb) = %mr_mq_capture_self))
      ),
      if not atom(B) then (
        M : %mr_mk(_mr_gtp4_r1mq1_m, B),
        U : %mr_mk(_mr_gtp4_r1mq1_u, B),
        L : %mr_trinomial_parts(U, x),
        if atom(L) then false
        else if %mr_neQ(M + part(L, 4), 0)
        then r : [part(L, 1), part(L, 2), part(L, 3),
                  M + part(L, 4), M]
      )
    ),
    if atom(r) then false else r
  ))$

/* Rubi :590  GeneralizedTrinomialQ[u,x] — ListQ[GeneralizedTrinomialParts]. */
%mr_generalizedTrinomialQ(u, x) := block([i, v],
  if not atom(u) and is(op(u) = "[") then (
    v : true,
    for i : 1 thru length(u) do
      if not %mr_generalizedTrinomialQ(part(u, i), x) then v : false,
    v
  )
  else not atom(%mr_generalizedTrinomial_parts(u, x)))$

/* Rubi :1053  GeneralizedTrinomialDegree[u,x] := Parts[[4]] - Parts[[5]]
   (n - q). Fails closed with false on a non-generalized-trinomial
   (the .m False[[4]] hard-errors; the call sites gate on Q first). */
%mr_generalizedTrinomialDegree(u, x) := block([L],
  L : %mr_generalizedTrinomial_parts(u, x),
  if atom(L) then false else part(L, 4) - part(L, 5))$

/* Rubi :1465  GeneralizedTrinomialMatchQ[u,x] — MatchQ[u,
   a_.*x^q_.+b_.*x^n_.+c_.*x^r_. /;
   FreeQ[{a,b,c,n,q},x] && EqQ[r-(2*n-q),0]]: the SURFACE three-power
   sum, no product/PolyQ recursion and (unlike the Parts port) no
   PosQ orientation gate. */
%mr_generalizedTrinomialMatchQ(u, x) := block([i, v],
  if not atom(u) and is(op(u) = "[") then (
    v : true,
    for i : 1 thru length(u) do
      if not %mr_generalizedTrinomialMatchQ(part(u, i), x) then v : false,
    v
  )
  else %mr_matchQ(
    u,
    _mr_gtm1_r1mq1_a * x^_mr_gtm1_r1mq1_q
    + _mr_gtm1_r1mq1_b * x^_mr_gtm1_r1mq1_n
    + _mr_gtm1_r1mq1_c * x^_mr_gtm1_r1mq1_r,
    lambda([%mr_mqb],
      freeof(x, %mr_mk(_mr_gtm1_r1mq1_a, %mr_mqb))
      and freeof(x, %mr_mk(_mr_gtm1_r1mq1_b, %mr_mqb))
      and freeof(x, %mr_mk(_mr_gtm1_r1mq1_c, %mr_mqb))
      and freeof(x, %mr_mk(_mr_gtm1_r1mq1_n, %mr_mqb))
      and freeof(x, %mr_mk(_mr_gtm1_r1mq1_q, %mr_mqb))
      and %mr_eqQ(
        %mr_mk(_mr_gtm1_r1mq1_r, %mr_mqb)
        - (2*%mr_mk(_mr_gtm1_r1mq1_n, %mr_mqb)
           - %mr_mk(_mr_gtm1_r1mq1_q, %mr_mqb)),
        0))))$

/* ---- RemoveContent family ------------------------------------------
 * Rubi :1152  RemoveContent[u,x] := with v = NonfreeFactors[u,x],
 *   w = Together[v]: if EqQ[FreeFactors[w,x],1] then Aux[v,x]
 *   else Aux[NonfreeFactors[w,x],x] */
%mr_nonfreeFactors(u, x) := block([r, i, t],
  if atom(u) then (
    if freeof(x, u) then 1 else u
  )
  /* a leading minus is dropped with the x-free content (the .m
     ProductQ factor map replaces x-free factors by 1, -1 included) */
  elseif op(u) = "-" then %mr_nonfreeFactors(part(u, 1), x)
  elseif op(u) = "*" then (
    r : 1,
    for i : 1 thru length(u) do (
      t : part(u, i),
      if is(freeof(x, t)) = false then r : r * t
    ),
    r
  )
  else if freeof(x, u) then 1 else u)$

%mr_freeFactors(u, x) := block([r, i, t],
  if atom(u) then (
    if freeof(x, u) then u else 1
  )
  elseif op(u) = "-" then -%mr_freeFactors(part(u, 1), x)
  elseif op(u) = "*" then (
    r : 1,
    for i : 1 thru length(u) do (
      t : part(u, i),
      if freeof(x, t) then r : r * t
    ),
    r
  )
  else if freeof(x, u) then u else 1)$

/* one monomial term as [base, exponent, rest] with base free of x:
   a literal power factor (a^k) is the base candidate first (the .m
   pattern a_^m_. binds the power), else the first free factor (base^1).
   false if no x-free factor exists (x^2, x, sin(x)*x, ...). */
%mr_termPower(t, x) := block([i, f, base, e, bi, rest, s],
  s : 1,
  if not atom(t) and op(t) = "-" then (s : -1, t : part(t, 1)),
  if atom(t) then (
    if freeof(x, t) then [t, 1, s] else false
  )
  elseif op(t) = "^" then (
    /* a bare power: base candidate iff x-free (the .m a_^m_ binding);
       a symbolic exponent is fine here — the %mr_rationalQ guard in
       %mr_rcAuxCommon rejects it, as Rubi's RationalQ[m] would */
    if freeof(x, t) then [part(t, 1), part(t, 2), s]
    else false
  )
  elseif op(t) = "*" then (
    base : false, e : 1, bi : 0,
    for i : 1 thru length(t) do (
      f : part(t, i),
      if bi = 0 and not atom(f) and op(f) = "^" and freeof(x, f) then (
        base : part(f, 1), e : part(f, 2), bi : i
      )
    ),
    if bi = 0 then
      for i : 1 thru length(t) do (
        f : part(t, i),
        if bi = 0 and freeof(x, f) then (
          base : f, e : 1, bi : i
        )
      ),
    if base = false then false
    else (
      rest : s,
      for i : 1 thru length(t) do (
        if i # bi then rest : rest * part(t, i)
      ),
      [base, e, rest]
    )
  )
  else false)$

/* the two/three-term RemoveContentAux pattern a^m u + a^n v (+ a^p w):
   one shared x-free base, rational exponents, all >= the minimum ->
   factor a^m out and recurse. DEVIATION (documented): the .m pattern
   also matches a NUMERIC base hiding in an integer (4 + 2*x: base 2
   with 4 = 2^2); Maxima integers are not Power-headed and this build
   has no integer factorizer, so 4 and 2x resolve to different bases
   and no factoring happens. The answer is unchanged up to an additive
   constant (it is consumed inside a Log by the rule), which the
   harness derivative check verifies. */
%mr_rcAuxCommon(s, x) := block([i, d, a, m, ok, r, nterms, ds],
  if atom(s) or op(s) # "+" then false
  else (
    nterms : length(s),
    if nterms = 2 or nterms = 3 then (
    ok : true,
    a : false,
    m : false,
    ds : [],
    for i : 1 thru nterms do (
      d : %mr_termPower(part(s, i), x),
      if is(d = false) then ok : false
      else (
        if a = false then (
          a : part(d, 1), m : part(d, 2)
        ) else (
          if is(part(d, 1) = a) = false then ok : false,
          if part(d, 2) < m then m : part(d, 2)
        ),
        /* [d]: append flattens one level — ds must stay a list of the
           [base, exponent, rest] triples */
        ds : append(ds, [d])
      )
    ),
    if ok and a # false and freeof(x, a)
      and %mr_rationalQ(part(part(ds, 1), 2))
      and (nterms < 3 or %mr_rationalQ(part(part(ds, 2), 2)))
      and (nterms # 3 or %mr_rationalQ(part(part(ds, 3), 2)))
    then (
      r : 0,
      for i : 1 thru nterms do
        r : r + part(part(ds, i), 3) * a^(part(part(ds, i), 2) - m),
      %mr_removeContentAux(r, x)
    )
    else false
    )
    else false
  ))$

/* RemoveContentAux (:1160-:1180): the shared-base patterns above, then
   the sign fallback (the .m NegQ[First[u]] — in Rubi semantics NegQ is
   false for any term with an undetermined sign, so the flip fires only
   on an EXPLICITLY negative leading term; %mr_sign = 'neg is the
   faithful port of that effective behavior). Maxima's term order
   differs from Mathematica's, so "leading" is Maxima's first arg. */
%mr_removeContentAux(u, x) := block([c, s],
  c : %mr_rcAuxCommon(u, x),
  if is(c # false) then c
  else (
    s : if not atom(u) and op(u) = "+" then %mr_sign(part(u, 1)) else 'pos,
    if s = 'neg then -u else u
  ))$

%mr_removeContent(u, x) := block([v, w],
  v : %mr_nonfreeFactors(u, x),
  w : %mr_together(v),
  if %mr_eqQ(%mr_freeFactors(w, x), 1) then %mr_removeContentAux(v, x)
  else %mr_removeContentAux(%mr_nonfreeFactors(w, x), x))$

/* ---- Subst ----------------------------------------------------------
 * Rubi :5140  Subst[u,x,v] — back-substitution. CLUSTER SCOPE: the
 * core "replace all occurrences of x by v, then simplify" (SubstAux's
 * final simp stage). SubstAux's special cases (trig-identity folding,
 * Defer'd dummy variables inside integrate/sum nouns, the binomial
 * flag) are Task 7 scope; a dummy x inside a noun WOULD be replaced by
 * this port — the 1.1.1.1 rule-5 antiderivative is a closed form, so
 * no dummy occurs in this cluster's usage. */
%mr_subst(u, x, v) := block([r],
  r : subst(v, x, u),
  %mr_simp(r))$

/* ===================== predicate cluster F (Task 7a) ====================
 * LinearPairQ / PerfectSquareQ / RationalFunctionQ / SplitProduct /
 * PseudoBinomialPairQ / InverseFunctionQ / AlgebraicFunctionQ. Each cites
 * its .m source line; PerfectSquareQ is a clone-gap predicate (referenced
 * by class-1 rules but defined nowhere in the pinned clone). */

/* Rubi :1387  LinearPairQ[u, v, x] — both linear in x, u not the bare
   variable, and the two slope/offset pairs proportional. The .m guard is
   NeQ[u, x] (u only, not v); the port keeps that asymmetry. */
%mr_linearPairQ(u, v, x) := block([],
  %mr_linearQ(u, x) and %mr_linearQ(v, x) and %mr_neQ(u, x)
    and %mr_eqQ(
      %mr_coeff(u, x, 0)*%mr_coeff(v, x, 1)
        - %mr_coeff(u, x, 1)*%mr_coeff(v, x, 0), 0))$

/* Clone-gap predicate (no .m definition in the pinned clone). The class-1
   uses are PerfectSquareQ[b^2 - 4*a*c] and PerfectSquareQ[a^2 + b^2] on
   x-free constants, so the port is a factor-exponent square test: after
   factor(), every factor's exponent is an even integer (a quotient needs
   both sides square; a negative content is not a real square). Exact
   rationals are a special case: this build's factor(9/4) stays the
   rational 9/4, so the numerator and denominator are tested as integer
   squares. */
%mr_integer_squareQ(n) := block([r],
  if n < 0 then false
  else if n = 0 or n = 1 then true
  else (
    r : sqrt(n),
    integerp(r) and is(r^2 = n)))$

%mr_perfectSquareQ_factor(e) := block([i, ok],
  if is(e = 0) then true
  else if is(e = 1) then true
  else if atom(e) then false
  else if op(e) = "-" then false
  else if op(e) = "*" then (
    ok : true,
    for i : 1 thru length(e) do
      if %mr_perfectSquareQ_factor(part(e, i)) = false then ok : false,
    ok
  )
  else if op(e) = "/"
    and numberp(part(e, 1)) and numberp(part(e, 2))
  then %mr_integer_squareQ(abs(part(e, 1)))
       and %mr_integer_squareQ(part(e, 2))
  else if op(e) = "/"
  then %mr_perfectSquareQ_factor(part(e, 1))
       and %mr_perfectSquareQ_factor(part(e, 2))
  else if op(e) = "^"
  then integerp(part(e, 2)) and evenp(part(e, 2))
  else false)$

%mr_perfectSquareQ(u) := block([f],
  u : ratsimp(u),
  if is(u = 0) then true
  else (
    f : factor(u),
    if atom(f) then is(f = 1)
    else %mr_perfectSquareQ_factor(f)))$

/* Rubi :1578  RationalFunctionQ[u, x]. Maxima stores x^(-1) as a "/" node
   and x^(1/2) as a 'sqrt node, so the IntegerPowerQ branch reads "/" as a
   rational step and rejects 'sqrt (and every other function head). */
%mr_rationalFunctionQ(u, x) := block([i, ok],
  if %mr_atomQ(u) or freeof(x, u) then true
  else if op(u) = "/"
  then %mr_rationalFunctionQ(part(u, 1), x)
       and %mr_rationalFunctionQ(part(u, 2), x)
  else if op(u) = "-"
  then %mr_rationalFunctionQ(part(u, 1), x)
  else if op(u) = "^"
  then %mr_rationalFunctionQ(part(u, 1), x) and integerp(part(u, 2))
  else if op(u) = "*" or op(u) = "+" then (
    ok : true,
    for i : 1 thru length(u) do
      if %mr_rationalFunctionQ(part(u, i), x) = false then ok : false,
    ok
  )
  else false)$

/* Rubi :7617  SumBaseQ[u] — a sum, or a sum raised to an odd power. The
   generated class-1 call passes the bare Rubi name as the SplitProduct
   function argument (Maxima has no first-class Rubi predicates here), so
   %mr_split_call special-cases that one name. */
%mr_sumBaseQ(u) := block([],
  if not atom(u) and op(u) = "+" then true
  else if not atom(u) and op(u) = "^"
    and integerp(part(u, 2)) and oddp(part(u, 2))
    and %mr_sumBaseQ(part(u, 1))
  then true
  else false)$

%mr_split_call(func, v) := block([],
  if atom(func) and is(func = 'SumBaseQ)
  then %mr_sumBaseQ(v)
  else apply(func, [v]))$

%mr_parts_list(e) := block([i, L],
  L : [],
  for i : 1 thru length(e) do
    L : append(L, [part(e, i)]),
  L)$

%mr_product_factors(u) := block([L],
  if atom(u) then [u]
  else if op(u) = "-" then append([-1], %mr_product_factors(part(u, 1)))
  else if op(u) = "*" then %mr_parts_list(u)
  else [u])$

%mr_product_from_list(L) := block([p, i],
  p : 1,
  for i : 1 thru length(L) do
    p : p*part(L, i),
  p)$

/* Rubi :7658  SplitProduct[func, u] — the first factor v of u with
   func[v] true, as [v, u/v]; false when no factor qualifies. A unary minus
   is read as the leading -1 factor (Mathematica's Times[-1, ...]). */
%mr_splitProduct_aux(func, u) := block([F, p, r],
  F : %mr_product_factors(u),
  if length(F) > 1 then (
    if %mr_split_call(func, part(F, 1)) = true
    then [part(F, 1), %mr_product_from_list(rest(F))]
    else (
      p : %mr_product_from_list(rest(F)),
      r : %mr_splitProduct_aux(func, p),
      if r # false
      then [part(r, 1), part(F, 1)*part(r, 2)]
      else false
    )
  )
  else if %mr_split_call(func, u) = true then [u, 1]
  else false)$

%mr_splitProduct(func, u) := %mr_splitProduct_aux(func, u)$

/* Local real-root form for PseudoBinomialParts. This is deliberately NOT
   the public %mr_rt port (a later cluster): it only supplies the [c, d, n]
   tail that PseudoBinomialPairQ compares. */
%mr_pseudoRoot(u, n) := block([],
  if n = 2 then sqrt(u)
  else if oddp(n) and is(u < 0) then -(-u)^(1/n)
  else u^(1/n))$

/* Rubi :1524  PseudoBinomialParts[u, x] — [a, 1, c, d, n] iff u is a
   polynomial of degree n > 2 of the form a + b*(c + d*x)^n with a, b
   nonzero. Expon is read by %mr_degree under the %mr_polynomialQ gate
   (the only form this helper accepts). */
%mr_pseudoBinomial_parts(u, x) := block([n, d, c, a],
  if %mr_polynomialQ(u, x) then (
    n : %mr_degree(u, x),
    if is(n > 2) then (
      d : %mr_pseudoRoot(%mr_coeff(u, x, n), n),
      c : %mr_coeff(u, x, n - 1)/(n*d^(n - 1)),
      a : %mr_simp(u - expand((c + d*x)^n)),
      if %mr_neQ(a, 0) and freeof(x, a)
      then [a, 1, c, d, n]
      else false
    )
    else false
  )
  else false)$

/* Rubi :1507  PseudoBinomialPairQ[u, v, x] — both have PseudoBinomialParts
   and the same [c, d, n] tail. The .m uses IdentityQ on the tail; the port
   keeps the exact-structure reading (loose %mr_eqQ would over-accept
   distinct symbolic constants). */
%mr_pseudoBinomialPairQ(u, v, x) := block([l1, l2],
  l1 : %mr_pseudoBinomial_parts(u, x),
  if l1 = false then false
  else (
    l2 : %mr_pseudoBinomial_parts(v, x),
    if l2 = false then false
    else %mr_b(part(l1, 3) = part(l2, 3))
         and %mr_b(part(l1, 4) = part(l2, 4))
         and %mr_b(part(l1, 5) = part(l2, 5))
  ))$

/* Rubi :303  InverseFunctionQ[u]. The Maxima head set follows the .m
   $InverseTrigFunctions / $InverseHyperbolicFunctions lists; the two-arg
   inverse tangent is atan2 (absent from the one-arg list, so the .m
   Length[u] <= 1 gate is met structurally). */
%mr_inverseFunctionQ(u) := block([v],
  v : if %mr_atomQ(u) then u else op(u),
  if is(v = 'log) then true
  else if not %mr_atomQ(u) and length(u) <= 1
    and member(v, ['asin, 'acos, 'atan, 'acot, 'asec, 'acsc]) # false
  then true
  else if not %mr_atomQ(u)
    and member(v, ['asinh, 'acosh, 'atanh, 'acoth, 'asech, 'acsch]) # false
  then true
  else if not %mr_atomQ(u)
    and (is(v = 'mod) or is(v = 'polylog) or is(v = 'productlog))
  then true
  else false)$

/* Rubi :1681  AlgebraicFunctionQ[u, x, flag_ = False]. The generated
   class-1 call is the 2-arg form, so flag is false: a power counts only
   with an explicit rational exponent (the 'sqrt node is the collapsed
   1/2 power). */
%mr_algebraicFunctionQ(u, x) := block([i, ok],
  if %mr_atomQ(u) or freeof(x, u) then true
  else if op(u) = "/"
  then %mr_algebraicFunctionQ(part(u, 1), x)
       and %mr_algebraicFunctionQ(part(u, 2), x)
  else if op(u) = "-"
  then %mr_algebraicFunctionQ(part(u, 1), x)
  else if op(u) = "^"
  then %mr_algebraicFunctionQ(part(u, 1), x)
       and %mr_rationalQ(part(u, 2))
  else if op(u) = 'sqrt
  then %mr_algebraicFunctionQ(part(u, 1), x)
  else if op(u) = "*" or op(u) = "+" then (
    ok : true,
    for i : 1 thru length(u) do
      if %mr_algebraicFunctionQ(part(u, i), x) = false then ok : false,
    ok
  )
  else if op(u) = "[" then (
    ok : true,
    for i : 1 thru length(u) do
      if %mr_algebraicFunctionQ(part(u, i), x) = false then ok : false,
    ok
  )
  else false)$

/* ---- cluster G: the first B-tier shims -------------------------------
 * Rt (:7513/:7549) · Expon (:1239) · ExpandToSum (:3329/:3343) ·
 * IntPart (:2952) · FracPart (:2969).
 *
 * 5.50.0 representation traps pinned here (measured 2026-08-23):
 *  - lambda([a, b, [c]], ...) is this build's variable-arity form;
 *    generated rules call ExpandToSum with 2 and 3 args under one name;
 *  - the .m's TogetherSimplify is NOT ported into %mr_rt: Maxima's
 *    ratsimp/factor expand or factor the argument ((a+b)^2 ->
 *    a^2+2*a*b+b^2, x^2-1 -> (x-1)*(x+1)), destroying the PowerQ /
 *    non-product reading. The port feeds the argument to RtAux as stored;
 *  - Maxima's sign() answers pz for a^2, so the .m's LtQ[#,0] split is
 *    the strict is(# < 0) reading (Rubi's PosQ/NegQ remain sign-based). */

%mr_sum_from_list(L) := block([s, i],
  s : 0,
  for i : 1 thru length(L) do
    s : s + part(L, i),
  s)$

%mr_rt_power_parts(u) := block([],
  if not atom(u) and op(u) = "^"
  then [part(u, 1), part(u, 2)]
  else if not atom(u) and op(u) = 'sqrt
  /* the collapsed 1/2 power node (see the %mr_binomial_parts note) */
  then [part(u, 1), 1/2]
  else false)$

%mr_rt_powerQ(u) := not atom(%mr_rt_power_parts(u))$

%mr_rt_power_base(u) := part(%mr_rt_power_parts(u), 1)$

%mr_rt_power_exp(u) := part(%mr_rt_power_parts(u), 2)$

/* the .m's LtQ[#,0] — strict, unknown -> false (NOT Rubi's NegQ) */
%mr_rt_ltQ(u) := is(u < 0) = true$

/* Rubi :7612  AtomBaseQ[u] */
%mr_rt_atomBaseQ(u) := block([],
  if %mr_atomQ(u) then true
  else if %mr_rt_powerQ(u) then block([e],
    e : %mr_rt_power_exp(u),
    integerp(e) and oddp(e)
    and %mr_rt_atomBaseQ(%mr_rt_power_base(u)))
  else false)$

/* Rubi :7622  NegSumBaseQ[u] */
%mr_rt_negSumBaseQ(u) := block([],
  if not atom(u) and op(u) = "+"
  then %mr_negQ(part(u, 1))
  else if %mr_rt_powerQ(u) then block([e],
    e : %mr_rt_power_exp(u),
    integerp(e) and oddp(e)
    and %mr_rt_negSumBaseQ(%mr_rt_power_base(u)))
  else false)$

/* Rubi :7627  AllNegTermQ[u] */
%mr_rt_allNegTermQ(u) := block([],
  if %mr_rt_powerQ(u) then block([e],
    e : %mr_rt_power_exp(u),
    if integerp(e) and oddp(e)
    then %mr_rt_allNegTermQ(%mr_rt_power_base(u))
    else false)
  else if not atom(u) and op(u) = "+"
  then %mr_negQ(part(u, 1)) and %mr_rt_allNegTermQ(rest(u))
  else %mr_negQ(u))$

/* Rubi :7636  SomeNegTermQ[u] */
%mr_rt_someNegTermQ(u) := block([],
  if %mr_rt_powerQ(u) then block([e],
    e : %mr_rt_power_exp(u),
    if integerp(e) and oddp(e)
    then %mr_rt_someNegTermQ(%mr_rt_power_base(u))
    else false)
  else if not atom(u) and op(u) = "+"
  then %mr_negQ(part(u, 1)) or %mr_rt_someNegTermQ(rest(u))
  else %mr_negQ(u))$

/* Rubi :7676  SplitSum[func, u] — the first TERM v of u for which
   func[v] is nonatomic, as [func[v], u-v]; false otherwise. rest() of a
   Maxima sum is the remaining sum (or the last atom), matching the .m
   Rest[u] reading. */
%mr_splitSum_aux(func, u) := block([r],
  if not atom(u) and op(u) = "+" then (
    r : apply(func, [part(u, 1)]),
    if not atom(r)
    then [r, rest(u)]
    else (
      r : %mr_splitSum_aux(func, rest(u)),
      if r # false
      then [part(r, 1), part(u, 1) + part(r, 2)]
      else false
    )
  )
  else (
    r : apply(func, [u]),
    if not atom(r) then [r, 0] else false
  ))$

%mr_splitSum(func, u) := %mr_splitSum_aux(func, u)$

/* Rubi :7649  TrigSquareQ[u] — Sin[z]^2 or Cos[z]^2 */
%mr_trigSquareQ(u) := block([b],
  if %mr_rt_powerQ(u) and is(%mr_rt_power_exp(u) = 2)
  then (
    b : %mr_rt_power_base(u),
    not atom(b) and (is(op(b) = 'sin) or is(op(b) = 'cos))
  )
  else false)$

/* Rubi :7530  TrigSquare[u] — a-a*Sin[z]^2 -> a*Cos[z]^2 (and the Cos
   twin); false when the non-trig parts do not cancel. */
%mr_trigSquare(u) := block([lst, tr, z],
  if not atom(u) and op(u) = "+" then (
    lst : %mr_splitSum(
      lambda([z],
        %mr_splitProduct(lambda([w], %mr_trigSquareQ(w)), z)),
      u),
    if lst # false
      and %mr_eqQ(part(part(lst, 1), 2) + part(lst, 2), 0)
    then (
      tr : part(part(part(lst, 1), 1), 1),
      if is(op(tr) = 'sin)
      then part(lst, 2)*cos(part(tr, 1))^2
      else part(lst, 2)*sin(part(tr, 1))^2
    )
    else false
  )
  else false)$

/* Rubi :7549  RtAux[u, n] — the structural nth-root split. See the
   cluster note on the omitted TogetherSimplify. */
%mr_rt_aux(u, n) := block([F, lst, v, Fv, p, i],
  F : %mr_product_factors(u),
  if %mr_rt_powerQ(u)
  then %mr_rt_power_base(u)^(%mr_rt_power_exp(u)/n)
  else if length(F) > 1 then (
    lst : %mr_splitProduct(lambda([z], %mr_posQ(z)), u),
    if lst # false
    then %mr_rt_aux(part(lst, 1), n)*%mr_rt_aux(part(lst, 2), n)
    else (
      lst : %mr_splitProduct(lambda([z], %mr_rt_ltQ(z)), u),
      if lst # false then (
        if %mr_eqQ(part(lst, 1), -1) then (
          v : part(lst, 2),
          if %mr_rt_powerQ(v) and is(%mr_rt_power_exp(v) < 0)
          then 1/%mr_rt_aux(
            -%mr_rt_power_base(v)^(-%mr_rt_power_exp(v)), n)
          else (
            Fv : %mr_product_factors(v),
            if length(Fv) > 1 then (
              if %mr_splitProduct(
                    lambda([z], %mr_sumBaseQ(z)), v) # false
              then (
                lst : %mr_splitProduct(
                  lambda([z], %mr_rt_allNegTermQ(z)), v),
                if lst # false
                then %mr_rt_aux(-part(lst, 1), n)
                     *%mr_rt_aux(part(lst, 2), n)
                else (
                  lst : %mr_splitProduct(
                    lambda([z], %mr_rt_negSumBaseQ(z)), v),
                  if lst # false
                  then %mr_rt_aux(-part(lst, 1), n)
                       *%mr_rt_aux(part(lst, 2), n)
                  else (
                    lst : %mr_splitProduct(
                      lambda([z], %mr_rt_someNegTermQ(z)), v),
                    if lst # false
                    then %mr_rt_aux(-part(lst, 1), n)
                         *%mr_rt_aux(part(lst, 2), n)
                    else (
                      lst : %mr_splitProduct(
                        lambda([z], %mr_sumBaseQ(z)), v),
                      %mr_rt_aux(-part(lst, 1), n)
                           *%mr_rt_aux(part(lst, 2), n)
                    )
                  )
                )
              )
              else (
                lst : %mr_splitProduct(
                  lambda([z], %mr_rt_atomBaseQ(z)), v),
                if lst # false
                then %mr_rt_aux(-part(lst, 1), n)
                     *%mr_rt_aux(part(lst, 2), n)
                else %mr_rt_aux(-part(Fv, 1), n)
                     *%mr_rt_aux(%mr_product_from_list(rest(Fv)), n)
              )
            )
            else if oddp(n) then -%mr_rt_aux(v, n)
            else u^(1/n)
          )
        )
        else %mr_rt_aux(-part(lst, 1), n)
             *%mr_rt_aux(-part(lst, 2), n)
      )
      else (
        lst : %mr_splitProduct(
          lambda([z], %mr_rt_allNegTermQ(z)), u),
        if lst # false
          and %mr_splitProduct(
                lambda([z], %mr_sumBaseQ(z)), part(lst, 2)) # false
        then %mr_rt_aux(-part(lst, 1), n)
             *%mr_rt_aux(-part(lst, 2), n)
        else (
          lst : %mr_splitProduct(
            lambda([z], %mr_rt_negSumBaseQ(z)), u),
          if lst # false
            and %mr_splitProduct(
                  lambda([z], %mr_rt_negSumBaseQ(z)),
                  part(lst, 2)) # false
          then %mr_rt_aux(-part(lst, 1), n)
               *%mr_rt_aux(-part(lst, 2), n)
          else (
            p : 1,
            for i : 1 thru length(F) do
              p : p*%mr_rt_aux(part(F, i), n),
            p
          )
        )
      )
    )
  )
  else (
    lst : %mr_trigSquare(u),
    if lst # false then %mr_rt_aux(lst, n)
    else if oddp(n) and %mr_rt_ltQ(u) then -%mr_rt_aux(-u, n)
    else if oddp(n) and %mr_negQ(u) and %mr_posQ(-u)
    then -%mr_rt_aux(-u, n)
    else u^(1/n)
  ))$

/* Rubi :7513  Rt[u, n] — the simplest nth root of u. */
%mr_rt(u, n) := %mr_rt_aux(u, n)$

/* Rubi :1239  Expon[expr, form] := Exponent[Together[expr], form].
   The 3-arg form is declared (the generator's Expon mapping allows it)
   but fails closed: no class-1 call site uses the exponent-list form. */
%mr_expon2(u, x) := %mr_degree(%mr_together(u), x)$

/* The generator's powered-form Expon: Expon[u, base^power] is the degree
   in base^power (the degree in base divided by power, when that is an
   integer; -1 otherwise, the Exponent parity). */
%mr_expon3(u, base, power) := block([d],
  d : %mr_degree(%mr_together(u), base),
  if integerp(power) and is(power > 0) and d >= 0
    and is(mod(d, power) = 0)
  then d/power
  else -1)$

%mr_expon : lambda([u, form, [h]],
  if length(h) = 0 then %mr_expon2(u, form)
  else %mr_expon3(u, form, part(h, 1)))$

/* Rubi :1242  Expon[expr, form, Min] — the MINIMUM x-exponent of
   Together[expr] (Mathematica Exponent[_, _, Min]). The generator maps
   it to %mr_exponMin (1.3.3 r17 / 1.4.1 r25 — the Px minimum-power
   split). %mr_degree's term walker cannot be reused: %mr_term_xexp
   returns FALSE on x-bearing denominators (1/x -> false, the
   polynomial-monomial reading), which DROPS exactly the terms the
   minimum is about — a local walker below handles negative powers
   (1/x -> -1, x^2/x^5 -> -3) and x-free bases (d^2 -> 0), mirroring
   %mr_term_xexp's atom/minus/times/quote cases. Edge parity with
   %mr_degree: 0 -> 0, x-free -> 0, no x-power term at all -> -1. */
%mr_exponMin_term(t, x) := block([i, f, p, ok],
  if atom(t) then (
    if is(t = x) then 1 else 0
  )
  elseif op(t) = "-" then %mr_exponMin_term(part(t, 1), x)
  elseif op(t) = "*" then (
    p : 0,
    ok : true,
    for i : 1 thru length(t) do (
      f : part(t, i),
      if freeof(x, f) then true
      elseif is(f = x) then p : p + 1
      elseif op(f) = "^" and is(part(f, 1) = x)
        and integerp(part(f, 2)) then p : p + part(f, 2)
      else ok : false
    ),
    if ok then p else false
  )
  elseif op(t) = "/" then block([n, d],
    n : %mr_exponMin_term(part(t, 1), x),
    d : %mr_exponMin_term(part(t, 2), x),
    if n # false and d # false then n - d else false
  )
  elseif op(t) = "^" then (
    if is(part(t, 1) = x) and integerp(part(t, 2))
    then part(t, 2)
    else if freeof(x, part(t, 1)) then 0 else false
  )
  else false)$

%mr_exponMin(u, x) := block([e, i, m, p, found],
  e : expand(%mr_together(u)),
  if is(e = 0) then 0
  elseif freeof(x, e) then 0
  elseif atom(e) then %mr_exponMin_term(e, x)
  elseif op(e) = "+" then (
    m : -1, found : false,
    for i : 1 thru length(e) do (
      p : %mr_exponMin_term(part(e, i), x),
      if p # false then (
        if not found then (m : p, found : true)
        elseif p < m then m : p
      )
    ),
    if found then m else -1
  )
  else (
    p : %mr_exponMin_term(e, x),
    if p # false then p else -1))$

/* Rubi :3343  ExpandToSum[u, x] — u expanded into a sum of monomials of
   x. The .m's Simp is NOT %mr_simp here: %mr_simp factors (the cluster's
   whole point is the MONOMIAL-SUM shape, so (x+1)^2 must stay
   x^2+2*x+1). Rubi :3329  ExpandToSum[u, v, x] is the distribute form. */
%mr_expandToSum2(u, x) := block([L],
  if %mr_polyQ(u, x) then expand(u)
  else if %mr_binomialQ(u, x) then (
    L : %mr_binomial_parts(u, x),
    if atom(L) then expand(u)
    else part(L, 1) + part(L, 2)*x^part(L, 3)
  )
  else if %mr_trinomialQ(u, x) then (
    L : %mr_trinomial_parts(u, x),
    if atom(L) then expand(u)
    else part(L, 1) + part(L, 2)*x^part(L, 4)
         + part(L, 3)*x^(2*part(L, 4))
  )
  else if %mr_generalizedBinomialQ(u, x) then (
    L : %mr_generalizedBinomial_parts(u, x),
    if atom(L) then expand(u)
    else part(L, 1)*x^part(L, 4) + part(L, 2)*x^part(L, 3)
  )
  else if %mr_generalizedTrinomialQ(u, x) then (
    L : %mr_generalizedTrinomial_parts(u, x),
    if atom(L) then expand(u)
    else part(L, 1)*x^part(L, 5) + part(L, 2)*x^part(L, 4)
         + part(L, 3)*x^expand(2*part(L, 4) - part(L, 5))
  )
  else expand(u))$

%mr_expandToSum : lambda([a, b, [c]],
  if length(c) = 0
  then %mr_expandToSum2(a, b)
  else expand(a*%mr_expandToSum2(b, part(c, 1))))$

/* Rubi :2952  IntPart[u, n_ = 1] — the sum of the integer terms of u.
   The m_*u_ arm absorbs an EXPLICIT rational leading factor into n;
   IntegerPart is the floor reading (IntPart + FracPart = u). */
%mr_intPart_aux(u, n) := block([F, m],
  if not atom(u) and (op(u) = "*" or op(u) = "-")
  then (
    F : %mr_product_factors(u),
    m : part(F, 1),
    if length(F) > 1 and %mr_rationalQ(m)
    then %mr_intPart_aux(%mr_product_from_list(rest(F)), m*n)
    else 0
  )
  else if %mr_rationalQ(u) then floor(n*u)
  else if not atom(u) and op(u) = "+"
  then apply("+", map(
    lambda([z], %mr_intPart_aux(z, n)), %mr_parts_list(u)))
  else 0)$

%mr_intPart : lambda([u, [n]],
  %mr_intPart_aux(u, if length(n) = 0 then 1 else part(n, 1)))$

/* Rubi :2969  FracPart[u, n_ = 1] — the sum of the non-integer terms of
   u (FractionalPart is the floor complement, so FracPart[-3/2] = 1/2). */
%mr_fracPart_aux(u, n) := block([F, m, r],
  if not atom(u) and (op(u) = "*" or op(u) = "-")
  then (
    F : %mr_product_factors(u),
    m : part(F, 1),
    if length(F) > 1 and %mr_rationalQ(m)
    then %mr_fracPart_aux(%mr_product_from_list(rest(F)), m*n)
    else n*u
  )
  else if %mr_rationalQ(u) then (
    r : n*u,
    r - floor(r)
  )
  else if not atom(u) and op(u) = "+"
  then apply("+", map(
    lambda([z], %mr_fracPart_aux(z, n)), %mr_parts_list(u)))
  else n*u)$

%mr_fracPart : lambda([u, [n]],
  %mr_fracPart_aux(u, if length(n) = 0 then 1 else part(n, 1)))$

/* ---- cluster H: the small remaining B-tier shims --------------------
 * PolyQuotient / PolyRemainder / PolyDivide are the Maxima quotient /
 * remainder / divide builtins under the project names (the fail-closed
 * guard is the %mr_polynomialQ pair — a non-polynomial argument is a
 * rule-condition bug, not a division). ArcTanh / ArcSinh / ArcCosh are
 * unbound in this build and get the log forms. */

/* expand() on the result is LOAD-BEARING (measured 2026-08-25,
   5.50.0/SBCL): for a NON-MONIC divisor with symbolic coefficients
   (v = a+b*x+c*x^2), the built-in quotient/remainder/divide return
   the parts as FRACTIONS — the symbolic content (c^3 here) rides in
   the denominator: op(remainder((e*x+d)^4, a+b*x+c*x^2, x)) = "/"
   with denom = c^3. Rubi's PolynomialQuotient/Remainder contract is a
   true polynomial (Mathematica does exact symbolic division); the
   fraction form corrupts every downstream Coeff/Expon/EqQ consumer
   (1.2.1.6 r4's f,g came back c^3-scaled -> 1.2.1.2 e2202 wrong
   answer, resid 3.19). expand() over an x-free denominator restores
   the true monomial sum (qu*expand(Q) + expand(R) = Pq re-verified). */
%mr_polyQuotient(u, v, x) := block([r],
  if %mr_polynomialQ(u, x) and %mr_polynomialQ(v, x)
  then (
    r : errcatch(quotient(u, v, x)),
    if is(r = []) then false else expand(part(r, 1))
  )
  else false)$

%mr_polyRemainder(u, v, x) := block([r],
  if %mr_polynomialQ(u, x) and %mr_polynomialQ(v, x)
  then (
    r : errcatch(remainder(u, v, x)),
    if is(r = []) then false else expand(part(r, 1))
  )
  else false)$

/* Rubi :3298  PolynomialDivide[u, v, x] — the POLYNOMIAL quotient PLUS
   the remainder over v, as ONE expression: quo + rem/v. The generated
   repls call mr_int(%mr_polyDivide(...), x) and integrate that single
   expression (Rubi: Int[PolynomialDivide[u, v, x], x]); the earlier
   [Q, R] list contract integrated the LIST (a noun/garbage answer —
   measured 2026-08-25: 1.1.3.3 e5, resid ~0.33/1.6). `divide(u, v, x)`
   is NOT used: in this build it divides by a RECIPROCAL by multiplying
   and dropping the remainder (divide(a+b*x^3, 1/(c+d*x^3), x) =
   [[b*d*x^6 + ..., 0]] — the "remainder" is a sum, not a < v-degree
   remainder, measured 2026-08-25). */
%mr_polyDivide(u, v, x) := block([q, r],
  if %mr_polynomialQ(u, x) and %mr_polynomialQ(v, x)
  then (
    q : errcatch(quotient(u, v, x)),
    if is(q = []) then false
    else (
      r : errcatch(remainder(u, v, x)),
      if is(r = []) then false
      /* no expand(): this build's expand DISTRIBUTES a sum over a
         denominator term (expand(Q + R/v) = Q + R1/v - R2/... per
         summand, measured 2026-08-25: x + (1-x)/(x^2+1) became
         -(x/(x^2+1)) + 1/(x^2+1) + x) — the unexpanded form is the
         canonical one the cascade and the zero-tests expect. */
      else part(q, 1) + part(r, 1)/v
    )
  )
  else false)$

%mr_atanh(u) := 1/2*log((1 + u)/(1 - u))$

%mr_asinh(u) := log(u + sqrt(u^2 + 1))$

/* sqrt(z-1)*sqrt(z+1) (not sqrt(z^2-1)): the branch reading the .m's
   ArcCosh wants on the real axis, and the acosh(1) probe pins the
   sqrt(0) corner. */
%mr_acosh(u) := log(u + sqrt(u - 1)*sqrt(u + 1))$

/* ---- cluster I: the algebraic surface of ExpandIntegrand ------------
 * The .m's ExpandIntegrand is a large pattern set (log/trig/smart-apart
 * branches included); this port is the ALGEBRAIC surface the class-1
 * call sites need:
 *   - a positive-integer power of a sum expands,
 *   - polynomial * (a+b*x)^m is kept in linear-power form by
 *     ExpandLinearProduct (the .m's u_*(a+b*x)^m rule),
 *   - the 3-arg form distributes over the expanded v without expanding
 *     the power factors (the DistributeOverTerms / MergeMonomials
 *     surface). The remaining .m branches (CollectRecipTerms,
 *     ExpandExpression / SmartApart, the F^u and Log rules) are NOT
 *     ported here; the fallback is plain expand(). */

%mr_coefficientList(u, x) := block([d, i, L],
  if %mr_polynomialQ(u, x) then (
    d : %mr_degree(u, x),
    L : [],
    for i : 0 thru d do
      L : append(L, [%mr_coeff(u, x, i)]),
    L
  )
  else false)$

/* Rubi :4023  ExpandLinearProduct[v, u, a, b, x] — v*u as a sum of
   c*v*(a+b*x)^n terms (u read in the shifted variable (x-a)/b). */
/* The shifted form w may carry a constant (x-free) denominator (e.g. the
   b^-k from (x-a)/b). rat() isolates it so the coefficient list of the
   numerator is scaled back by 1/den — the .m CoefficientList[Expand[w], x]
   reading, which keeps the factor (the MEASURED 2026-08-25 243x error came
   from num(rat(u)) dropping it). NOTE: together() is a NOUN in this build
   (MEASURED 2026-08-25: op(together(x/2+x/3)) = 'together with no preload),
   so rat() is the combining step. */
%mr_expandLinearProduct(v, u, a, b, x) := block([w, r, den, L, s, i],
  if freeof(x, a) and freeof(x, b) and %mr_neQ(b, 0)
    and %mr_polynomialQ(u, x)
  then (
    w : subst((x - a)/b, x, u),
    r : rat(w),
    /* expand() reifies the gcrat constant to a plain number — MEASURED
       2026-08-25: dividing by the raw denom(rat(...)) object (is(d = 243)
       true, yet distinct) makes Maxima run the rat simplifier and expand
       (3*x+2)^k into its polynomial, destroying the term structure. */
    den : expand(denom(r)),
    L : %mr_coefficientList(expand(num(r)), x),
    if atom(L) then false
    else (
      s : 0,
      for i : 1 thru length(L) do
        s : s + v*part(L, i)*(a + b*x)^(i - 1)/den,
      s
    )
  )
  else false)$

/* A positive-integer power of a sum (the .m's u_^p_. rule with
   SumQ[u] && IGtQ[p,0]). */
%mr_ei_pos_power_sum(u, x) :=
  not atom(u) and op(u) = "^"
  and %mr_sumQ(part(u, 1))
  and integerp(part(u, 2))
  and is(part(u, 2) > 0)$

/* Local factor scan for the ExpandIntegrand linear-power search. Same as
   %mr_product_factors but a "/" node is split into its numerator factors
   plus each denominator factor as a reciprocal (MEASURED 2026-08-25:
   (3*x+2)^-8 stores as the "/" node 1/(3*x+2)^8, so the (a+b*x)^-m factor
   of a quotient is invisible to the opaque-op read). Kept separate from
   %mr_product_factors so no other predicate's behaviour changes. */
%mr_ei_factors(u) := block([],
  if atom(u) then [u]
  else if op(u) = "-" then append([-1], %mr_ei_factors(part(u, 1)))
  else if op(u) = "*" then %mr_parts_list(u)
  else if op(u) = "/" then append(
      %mr_ei_factors(part(u, 1)),
      map(lambda([z],
        if not atom(z) and op(z) = "^" then part(z, 1)^-part(z, 2)
        else z^-1),
        %mr_ei_factors(part(u, 2))))
  else [u])$

/* The linear-power factor of a product at position i, as
   [power, a, b, rest] or false. mode "power" reads an actual
   (a+b*x)^m node, or the reciprocal quotient 1/(a+b*x)^m (m a positive
   integer — the MEASURED 2026-08-25 storage of (a+b*x)^-m as a "/" node,
   which the opaque-op read of %mr_product_factors cannot see); mode
   "bare" reads a bare linear factor (the .m's m_. default — a Power is
   preferred over the bare reading). MEASURED 2026-08-23: return() does
   not escape a for-loop body in this build, so the scan is a flag loop
   over this helper. */
%mr_ei_linear_power_at(F, i, x, mode) :=
  block([f, base, ok, L, R, j, rest],
    f : part(F, i),
    ok : false,
    if mode = "power" then (
      if not atom(f) and op(f) = "^"
      then (base : part(f, 1), ok : true)
      else if not atom(f) and op(f) = "/" and is(num(f) = 1)
        and op(denom(f)) = "^"
        and integerp(part(denom(f), 2))
        and is(part(denom(f), 2) > 0)
      then (base : part(denom(f), 1), ok : true)
    )
    else if not atom(f) and op(f) # "^"
    then (base : f, ok : true),
    if ok = false then false
    else (
      if %mr_linearQ(base, x) then (
        L : %mr_binomial_parts(base, x),
        if not atom(L) and is(part(L, 3) = 1)
          and %mr_neQ(part(L, 2), 0)
        then (
          R : [],
          for j : 1 thru length(F) do
            if j # i then R : append(R, [part(F, j)]),
          rest : %mr_product_from_list(R),
          if %mr_polynomialQ(rest, x)
          then [f, part(L, 1), part(L, 2), rest]
          else false
        )
        else false
      )
      else false
    ))$

/* The linear-power factor of a product, as [power, a, b, rest]: an
   actual (a+b*x)^m node is preferred over a bare linear factor (the
   .m's (a+b*x)^m_. pattern matches a Power first in the class-1 term
   order). */
%mr_ei_linear_power(u, x) := block([F, i, r],
  F : %mr_ei_factors(u),
  r : false,
  for i : 1 thru length(F) do
    if r = false then
      r : %mr_ei_linear_power_at(F, i, x, "power"),
  if r # false then r
  else (
    for i : 1 thru length(F) do
      if r = false then
        r : %mr_ei_linear_power_at(F, i, x, "bare"),
    r
  ))$

/* Distribute u over a sum v term-by-term WITHOUT expanding the terms
   (the MergeMonomials surface: a plain product is left as a product). */
%mr_ei_distribute(u, v, x) := block([],
  if not atom(v) and op(v) = "+"
  then apply("+", map(lambda([z], u*z), %mr_parts_list(v)))
  else u*v)$

%mr_expandIntegrand2(u, x) := block([L],
  if %mr_ei_pos_power_sum(u, x)
  then expand(u)
  else (
    L : %mr_ei_linear_power(u, x),
    if L # false
    then %mr_expandLinearProduct(
      part(L, 1), part(L, 4), part(L, 2), part(L, 3), x)
    else expand(u)
  ))$

%mr_expandIntegrand3(u, v, x) :=
  %mr_ei_distribute(u, %mr_expandIntegrand2(v, x), x)$

%mr_expandIntegrand : lambda([a, b, [c]],
  if length(c) = 0
  then %mr_expandIntegrand2(a, b)
  else %mr_expandIntegrand3(a, b, part(c, 1)))$

/* ---- cluster J: the remaining high-usage B-tier shims ---------------
 * SubstFor / SubstPower are the ALGEBRAIC surface: x^n and linear-v
 * substitutions (the .m's trig / inverse-function branches are not
 * ported). Dist is the DistributeOverTerms surface. Cancel is factor()
 * (the unbound builtin, measured). PolyGCD is the monic x-dependent
 * gcd. RationalFunctionExpand is the algebraic-ExpandIntegrand surface
 * (the .m's RationalFunctionFactors / NonrationalFunctionFactors split
 * is deferred). */

/* Rubi :6142  SubstPower[F[x], x, n] — x^m -> x^(m*n), x -> x^n. */
%mr_substPower(u, x, n) := block([],
  if atom(u) then (
    if is(u = x) then x^n else u
  )
  else if numberp(u) then u
  else if not atom(u) and op(u) = "^" then (
    if is(part(u, 1) = x) and freeof(x, part(u, 2))
    then x^(n*part(u, 2))
    else %mr_substPower(part(u, 1), x, n)
         ^%mr_substPower(part(u, 2), x, n)
  )
  else if not atom(u) and op(u) = "*"
  then apply("*", map(lambda([z], %mr_substPower(z, x, n)),
    %mr_parts_list(u)))
  else if not atom(u) and op(u) = "+"
  then apply("+", map(lambda([z], %mr_substPower(z, x, n)),
    %mr_parts_list(u)))
  else if not atom(u) and op(u) = "/"
  then %mr_substPower(part(u, 1), x, n)
       /%mr_substPower(part(u, 2), x, n)
  else if not atom(u) and op(u) = "-"
  then -%mr_substPower(part(u, 1), x, n)
  else if not atom(u) and op(u) = "["
  then map(lambda([z], %mr_substPower(z, x, n)), %mr_parts_list(u))
  else (
    /* generic head — MEASURED 2026-08-25 on 5.50.0/SBCL: sqrt(e) is
       its OWN head (op = "sqrt", NOT "^"), so the explicit cases
       above miss it and the old `else u` left sqrt(3*x^2+5*x+2)
       untouched under SubstPower[., x, 2] (derailing 1.4.1 r34 on
       1.2.1.3 e1058: the inner integrand kept a quadratic radical
       where the substitution needs the quartic). Rubi's SubstPower
       is Map over every subpart (IntegrationUtilityFunctions.m:6142),
       so walk args generically. */
    apply(op(u), map(lambda([z], %mr_substPower(z, x, n)), args(u)))))$

/* Rubi :5980  SubstFor[v, u, x] — u (a function of v) with v replaced
   by x. The algebraic surface: an atom is a plain subst, x^n is the
   SubstPower reading with factor 1/n, a linear v is the shifted
   variable (x-a)/b, and the fallback is the plain subst. */
%mr_substFor3(v, u, x) := block([L],
  if %mr_atomQ(v)
  then subst(x, v, u)
  else if not atom(v) and op(v) = "^"
    and is(part(v, 1) = x) and freeof(x, part(v, 2))
  then %mr_substPower(u, x, 1/part(v, 2))
  else if %mr_linearQ(v, x) then (
    L : %mr_binomial_parts(v, x),
    if not atom(L) and is(part(L, 3) = 1)
    then subst((x - part(L, 1))/part(L, 2), x, u)
    else subst(x, v, u)
  )
  else subst(x, v, u))$

%mr_substFor4(w, v, u, x) := w*%mr_substFor3(v, u, x)$

%mr_substFor : lambda([a, b, c, [d]],
  if length(d) = 0
  then %mr_substFor3(a, b, c)
  else %mr_substFor4(a, b, c, part(d, 1)))$

/* Rubi's Dist — distribute the first argument over a Plus sum, leave
   everything else (a noun Sum in particular) multiplied. */
%mr_dist : lambda([a, b, [c]],
  %mr_ei_distribute(a, b, 0))$

/* Rubi :1518  NormalizePseudoBinomial[u, x] — a + b*(c+d*x)^n. Fails
   closed to u (the .m's False[[1]] hard-errors; the class-1 call sites
   gate on PseudoBinomialPairQ first). */
%mr_normalizePseudoBinomial(u, x) := block([L],
  L : %mr_pseudoBinomial_parts(u, x),
  if atom(L) then u
  else part(L, 1) + part(L, 2)*(part(L, 3) + part(L, 4)*x)^part(L, 5))$

/* Rubi's Cancel — the builtin is unbound in this build (measured);
   factor() is the closest cancel-without-expand reading. */
%mr_cancel(u) := block([r],
  r : errcatch(factor(u)),
  if is(r = []) then u else part(r, 1))$

/* Rubi :1668  PolyGCD[u, v, x] — the monic x-dependent gcd. The .m's
   PolynomialGCD is monic; Maxima's gcd carries the content, so the
   leading coefficient is divided out (the NonfreeFactors reading then
   drops any surviving free factor). */
%mr_polyGCD(u, v, x) := block([g, d, lc],
  if %mr_polynomialQ(u, x) and %mr_polynomialQ(v, x)
  then (
    g : errcatch(gcd(u, v, x)),
    if is(g = []) then false
    else (
      g : part(g, 1),
      if freeof(x, g) then 1
      else (
        d : %mr_degree(g, x),
        lc : %mr_coeff(g, x, d),
        g/lc
      )
    )
  )
  else false)$

/* Rubi :1644  RationalFunctionExpand[u, x] — the algebraic
   ExpandIntegrand surface (the .m's RationalFunctionFactors /
   NonrationalFunctionFactors split is deferred). */
%mr_rationalFunctionExpand(u, x) := %mr_expandIntegrand2(u, x)$

/* ---- cluster K: the last B-tier names --------------------------------
 * Root is the rational reading of the Mathematica Root object (the
 * k-th root, in ascending real-part order, when it is an explicit
 * rational). IntSum / IntTerm are the .m definitions (:7694/:7699) over
 * the already-ported FreeFactors / NonfreeFactors / RemoveContent
 * surface. Hold is the ShowStep wrapper — identity outside the
 * (unported) ShowStep machinery. */

%mr_freeTerms(u, x) := block([],
  if %mr_sumQ(u)
  then apply("+", map(
    lambda([z], if freeof(x, z) then z else 0), %mr_parts_list(u)))
  else if freeof(x, u) then u else 0)$

%mr_nonfreeTerms(u, x) := block([],
  if %mr_sumQ(u)
  then apply("+", map(
    lambda([z], if freeof(x, z) then 0 else z), %mr_parts_list(u)))
  else if freeof(x, u) then 0 else u)$

%mr_intTerm_linear_at(F, i, x, mode) :=
  block([f, base, m, R, j, rest],
    f : part(F, i),
    if mode = "power" then (
      if not atom(f) and op(f) = "^" then (
        base : part(f, 1),
        m : part(f, 2),
        if %mr_linearQ(base, x) and freeof(x, m)
        then (
          R : [],
          for j : 1 thru length(F) do
            if j # i then R : append(R, [part(F, j)]),
          rest : %mr_product_from_list(R),
          if freeof(x, rest) then [rest, base, m] else false
        )
        else false
      )
      else false
    )
    else (
      if not atom(f) and op(f) # "^" and %mr_linearQ(f, x)
      then (
        R : [],
        for j : 1 thru length(F) do
          if j # i then R : append(R, [part(F, j)]),
        rest : %mr_product_from_list(R),
        if freeof(x, rest) then [rest, f, 1] else false
      )
      else false
    ))$

/* c*v^m (v linear in x, c and m free of x) as [c, v, m], or false. A
   quotient is read as the negative-power form (Maxima stores c/v^m as
   a / node, not a ProductQ). */
%mr_intTerm_linear(u, x) := block([F, i, r, num, den],
  if not atom(u) and op(u) = "/" then (
    num : part(u, 1),
    den : part(u, 2),
    if freeof(x, num)
    then (
      if %mr_linearQ(den, x) then [num, den, -1]
      else if not atom(den) and op(den) = "^"
        and %mr_linearQ(part(den, 1), x)
        and freeof(x, part(den, 2))
      then [num, part(den, 1), -part(den, 2)]
      else false
    )
    else false
  )
  else (
    F : %mr_product_factors(u),
    r : false,
    for i : 1 thru length(F) do
      if r = false then
        r : %mr_intTerm_linear_at(F, i, x, "power"),
    if r # false then r
    else (
      for i : 1 thru length(F) do
        if r = false then
          r : %mr_intTerm_linear_at(F, i, x, "bare"),
      r
    )
  ))$

/* Rubi :7699  IntTerm[u, x] */
%mr_intTerm(u, x) := block([L, c, base, m, u1, ff, nf],
  if %mr_sumQ(u)
  then apply("+", map(lambda([z], %mr_intTerm(z, x)),
    %mr_parts_list(u)))
  else (
    L : %mr_intTerm_linear(u, x),
    if L # false
    then (
      c : part(L, 1),
      base : part(L, 2),
      m : part(L, 3),
      u1 : %mr_cancel(base),
      if is(m = -1)
      then c*log(%mr_removeContent(u1, x))/%mr_coeff(u1, x, 1)
      else if is(m = 1) and %mr_eqQ(c, 1) and %mr_sumQ(u1)
      then %mr_intSum(u1, x)
      else c*u1^(m + 1)/(%mr_coeff(u1, x, 1)*(m + 1))
    )
    else (
      ff : %mr_freeFactors(u, x),
      nf : %mr_nonfreeFactors(u, x),
      ff*mr_int(nf, x)
    )
  ))$

/* Rubi :7694  IntSum[u, x] */
%mr_intSum(u, x) :=
  %mr_freeTerms(u, x)*x + %mr_intTerm(%mr_nonfreeTerms(u, x), x)$

/* The rational-root reading of the Mathematica Root object: the k-th
   root of an explicit-rational polynomial (ascending, with multiplicity)
   when it is an explicit rational, else false (the rule declines). */
%mr_root_roots(e, var, acc) := block([base, exp, i],
  if atom(e) then acc
  else if numberp(e) then acc
  else if op(e) = "^" then (
    base : part(e, 1),
    exp : part(e, 2),
    if integerp(exp) and is(exp > 0)
    then (
      for i : 1 thru exp do
        acc : %mr_root_roots(base, var, acc),
      acc
    )
    else acc
  )
  else if op(e) = "*" then (
    for i : 1 thru length(e) do
      acc : %mr_root_roots(part(e, i), var, acc),
    acc
  )
  else if %mr_polynomialQ(e, var) and is(%mr_degree(e, var) = 1)
  then append(acc,
    [-%mr_coeff(e, var, 0)/%mr_coeff(e, var, 1)])
  else acc)$

%mr_root(poly, k) := block([d, i, ok, r, roots],
  if integerp(k) and %mr_polynomialQ(poly, x)
  then (
    d : %mr_degree(poly, x),
    ok : true,
    for i : 0 thru d do
      if not %mr_rationalQ(%mr_coeff(poly, x, i)) then ok : false,
    if ok
    then (
      r : errcatch(factor(poly)),
      if is(r = []) then false
      else (
        roots : %mr_root_roots(part(r, 1), x, []),
        roots : sort(roots),
        if length(roots) >= k
          and %mr_rationalQ(part(roots, k))
        then part(roots, k)
        else false
      )
    )
    else false
  )
  else false)$

/* Manual matcher for the two-binomial-power shape
   (A1 + B1*x^n)^E1 * (A2 + B2*x^n)^E2 (shared n != 0) — the 1.1.3.3
   (a+b x^n)^p (c+d x^n)^q family. matchfix CANNOT match the free-n
   pattern: it binds (a + b*x^3) against (A + B*x_^n) degenerately as
   n := 0 with B := b*x^3 (the coefficient absorbs the power), and the
   freeof(x) matchdeclare predicate that kills the degenerate binding
   also kills the match — no backtracking to the correct (a, b, 3)
   reading (measured 2026-08-25, 5.50.0/SBCL, /tmp/opencode/n1133b.mac:
   with true-declared coeffs the pattern returns the degenerate
   binding; with freeof(x)-declared coeffs it returns false). The
   1.1.3.3 free-n rules (27 of 67) therefore decline every integrand
   (191/286 family no-answers in the 2026-08-25 full run). This
   matcher walks the integrand structurally instead.
   Build quirk honored throughout (measured 2026-08-25, 5.50.0/SBCL):
   op() on an ATOM is FATAL ("part: argument must be a non-atomic
   expression") — every op() call below is guarded by an atom() test;
   and op(t) = "sqrt" (a FUNCTION head) never evaluates true inside a
   compiled if while op(t) = "^" / "*" / "/" / "+" (operator heads)
   do — the sqrt head is tested via string(op(t)) = "sqrt".
   Returns [A1, B1, n, E1, A2, B2, n, E2] (E signed: denominator
   factors carry a negative exponent) or false. Factor order is
   Maxima's canonical times order — the generated rule wrapper retries
   the swapped slot assignment. */

/* Build quirk honored in the %mr_mbp* walkers below (measured
   2026-08-25, 5.50.0/SBCL): return() inside a for-loop is LOOP-LEVEL
   in this build — it exits the loop, not the function (probe:
   for i : 1 thru 4 do (if is(i = 2) then return(false), ...) then
   "DONE" returns "DONE", not false). Every early bail-out below is
   therefore a flag + `thru 1` exit; no return() appears inside a
   loop. The same build also leaves `var = false` unevaluated in a
   compiled if (the lazy-boolean trap), so every such comparison goes
   through is(...). */

/* one term of a binomial base: [c, 0] (constant) or [c, n] (monomial
   c*x^n, n nonzero and free of x), else false. The `res : <expr>`
   assignment-bail idiom (measured 2026-08-25, 5.50.0/SBCL): a bare
   `if ... then false else ...` used as the block's value evaluates
   to UNDEFINED inside a compiled function (the 5.50.0 dev-build
   if-then-else-value trap), so every branch assigns res. */
%mr_mbp_term(t, x) := block([i, u, k, b, ok, res],
  t : expand(t),
  if freeof(x, t) then return([t, 0]),
  if t = x then return([1, 1]),
  if atom(t) then return(false),
  if op(t) = "^" then (
    if part(t,1) = x then return([1, part(t,2)])
    else return(false)
  ),
  if op(t) = "*" then (
    k : false, b : 1, ok : true,
    for i : 1 thru length(t) do (
      u : part(t, i),
      if is(ok = true) then (
        if u = x then (
          if k # false then ok : false else k : 1
        ) else (
          if atom(u) then (
            if freeof(x, u) then b : b*u
            else ok : false
          ) else (
            if op(u) = "^" then (
              if part(u,1) = x then (
                if k # false then ok : false else k : part(u,2)
              )
              else ok : false
            ) else (
              if freeof(x, u) then b : b*u
              else ok : false
            )
          )
        )
      )
    ),
    if is(ok = false) then (
      res : false
    ) else (
      if k = false then (
        res : false
      ) else (
        res : [b, k]
      )
    )
  ) else (
    if op(t) = "/" then (
      res : %mr_mbp_mono2(t, x)
    ) else (
      res : false
    )
  ),
  res)$

/* (c1*x^n1)/(c2*x^n2) style quotient monomial -> [c, n]. */
%mr_mbp_mono2(t, x) := block([num, den, nr, dr, res],
  num : part(t,1), den : part(t,2),
  nr : %mr_mbp_term(num, x),
  dr : %mr_mbp_term(den, x),
  if is(nr = false) or is(dr = false) then (
    res : false
  ) else (
    if is(part(nr,2) = 0) or is(part(dr,2) = 0) then (
      res : false
    ) else (
      res : [part(nr,1)/part(dr,1), part(nr,2) - part(dr,2)]
    )
  ),
  res)$

/* base = A + B*x^n (exactly two terms: one constant, one nonzero
   power monomial) -> [A, B, n], else false. */
%mr_mbp_base(base, x) := block([t1, t2, r1, r2, res],
  if atom(base) then (
    res : false
  ) else (
    if op(base) # "+" or length(base) # 2 then (
      res : false
    ) else (
      t1 : part(base,1), t2 : part(base,2),
      r1 : %mr_mbp_term(t1, x), r2 : %mr_mbp_term(t2, x),
      if is(r1 = false) or is(r2 = false) then (
        res : false
      ) else (
        if is(part(r1,2) = 0) and is(part(r2,2) # 0) then (
          if is(part(r2,2) = 0) then (
            res : false
          ) else (
            if not freeof(x, part(r2,2)) then (
              res : false
            ) else (
              res : [part(r1,1), part(r2,1), part(r2,2)]
            )
          )
        ) else (
          if is(part(r2,2) = 0) and is(part(r1,2) # 0) then (
            if not freeof(x, part(r1,2)) then (
              res : false
            ) else (
              res : [part(r2,1), part(r1,1), part(r1,2)]
            )
          ) else (
            res : false
          )
        )
      )
    )
  ),
  res)$

/* one factor of a times node (or the whole of a non-times side).
   sgn = 1 (numerator) or -1 (denominator); t may be a binomial sum,
   a power of one, or a sqrt of one. Returns [A, B, n, sgn*E]. */
%mr_mbp_factor(t, x, sgn) := block([base, E, r, res],
  if atom(t) then (
    res : false
  ) else (
    E : 1, base : t,
    if op(t) = "^" then (base : part(t,1), E : part(t,2)),
    if string(op(t)) = "sqrt" then (base : part(t,1), E : 1/2),
    r : %mr_mbp_base(base, x),
    if is(r = false) then (
      res : false
    ) else (
      res : [part(r,1), part(r,2), part(r,3), sgn*E]
    )
  ),
  res)$

/* one side of a quotient -> list of [A, B, n, sgn*E] factors, or
   false on the first non-binpow factor. A side that is not a "*"
   node is a single factor. */
%mr_mbp_side(e, x, sgn) := block([facs, i, r, ok, res],
  if atom(e) then (
    res : false
  ) else (
    facs : [], ok : true,
    if op(e) = "*" then (
      for i : 1 thru length(e) do (
        if is(ok = true) then (
          r : %mr_mbp_factor(part(e, i), x, sgn),
          if is(r = false) then (
            ok : false
          ) else (
            facs : cons(r, facs)
          )
        )
      )
    ) else (
      r : %mr_mbp_factor(e, x, sgn),
      if is(r = false) then (
        ok : false
      ) else (
        facs : [r]
      )
    ),
    if is(ok = false) then (
      res : false
    ) else (
      res : facs
    )
  ),
  res)$

/* the shared-n / exactly-two / n-free-of-x checks on a factor list. */
%mr_mbp2_tail(facs, x) := block([res],
  if length(facs) # 2 then (
    res : false
  ) else (
    if part(part(facs,1),3) # part(part(facs,2),3) then (
      res : false
    ) else (
      if not freeof(x, part(facs,1), 3) then (
        res : false
      ) else (
        res : facs
      )
    )
  ),
  res)$

/* f -> [A1, B1, n, E1, A2, B2, n, E2]: f is EXACTLY two binpow
   factors with a shared n, else false (a third factor — a stray
   constant, another power — declines, the same way the .m pattern
   would). */
%mr_mbp2(f, x) := block([facs, s, res],
  if atom(f) then (
    res : false
  ) else (
    if op(f) = "/" then (
      s : %mr_mbp_side(part(f,1), x, 1),
      if is(s = false) then (
        res : false
      ) else (
        facs : %mr_mbp_side(part(f,2), x, -1),
        if is(facs = false) then (
          res : false
        ) else (
          res : %mr_mbp2_tail(append(s, facs), x)
        )
      )
    ) else (
      facs : %mr_mbp_side(f, x, 1),
      if is(facs = false) then (
        res : false
      ) else (
        res : %mr_mbp2_tail(facs, x)
      )
    )
  ),
  res)$

/* ---- generic binomial-power factor decomposition ----------------------
 * %mr_binpowfactors(f, x) -> false | [c, M, rest, L]
 *   c    x-free constant factor (1 when none)
 *   M    the monomial factor c2*x^k as [c2, k], false when none
 *   rest product of the factors that are NEITHER binomial powers nor a
 *        monomial (1 when none) — the slot the .m's P(x) pattern
 *        variable consumes
 *   L    list of [A, B, n, E], one per binomial-power factor (A + B*x^n)^E
 *        (a bare factor is E=1; sqrt(A + B*x^n) is E=1/2; a factor in the
 *        DENOMINATOR carries the sign of E — the same convention as the
 *        two-factor %mr_mbp2). n is x-free for every factor.
 *
 * The generalization of %mr_mbp2 (which stays for the committed 1.1.3.1/
 * 1.1.3.3 rules): any NUMBER of binpow factors, plus the monomial and
 * P(x) slots the 1.1.1.x (m-free linear) and 1.1.3.2/4/6/8 (free n,
 * (c x)^m) families need. The generated manual-matcher rule body assigns
 * the factors of L to the rule's slots and takes `rest` for its P(x)
 * slot.
 *
 * Measured 2026-08-25, 5.50.0/SBCL (the walker's two premises):
 *  - length() of a times node is the factor count and part() reaches
 *    every factor (probes/maxima/probe-times-length.out);
 *  - a free-exponent pattern slot (pa + pb*x)^pm does NOT match a BARE
 *    target factor (a + b*x): Maxima's canonical form strips (expr)^1,
 *    so the target has no Power node — probes/maxima/
 *    probe-matchfix-bare-factor.out. This walker recovers the implicit
 *    E=1 that the matcher cannot see.
 *
 * Build quirks observed while writing this (see the %mr_mbp2 block):
 * op() on an atom is fatal (every op() is atom()-guarded); sqrt heads
 * are tested via string(op(t)); every branch assigns res (a bare
 * if/then/else as the block value evaluates UNDEFINED); comparisons go
 * through is(); no return() inside for-loops (loop-level only). */
/* Split an expression into IRREDUCIBLE factors, carrying a sign: a
   factor from the NUMERATOR has sgn=1, from the DENOMINATOR sgn=-1.
   A times node distributes over its factors; a quotient recurses into
   both sides. Everything else (atoms, powers, sqrt, sums) is one
   factor — the classifier below decides what it is. */
%mr_bpf_walk(e, x, sgn) := block([facs, i, res],
  if atom(e) then (
    res : [[e, sgn]]
  ) else (
    if op(e) = "*" then (
      facs : [],
      for i : 1 thru length(e) do
        facs : append(facs, %mr_bpf_walk(part(e, i), x, sgn)),
      res : facs
    ) else (
      if op(e) = "/" then (
        res : append(%mr_bpf_walk(part(e, 1), x, sgn),
                     %mr_bpf_walk(part(e, 2), x, -sgn))
      ) else (
        res : [[e, sgn]]
      )
    )
  ),
  res)$

/* a monomial factor: bare x^k, (c0*x)^k, or x -> [c0, k*sgn], else
   false. (c0*x)^k is the .m's (c_.*x_)^m_ slot: c0 binds to c_, k to
   m_. */
%mr_bpf_monomial(t, sgn, x) := block([base, k, res],
  if is(t = x) then (
    res : [1, sgn]
  ) else (
    if op(t) = "^" then (
      base : part(t, 1), k : part(t, 2),
      if is(base = x) then (
        res : [1, sgn*k]
      ) else (
        if op(base) = "*" and is(length(base) = 2) and is(part(base, 2) = x)
           and is(freeof(x, part(base, 1))) then (
          res : [part(base, 1), sgn*k]
        ) else (
          res : false
        )
      )
    ) else (
      res : false
    )
  ),
  res)$

/* a binomial-power factor (A + B*x^n)^E, incl. the BARE form (implicit
   E=1 — the case the matchfix cannot see, probe-matchfix-bare-factor)
   and sqrt (E=1/2); the denominator sign is folded into E.
   -> [A, B, n, E], else false. */
%mr_bpf_factor(t, sgn, x) := block([base, E, r, res],
  base : t, E : sgn,
  if op(t) = "^" then (
    E : sgn*part(t, 2),
    base : part(t, 1)
  ),
  if string(op(base)) = "sqrt" then (
    E : E/2,
    base : part(base, 1)
  ),
  /* sqrt of a power: 1/sqrt((A+B x^n)^k) may stay uncombined */
  if op(base) = "^" then (
    E : E*part(base, 2),
    base : part(base, 1)
  ),
  r : %mr_mbp_base(base, x),
  if is(r = false) then (
    res : false
  ) else (
    res : [part(r, 1), part(r, 2), part(r, 3), E]
  ),
  res)$

/* One walk factor [t, sgn] -> a list of [A, B, n, E] binomial-power
   factors (empty list: not a binpow factor). The exponent distributes
   over a PRODUCT base: Sqrt[(A+B x)(C+D x)] is the .m's single factor
   Sqrt[(a_+b_.*x_)*(c_+d_.*x_)] and equals (A+B x)^(1/2)*(C+D x)^(1/2);
   (c0*(A+B x)^E1*(C+D x)^E2)^p expands the same way. Each sub-factor
   must itself be binpow or monomial — anything else (a polynomial
   base, a nested product) declines the whole factor. */
%mr_bpf_factorlist(t, sgn, x) := block([base, E, i, u, r, out, res],
  base : t, E : sgn,
  if op(t) = "^" then (
    E : sgn*part(t, 2),
    base : part(t, 1)
  ),
  if string(op(base)) = "sqrt" then (
    E : E/2,
    base : part(base, 1)
  ),
  if op(base) = "^" then (
    E : E*part(base, 2),
    base : part(base, 1)
  ),
  if op(base) = "*" then (
    out : [],
    for i : 1 thru length(base) do (
      u : part(base, i),
      if is(freeof(x, u)) then (
        /* a constant inside the product base: the .m has no constant
           slot — a stray constant declines the factor */
        out : false
      ) else (
        r : %mr_bpf_monomial(u, 1, x),
        if is(r = false) then (
          r : %mr_bpf_factor(u, 1, x),
          if is(r = false) or is(out = false) then (
            out : false
          ) else (
            out : append(out,
                         [[part(r, 1), part(r, 2), part(r, 3),
                           part(r, 4)*E]])
          )
        ) else (
          if is(out = false) then (
            true
          ) else (
            out : false
          )
        )
      )
    ),
    if is(out = false) then (
      res : []
    ) else (
      res : out
    )
  ) else (
    r : %mr_mbp_base(base, x),
    if is(r = false) then (
      res : []
    ) else (
      res : [[part(r, 1), part(r, 2), part(r, 3), E]]
    )
  ),
  res)$

/* f -> [c, M, rest, L]
   c    product of the x-free factors (1 when none)
   M    [c0, k] the combined monomial factor, false when none
   rest product of the factors that are NEITHER monomial NOR binomial
        power (1 when none) — what the .m's P(x) pattern variable
        consumes, times any lin factors a rule body leaves unassigned
   L    [A, B, n, E] per binomial-power factor, part order
 * The generalization of the two-factor %mr_mbp2 (which stays for the
   committed 1.1.3.1/1.1.3.3 rules): any number of binpow factors,
   plus the monomial and P(x) slots the 1.1.1.x (m-free linear) and
   1.1.3.2/4/6/8 ((c x)^m, free n) families need.
 * Measured 2026-08-25, 5.50.0/SBCL: length() of a times node is the
   factor count (probe-times-length.out); a free-exponent pattern slot
   declines bare (exponent-1) factors (probe-matchfix-bare-factor.out).
 * Build quirks applied (see the %mr_mbp2 block): op() atom-guarded,
   sqrt tested via string(op), every branch assigns res, comparisons
   through is(), no return() in for-loops. */
%mr_binpowfactors(f, x) := block([facs, c, M, rest, L, i, t, sgn, r, res],
  facs : %mr_bpf_walk(f, x, 1),
  c : 1, M : false, rest : 1, L : [],
  for i : 1 thru length(facs) do (
    t : part(part(facs, i), 1),
    sgn : part(part(facs, i), 2),
    if is(freeof(x, t)) then (
      c : c*t^sgn
    ) else (
      r : %mr_bpf_monomial(t, sgn, x),
      if is(r = false) then (
        r : %mr_bpf_factorlist(t, sgn, x),
        if is(length(r) = 0) then (
          rest : rest*t^sgn
        ) else (
          L : append(L, r)
        )
      ) else (
        if is(M = false) then (
          M : r
        ) else (
          M : [part(M, 1)*part(r, 1), part(M, 2) + part(r, 2)]
        )
      )
    )
  ),
  res : [c, M, rest, L],
  res)$

/* Rubi's ShowStep Hold wrapper — identity outside the (unported)
   ShowStep machinery. */
%mr_hold(u) := u$

/* ---- class-2 (exponentials) cluster A: the small predicates -------- */

/* Rubi TrueQ — true iff the value is literally true (Mathematica
   builtin; used in class 2 only on the $UseGamma control flag).
   is() gives the true/false/unknown trichotomy; unknown is not true. */
%mr_trueQ(v) := block([], is(is(v) = true))$

/* The package home of Rubi's $UseGamma control variable. Undefined in
   the pinned Rubi 4 (grep 2026-08-28: absent from Rubi.m and the
   utility files); the class-2 corpus file headers state the optimal
   antiderivatives assume $UseGamma = false, so the default is false —
   the gamma-form rules decline by default and fire only if the flag is
   set true explicitly. A variable, not a function (the
   mr_simplify_flag precedent, this file's line 11). */
mr_use_gamma_flag : false$

/* Rubi PowerQ (:162) — the Power-head test. MEASURED 2026-08-28
   (5.50.0/SBCL): a power's op is the STRING "^" (stringp(op(x^2))
   true, symbolp false; is(op(x^2) = "^") true — this file's house
   idiom, e.g. line 624; is(op(x^2) = power) false, the symbol
   power # the string "^"). Maxima strips the exponent-1 Power head
   (x^1 -> x), so a bare linear form reaches this as an atom/sum —
   the %mr_linearQ branch of the PowerOfLinear family covers that
   case; and x^(1/2) is stored as a 'sqrt node (the line-1242 note),
   so PowerQ(sqrt(x)) is false by design — this predicate sees only
   kept power nodes. */
%mr_powerQ(u) := block([],
  if atom(u) then false
  else is(op(u) = "^"))$

/* Rubi CalculusQ — head in the calculus list ($CalculusFunctions =
   {D, Integrate, Sum, Product, Int, Unintegrable, CannotIntegrate,
   Dif, Subst}). The package nouns stand in: the diff/integrate/
   sum/product nouns, and the 'unintegrable[f, x] noun (the catch-all
   marker, mr_unintegrable, this file's line 27); mr_int and %mr_subst
   are evaluated functions (no noun forms) but their names stay in the
   list for the stand-in mapping. Dif and CannotIntegrate have no
   package counterpart.
   MEASURED 2026-08-28 (5.50.0/SBCL): a NOUN's op is an internal
   symbol whose string() is the head name — NOT the function symbol
   (is(op('integrate[f(x),x]) = 'integrate) false, likewise for
   'diff/'sum/'product/'unintegrable), so a member() over function
   symbols never matches; the test runs on string(op(u)) — a member
   over the string list (is(string(op('diff[f(x),x])) = "diff")
   true; the diff noun's internal symbol DISPLAYS as `derivative` in
   math mode but string()s to "diff"). For operator heads op()
   already returns a string (op(f*x) is "*"), and string() of a
   string is the identity, so one test covers both. member() returns
   a boolean in this build (note at line 758). Also measured:
   integrate/sum of an x-INDEPENDENT summand EVALUATE to an ordinary
   product (integrate(f, x) = f*x, sum(f, x, 1, n) = f*n even at
   symbolic bound, op "*"), so a calculus noun only exists when the
   summed/integrated object depends on the variable. */
%mr_calculusQ(u) := block([s],
  if atom(u) then false
  else (
    s : string(op(u)),
    is(member(s, ["integrate", "diff", "sum", "product",
                  "mr_int", "mr_subst", "unintegrable"]) = true)
  ))$

/* Rubi HyperbolicQ — the six hyperbolic heads (the
   FunctionOfExponential machinery's explicit-exponential detection).
   All six are native in this build (5.50.0). MEASURED 2026-08-28: an
   ordinary function application's op IS the function symbol
   (is(op(sinh(x)) = sinh) true — unlike the noun heads above), so
   member() over the symbol list matches directly. */
%mr_hyperbolicQ(u) := block([],
  if atom(u) then false
  else is(member(op(u), [sinh, cosh, tanh, coth, sech, csch]) = true))$

/* Rubi EveryQ — func over every element, true iff all true.
   MEASURED 2026-08-28 (5.50.0/SBCL): list iteration is `for e in
   lst` — `for e : lst` parses as `for e from lst` (the definition
   echo shows it), which iterates the whole list as ONE element and
   made everyQ([1,2,3]) false. f(e) applies the lambda parameter
   directly (measured equal to apply(f, [e])). */
%mr_everyQ(f, lst) := block([r],
  r : true,
  for e in lst while r do r : is(f(e)),
  r)$

/* PowerOfLinearQ / PowerOfLinearMatchQ / NormalizePowerOfLinear —
   UNDEFINED in the pinned Rubi 4 clone (measured 2026-08-28: no
   definition in IntegrationUtilityFunctions.m or anywhere in the
   clone; absent from the Rubi-5 stub too), yet called by 2.1 r17/r18
   and 2.3 r8/r9/r10/r38 (upstream numbers; the generated call sites
   are 2.1 r12/r13 and 2.3 r5/r6/r7/r35). Ported from the call-site
   contract: u is a rational power of a linear form in x.
   Storage adaptations, MEASURED 2026-08-28 (5.50.0/SBCL):
   * Maxima strips the exponent-1 Power head (x^1 -> x), so a bare
     linear form counts (the %mr_linearQ branch is the exponent-1
     case);
   * (linear)^(1/2) is stored as a 'sqrt node (the op string of
     (a+b*X)^(1/2) is "sqrt"), so the sqrt-of-linear branch is the
     exponent-1/2 case;
   * the EXONENT is the part that must be x-free (freeof(x,
     part(u, 2))): the base is linear in x by construction, and the
     generated replacement code itself tests freeof(x, part(uu, 2))
     (rules/class2/2_1.mac r13, 2.3 r7).
   The MatchQ variant is the same test WITHOUT the implicit-exponent
   branches — the strict stored-power structure (%mr_powerQ, x-free
   rational exponent, linear base). It must not be identical to the
   value test: the 2.3 r35 condition
   %mr_powerOfLinearQ(v, x) and not(%mr_powerOfLinearMatchQ(v, x))
   is unsatisfiable (Q and not Q) if the two coincide, which would
   kill the rule; the strict reading fires exactly for the
   implicit-exponent v (bare linear / sqrt-linear), whose
   replacement re-dispatches into the F^(linear) rules.
   NormalizePowerOfLinear is the identity: the progress the recursion
   needs (u^m combining into a simpler power) comes from Maxima's own
   power combining — ((a+b x)^(p/q))^m -> (a+b x)^(p m/q) — which the
   specialized 2.1 r1-r16 patterns then re-match. A pathological
   no-progress self-refire is bounded by the runner depth cap (no
   wrong answers; bounded cost) — revisit if the class-2 run shows
   timeout mass in these shapes (ledger: milestone-2). */
%mr_powerOfLinearQ(u, x) := block([],
  if atom(u) then false
  else if %mr_linearQ(u, x) then true
  else if string(op(u)) = "sqrt" and %mr_linearQ(part(u, 1), x) then true
  else if %mr_powerQ(u) and freeof(x, part(u, 2))
       and %mr_linearQ(part(u, 1), x) and %mr_rationalQ(part(u, 2))
  then true
  else false)$

%mr_powerOfLinearMatchQ(u, x) := block([],
  if atom(u) then false
  else if %mr_powerQ(u) and freeof(x, part(u, 2))
       and %mr_linearQ(part(u, 1), x) and %mr_rationalQ(part(u, 2))
  then true
  else false)$

%mr_normalizePowerOfLinear(u, x) := u$

/* ---- class-2 cluster B: the NormalizeIntegrand chain ---------------
 * Pinned definitions (IntegrationUtilityFunctions.m @
 * 61e9c18ea248061cd83c67882f7c91a73cef912d): :1988 (NormalizeIntegrand),
 * :1999 (Aux), :2007 (Factor), :2025/:2030 (FactorBase),
 * :2060 (NormalizeLeadTermSigns), :2068-:2075 (AbsorbMinusSign),
 * :2093 (SignOfFactor), :2111-:2133 (MergeMonomials), :2168
 * (SimplifyTerm), :2178 (TogetherSimplify), :1399 (MinimumMonomial-
 * Exponent), :1406-:1410 (MonomialExponent), :3996-:4009 (UnifySum /
 * UnifyTerms / UnifyTerm), :2995 (ContentFactorAux), :162 (PowerQ),
 * :198 (IntegerPowerQ).
 * The only class-2 call site is 2.3 r60 (upstream number; generated
 * 2.3 r101, rules/class2/2_3.mac:2231): Int[u*F^v*G^w, x] ->
 * Int[u*NormalizeIntegrand[E^z, x], x] with z binomial or quadratic —
 * the FactorBase base reaching this chain is %e (atom) or a
 * linear/quadratic sum.
 * Documented deviations:
 *   (a) TogetherSimplify drops TimeConstrained/$TimeLimit (Maxima has
 *       no time-constrained evaluator) and FixSimplify (its GCD power
 *       combining is subsumed by Maxima's own power combining on
 *       re-simplification) — the ratsimp/together chain is the
 *       measured Maxima equivalent.
 *   (b) ContentFactorAux's product-branch negative-sum-factor sign
 *       extraction and the UnifyNegativeBaseFactors deep branch
 *       (:3016, negative-base factor unification) are unreachable on
 *       the r60 shape — the port keeps the atom/integer-power/
 *       product/sum surface (the sum branch factors out the GCD of the
 *       term contents) and returns its input in the deep case.
 *   (c) %mr_numericFactor is NOT redefined here: the M1 definition
 *       (this file's line 1547, Rubi :1100) already IS the .m's
 *       NumericFactor, with the .m's leafCount<50 ContentFactor-
 *       round-trip kludge replaced by the direct GCD-of-term-factors
 *       sum branch. The kludge cannot work in Maxima: MEASURED
 *       2026-08-28 (5.50.0/SBCL) 2*((4+6*x)/2) -> 6*x+4 — the factored
 *       sum does not survive the quotient round-trip, so the kludge
 *       would always return 1 for a sum, contradicting this cluster's
 *       own test %mr_numericFactor(4 + 6*X) = 2. M1's version
 *       additionally carries the sqrt-node and unary-minus branches
 *       Maxima's storage needs; redefining it would shadow the M1
 *       consumers (%mr_nonnumericFactors, the sumSimplerQ family).
 * SignOfFactor's ProductQ branch: the .m's Map[SignOfFactor, u] over a
 * product yields a product of {n, v} pairs whose Part extraction
 * (NormalizeLeadTermSigns takes lst[[1]]/lst[[2]]) only composes when
 * the pairs are multiplied out; the port returns the COMPOSED pair
 * [n_total, v_total], which satisfies the usage contract ("n*v equals
 * u") and matches the .m on every r60-reachable shape (all factors
 * {1, f}).
 * Storage adaptations, MEASURED 2026-08-28 (5.50.0/SBCL 2.6.7):
 *   * a power's op is the STRING "^" — every power head test goes
 *     through %mr_powerQ (Task 4, this file's line 4548);
 *   * list iteration is `for t in args(u)` — `for t : args(u)` parses
 *     as `for t from args(u)` and iterates the whole list as ONE
 *     element (the Task-4 everyQ note, this file's line 4594);
 *   * return() inside a for-loop does not escape the block (probe:
 *     block([r], r:0, for i thru 3 while r=0 do if i=2 then return(99),
 *     r) -> 0, not 99) — monomialExponent's first-match scan is a
 *     for-thru guarded by is(r = 0);
 *   * while is(m - k >= 0) ERRORS ("Unable to evaluate predicate m - k
 *     >= 0") on symbolic exponents, but if is(m - k >= 0) stays a no-op
 *     NOUN — minimumMonomialExponent's scan is therefore a for-thru
 *     with an `if is(n - e > 0) then n : e` guard (the .m's PosQ is
 *     strict and false on undecidable; the zero-difference case is a
 *     no-op update either way, so the strict and >= readings agree on
 *     every decidable input);
 *   * Maxima has no Prepend — cons(x, lst) puts x FIRST (measured:
 *     cons(9,[1,2]) = [9,1,2]); prepend is a NOUN in this build;
 *   * 2*((6*X+4)/2) -> 6*X+4 at evaluation — contentFactor's sum
 *     branch c*(u/c) evaluates back to the input sum, the identity
 *     there being the decline-safe behavior;
 *   * 2*(-a-b*X) STAYS a 2-factor product at evaluation (op "*",
 *     args [2, -a-b*X]) — absorbMinusSign's 2-factor structural
 *     branch is reachable (and -u distributes anyway, so the arms
 *     agree in value);
 *   * A+B*X is a binomial (binomialMatchQ TRUE at n = 1), while
 *     A+B*X+C*X^2 is NOT a trinomial — the .m's QuadraticQ is true on
 *     symbolic coefficients (Coefficient[u,x,0]===0 is FALSE for a
 *     symbol), so the .m's own TrinomialQ is false — and the
 *     quadratic base reaches the PolynomialQ/degree<=4 ->
 *     ExpandToSum branch, exactly as in the .m's FactorBase;
 *   * M1 %mr_monomialQ(4, X) is false (a constant is not a monomial —
 *     M1 test "monomialQ 5"), unlike the .m's MonomialQ (a_ = 4,
 *     n_ defaulting to 0); in the normalizeIntegrandFactor monomial-
 *     sum branches the difference is a no-op: a constant term makes
 *     the minimum exponent 0, where x^min*(t/x^min) is the identity;
 *   * the .m's active SimplifyTerm returns w in BOTH branches (the
 *     LeafCount choice is commented out upstream at :2160-:2167);
 *     ported as written, with %mr_simp (the value-preserving
 *     zero-chain, this file's line 498) standing in for Simplify.
 */

/* Rubi :198  IntegerPowerQ — Head === Power && IntegerQ[exponent].
   Maxima: a kept power node (%mr_powerQ — the op is the string "^")
   with an explicit integer exponent; Maxima strips exponent-1 powers
   and stores exponent-1/2 as a sqrt node (the Task-4 notes), neither
   of which is an IntegerPower in the .m either. */
%mr_integerPowerQ(u) := block([],
  if atom(u) then false
  else if %mr_powerQ(u) and integerp(part(u, 2)) then true
  else false)$

/* Rubi :2995  ContentFactorAux (ContentFactor :2992 minus its
   TimeConstrained wrapper — the identity on timeout is decline-safe
   and Maxima has no time-constrained evaluator). Deviation (b): the
   product branch drops the .m's negative-sum-factor sign extraction
   (num=-num; ContentFactorAux[-#]) and the UnifyNegativeBaseFactors
   deep branch (:3016) — both unreachable on the r60 shape; the
   identity there is decline-safe. The sum branch factors out the GCD
   of the terms' numeric factors directly (the .m's sum branch goes
   through CommonFactors; the GCD reading is the numeric-content
   subset the r60 shapes need — and the numericFactor call is on each
   TERM, so it cannot cycle). The c*(u/c) result: MEASURED 2026-08-28
   (5.50.0/SBCL) 2*((6*X+4)/2) -> 6*X+4 — Maxima auto-expands the
   factored sum over the quotient round-trip, so the branch returns
   the input sum (the identity, decline-safe). */
%mr_contentFactor(u) := block([c, t],
  if atom(u) then u
  else if %mr_integerPowerQ(u) then
    if %mr_sumQ(part(u, 1)) and is(%mr_numericFactor(part(part(u, 1), 1)) < 0)
    then (-1)^part(u, 2)*%mr_contentFactor(-part(u, 1))^part(u, 2)
    else %mr_contentFactor(part(u, 1))^part(u, 2)
  else if not atom(u) and is(op(u) = "*") then
    apply("*", map(lambda([f], %mr_contentFactor(f)), args(u)))
  else if %mr_sumQ(u) then (
    c : 0,
    for t in args(u) do c : gcd(c, %mr_numericFactor(t)),
    if is(c # 1) and is(c # 0) then c*(u/c) else u
  )
  else u)$

/* Rubi :2093  SignOfFactor — the pair [n, v] with n*v = u and
   n^2 = 1. Storage adaptation, MEASURED 2026-08-28 (5.50.0/SBCL):
   the ProductQ branch returns the COMPOSED pair (the .m's
   Map[SignOfFactor, u] leaves a product of pairs — see the cluster
   header). Maxima sum term order is x-terms first (args(-4-6*X) =
   [-6*X, -4]), so "first term" is Maxima's stored first part; for
   the r60 shapes the lead-term sign is undecidable anyway (symbolic
   coefficients, numericFactor of a symbolic monomial is 1). */
%mr_signOfFactor(u) := block([],
  if (if %mr_rationalQ(u) then is(u < 0) else false)
     or (if %mr_sumQ(u) then is(%mr_numericFactor(part(u, 1)) < 0) else false)
  then [-1, -u]
  else if %mr_integerPowerQ(u) and %mr_sumQ(part(u, 1))
        and is(%mr_numericFactor(part(part(u, 1), 1)) < 0)
  then [(-1)^part(u, 2), (-part(u, 1))^part(u, 2)]
  else if not atom(u) and is(op(u) = "*") then
    block([ns, vs, p, f],
      ns : 1, vs : 1,
      for f in args(u) do (
        p : %mr_signOfFactor(f),
        ns : ns*part(p, 1),
        vs : vs*part(p, 2)),
      [ns, vs])
  else if not atom(u) and is(op(u) = "-") then
    /* a unary minus wraps the product: the .m reads -u as a product
       with a -1 factor (SignOfFactor[-1] = {-1, 1}); the minus-wrap
       is Maxima's storage of that — MEASURED 2026-08-28 (5.50.0/SBCL):
       op(-3*X) = "-", args(-3*X) = [3 X] (likewise -2*(a+b*X) — the
       bare sum case auto-distributes to a sum node and is caught by
       the first condition above). M1 precedent: the numericFactor
       "-" branch, this file's line 1573. */
    block([p],
      p : %mr_signOfFactor(part(u, 1)),
      [-part(p, 1), part(p, 2)])
  else [1, u])$

/* Rubi :2068-:2075  AbsorbMinusSign — an expression equal to -u with
   the minus pushed into a sum factor where possible. The .m's
   odd-power branch (u_*v_Plus^m /; OddQ[m]) is dropped: for the r60
   shapes the value -u is the normalized form. The 2-factor
   structural branch IS reachable — MEASURED 2026-08-28
   (5.50.0/SBCL): 2*(-a-b*X) stays a 2-factor product at evaluation
   (op "*", args [2, -a-b*X]); -u distributes anyway, so the arms
   agree in value. */
%mr_absorbMinusSign(u) := block([f1, f2],
  if not atom(u) and is(op(u) = "*") and is(length(args(u)) = 2) then (
    f1 : part(args(u), 1), f2 : part(args(u), 2),
    if %mr_sumQ(f1) then -f1*f2
    else if %mr_sumQ(f2) then -f2*f1
    else -u
  )
  else -u)$

/* Rubi :2060  NormalizeLeadTermSigns — the .m's
   With[{lst=If[ProductQ[u], Map[SignOfFactor,u], SignOfFactor[u]]},
   If[lst[[1]]==1, lst[[2]], AbsorbMinusSign[lst[[2]]]]]: in the port
   %mr_signOfFactor already returns the COMPOSED pair for products
   (the .m's Map leaves a product of pairs — see the cluster header),
   so the single signOfFactor call is the faithful structure for both
   .m branches. */
%mr_normalizeLeadTermSigns(u) := block([lst],
  lst : %mr_signOfFactor(u),
  if is(part(lst, 1) = 1) then part(lst, 2)
  else %mr_absorbMinusSign(part(lst, 2)))$

/* Rubi :1406-:1410  MonomialExponent — the exponent of x in the
   monomial u (the first x-power factor in the product scan).
   MEASURED 2026-08-28 (5.50.0/SBCL): return() inside a for-loop does
   not escape the block (probe: block([r], r:0, for i thru 3 while
   r=0 do if i=2 then return(99), r) -> 0, not 99), so the first-
   match scan is a for-thru guarded by is(r = 0) — the guard also
   stays a no-op NOUN if a symbolic exponent was captured (the .m's
   n_ may be x-free symbolic). The .m's x_^n_. matches BARE x (n
   defaulting to 1); Maxima strips exponent-1 powers, so a bare-x
   factor is an ATOM — the scan's powerQ test misses it (fix round 1,
   2026-08-28 review; measured before the fix on 5.50.0/SBCL:
   monomialExponent(5*X, X) = 0, monomialExponent(X, X) = 0, where
   the .m gives 1) — the atom(f) and is(f = x) scan arm and the
   is(u = x) whole-u arm are that default. M1 precedent: the
   monomialQ product scan counts bare x the same way (this file's
   line 661). */
%mr_monomialExponent(u, x) := block([f, r, i, n],
  if freeof(x, u) then 0
  else if not atom(u) and is(op(u) = "*") then (
    r : 0,
    n : length(args(u)),
    for i : 1 thru n do (
      f : part(args(u), i),
      if is(r = 0) then (
        if %mr_powerQ(f) and is(part(f, 1) = x) and freeof(x, part(f, 2))
        then r : part(f, 2)
        else if atom(f) and is(f = x) then r : 1)),
    r
  )
  else if %mr_powerQ(u) and is(part(u, 1) = x) and freeof(x, part(u, 2))
  then part(u, 2)
  else if is(u = x) then 1
  else 0)$

/* Rubi :1399  MinimumMonomialExponent — u is a sum whose terms are
   monomials; the smallest x-exponent. The .m's Scan/PosQ update
   (PosQ strict, false on undecidable) is a for-thru with an if-
   guard: MEASURED 2026-08-28 (5.50.0/SBCL) while is(m - k >= 0)
   ERRORS ("Unable to evaluate predicate m - k >= 0") on symbolic
   exponents, but if is(m - k >= 0) stays a no-op NOUN — the guard
   form is safe in both cases and implements the strict reading (the
   zero-difference case is a no-op update either way, so the .m's
   PosQ and a >= 0 reading agree on every decidable input). */
%mr_minimumMonomialExponent(u, x) := block([n, t, e],
  n : %mr_monomialExponent(part(u, 1), x),
  for t in args(u) do (
    e : %mr_monomialExponent(t, x),
    if is(n - e > 0) then n : e),
  n)$

/* Rubi :2111-:2133  MergeMonomials. Rules 1-2 (the proportional-
   linear-power merge, :2111; the (c*(a+b x)^n)^p merge, :2120) are
   unreachable on the r60 shape (the factors are %e, %e^(linear),
   %e^(quadratic) — no linear powers); deferred with the identity
   (decline-safe). Rule 3 (a_*u_^m_ -> a*u^Simplify[m], :2129) is a
   Maxima no-op: a*u^m is already the simplified form for x-free m.
   Rule 4 (linear -> Cancel, :2133) is ported. */
%mr_mergeMonomials(u, x) := block([],
  if atom(u) then u
  else if %mr_linearQ(u, x) then %mr_cancel(u)
  else u)$

/* Rubi :2178  TogetherSimplify — the .m's
   TimeConstrained[With[{v=Together[Simplify[Together[u]]]},
   TimeConstrained[FixSimplify[v],$TimeLimit/3,v]],$TimeLimit,u]:
   deviation (a) — Maxima has no time-constrained evaluator and
   FixSimplify's GCD power combining is subsumed by Maxima's own
   power combining on re-simplification; the ratsimp/together chain
   is the measured Maxima equivalent (value-preserving). */
%mr_togetherSimplify(u) := ratsimp(%mr_together(ratsimp(%mr_together(u))))$

/* The .m's MatchQ[v, x^m_.*w_ /; FreeQ[m, x] && SumQ[w]] (the
   :2046 FactorBase sum-branch condition) — a 2-factor product with
   one x-power factor (exponent x-free) and one sum factor, either
   order. In the .m pattern the w_ slot absorbs the remaining
   product, so extra factors make w a product, not a sum — the
   2-factor test is the faithful reading. */
%mr_xpwsumP(v, x) := block([f1, f2],
  if atom(v) or not is(op(v) = "*") or is(length(args(v)) # 2) then false
  else (
    f1 : part(args(v), 1), f2 : part(args(v), 2),
    if %mr_powerQ(f1) and is(part(f1, 1) = x) and freeof(x, part(f1, 2))
       and %mr_sumQ(f2) then true
    else if %mr_powerQ(f2) and is(part(f2, 1) = x) and freeof(x, part(f2, 2))
         and %mr_sumQ(f1) then true
    else false))$

/* Rubi :4009  UnifyTerm — the .m's Prepend[lst, x] puts x FIRST;
   Maxima has no Prepend (it is a NOUN in this build) — MEASURED
   2026-08-28 (5.50.0/SBCL): cons(x, lst) is the equivalent
   (cons(9,[1,2]) = [9,1,2]). */
%mr_unifyTerm(term, lst, x) := block([tmp],
  if length(lst) = 0 then [term]
  else (
    tmp : %mr_simp(part(lst, 1)/term, [x]),
    if freeof(x, tmp) then cons((1 + tmp)*term, rest(lst))
    else cons(part(lst, 1), %mr_unifyTerm(term, rest(lst), x))))$

/* Rubi :4003  UnifyTerms */
%mr_unifyTerms(lst, x) := block([],
  if length(lst) = 0 then lst
  else %mr_unifyTerm(part(lst, 1), %mr_unifyTerms(rest(lst), x), x))$

/* Rubi :3996  UnifySum */
%mr_unifySum(u, x) := block([],
  if %mr_sumQ(u) then apply("+", %mr_unifyTerms(args(u), x))
  else %mr_simplifyTerm(u, x))$

/* Rubi :2168  SimplifyTerm — the .m's ACTIVE definition returns w in
   BOTH branches (the LeafCount[v]<LeafCount[w] choice is commented
   out upstream at :2160-:2167); ported as written. Maxima has no
   Simplify — %mr_simp (the value-preserving zero-chain, this file's
   line 498) stands in. */
%mr_simplifyTerm(u, x) := block([v, w],
  v : %mr_simp(u, [x]),
  w : %mr_together(v),
  %mr_normalizeIntegrand(w, x))$

/* Rubi :2030  NormalizeIntegrandFactorBase (the :2025 x^m_*u_
   pre-distribution rule is unreachable on the r60 shape — deferred
   with the fall-through, decline-safe). Branch order is the .m's:
   binomial, trinomial, product, polynomial of degree <= 4, sum,
   (last) Map over parts. MEASURED 2026-08-28 (5.50.0/SBCL): A+B*X
   is a binomial (binomialMatchQ TRUE at n = 1) so the linear base
   returns as-is; A+B*X+C*X^2 is NOT a trinomial (the .m's
   QuadraticQ is true on symbolic coefficients — Coefficient[u,x,0]
   === 0 is FALSE for a symbol — so the .m's own TrinomialQ is
   false), and the quadratic base reaches the polynomial/degree<=4 ->
   ExpandToSum branch, exactly as in the .m. The last branch is the
   .m's Map-over-parts (:2048) — Map KEEPS THE HEAD, so the port
   reconstructs with apply(op(u), …) (Maxima's op/args decomposition
   round-trips any non-list expression: "^", "sqrt", "-", function
   heads). Fix round 1 (2026-08-28 review): the branch previously
   read apply("+", …), destroying the head (measured on this build
   5.50.0/SBCL: sqrt(a+b*x) -> a+b*x, -(x^2+3*x)^3 -> the expanded
   -x^6-9*x^5-27*x^4-27*x^3). Reachable domain (sums never reach it —
   the sumQ branch above catches them; products and degree<=4
   polynomials are caught earlier): powers with x in the base whose
   degree exceeds 4 or whose base is not a (match) binomial/trinomial
   ((x+1)^5 — x^5 is a binomial, a = 0), sqrt nodes, minus-wrapped
   powers, other function heads. Quotients are stored as products of
   an inverse-power factor, which the product branch above handles. */
%mr_normalizeIntegrandFactorBase(u, x) := block([v],
  if atom(u) then u
  else if %mr_binomialQ(u, x) then
    if %mr_binomialMatchQ(u, x) then u else %mr_expandToSum(u, x)
  else if %mr_trinomialQ(u, x) then
    if %mr_trinomialMatchQ(u, x) then u else %mr_expandToSum(u, x)
  else if not atom(u) and is(op(u) = "*") then
    apply("*", map(lambda([f], %mr_normalizeIntegrandFactor(f, x)), args(u)))
  else if %mr_polynomialQ(u, x) and is(%mr_degree(u, x) <= 4) then
    %mr_expandToSum(u, x)
  else if %mr_sumQ(u) then (
    v : %mr_togetherSimplify(u),
    if %mr_sumQ(v) or %mr_xpwsumP(v, x)
       or is(%mr_leafCount(v) > %mr_leafCount(u) + 2)
    then %mr_unifySum(u, x)
    else %mr_normalizeIntegrandFactorBase(v, x)
  )
  else apply(op(u), map(lambda([f], %mr_normalizeIntegrandFactor(f, x)), args(u))))$

/* Rubi :2007  NormalizeIntegrandFactor. The .m's last branch maps
   #/x^min without Simplify while the integer-power branch maps
   Simplify[#/x^min] — the port uses %mr_simp in both (value-
   preserving zero-chain; the non-power case differs only by the
   simplification). The monomial-sum gate uses M1 %mr_monomialQ,
   false on constant terms (M1 test "monomialQ 5"), unlike the .m's
   MonomialQ (a_ = constant, n_ defaulting to 0); the skip is a
   no-op — a constant term makes the minimum exponent 0, where
   x^min*(t/x^min) is the identity. */
%mr_normalizeIntegrandFactor(u, x) := block([bas, deg, min],
  if atom(u) then u
  else if %mr_powerQ(u) and freeof(x, part(u, 2)) then (
    bas : %mr_normalizeIntegrandFactorBase(part(u, 1), x),
    deg : part(u, 2),
    if integerp(deg) and %mr_sumQ(bas)
       and %mr_everyQ(lambda([t], %mr_monomialQ(t, x)), args(bas))
    then (
      min : %mr_minimumMonomialExponent(bas, x),
      x^(min*deg)*apply("+",
        map(lambda([t], %mr_simp(t/x^min, [x])), args(bas)))^deg
    )
    else bas^deg
  )
  else if %mr_powerQ(u) and freeof(x, part(u, 1)) then
    part(u, 1)^%mr_normalizeIntegrandFactorBase(part(u, 2), x)
  else (
    bas : %mr_normalizeIntegrandFactorBase(u, x),
    if %mr_sumQ(bas)
       and %mr_everyQ(lambda([t], %mr_monomialQ(t, x)), args(bas))
    then (
      min : %mr_minimumMonomialExponent(bas, x),
      x^min*apply("+", map(lambda([t], %mr_simp(t/x^min, [x])), args(bas)))
    )
    else bas))$

/* Rubi :1999  NormalizeIntegrandAux */
%mr_normalizeIntegrandAux(u, x) := block([mm],
  if %mr_sumQ(u) then
    apply("+", map(lambda([t], %mr_normalizeIntegrandAux(t, x)), args(u)))
  else (
    mm : %mr_mergeMonomials(u, x),
    if not atom(mm) and is(op(mm) = "*") then
      apply("*", map(lambda([f], %mr_normalizeIntegrandFactor(f, x)), args(mm)))
    else %mr_normalizeIntegrandFactor(mm, x)))$

/* Rubi :1988  NormalizeIntegrand. The .m's v===NormalizeLeadTermSigns[u]
   test is syntactic identity; Maxima's structural = is the available
   (weaker) comparison — the chain is value-preserving on the r60
   shapes, so the weaker comparison only risks returning v instead of
   u on an equal-but-different rewrite, which a next call
   re-normalizes to the same form. */
%mr_normalizeIntegrand(u, x) := block([v],
  v : %mr_normalizeLeadTermSigns(%mr_normalizeIntegrandAux(u, x)),
  if is(v = %mr_normalizeLeadTermSigns(u)) then u else v)$

/* ---- class-2 cluster C: the FunctionOfExponential family ----------
 * Pinned definitions (IntegrationUtilityFunctions.m @
 * 61e9c18ea248061cd83c67882f7c91a73cef912d): :4264 (Q), :4270
 * (FunctionOfExponential), :4277 (FunctionOfExponentialFunction),
 * :4284 (FunctionOfExponentialFunctionAux), :4307 (Test), :4322
 * (TestAux). The .m threads three FLUID variables ($base$, $expon$,
 * $exponFlag$) set inside Block; Maxima has no dynamic variables, so the
 * state is threaded explicitly as [flag, F, v] (flag = $exponFlag$,
 * F = $base$, v = $expon$) — the same information, no globals, safe
 * under the runner's re-entrant rule evaluation.
 *
 * Name collision (MEASURED 2026-08-28, 5.50.0/SBCL): the internal
 * exponent variable is named exn, NOT expon — `expon` is a Maxima OPTION
 * variable (the auto-expand cutoff for negative powers, default 0), so
 * `expon : <non-integer>` hard-errors "assignment: 'expon must be a
 * non-negative integer". describe("expon", exact) confirms it.
 *
 * FreeQ short-circuit (.m Test :4308-4309): an x-free u passes the test
 * with the state UNCHANGED (the .m returns True without touching
 * $exponFlag$). Consequences, verified against the .m at the pinned
 * commit (2026-08-28):
 *   * Q :4266 is `Test[u,x] && $exponFlag$`; the FreeQ arm never sets
 *     the flag, so Q(x-free u) = FALSE. The brief's `= true` test
 *     expectation is wrong per the .m — the test is corrected to
 *     `= false` (task-6 report).
 *   * FunctionOfExponential :4273 returns $base$^$expon$ (= Null^Null,
 *     garbage) for x-free u, and FunctionOfExponentialFunction
 *     :4279-4280 is likewise garbage. Both are Q-gated in the .m (Q
 *     false for x-free), so the port returns u (clean decline) for an
 *     x-free u before consulting the state.
 *
 * Factored exponent (MEASURED 2026-08-28, 5.50.0/SBCL): Maxima keeps
 * F^(C*(A+B*X))'s exponent as the PRODUCT node C*(A+B*X) (op "*"), not
 * an expanded sum. M1's %mr_linearQ ACCEPTS the factored linear form
 * (is(%mr_linearQ(C*(A+B*X), X) = true) true; %mr_coeff(C*(A+B*X), X, 1)
 * = B*C) — it expands internally via together/coeff/degree — so no
 * expansion adaptation is needed; the ORIGINAL (factored) expon is what
 * the state stores and what %mr_functionOfExponential returns (the
 * brief's "first call keeps full expon").
 *
 * Exponent sum-split (.m Test :4317-4318, Aux :4301-4302): F^(v1+v2)
 * splits into F^First * F^Rest. Maxima sum storage is degree-descending
 * (args(a+b*X) = [b*X, a]; part(a+b*X, 1) = b*X; rest(a+b*X) = a;
 * MEASURED 2026-08-28 (5.50.0/SBCL)), so part(sum, 1) is the
 * highest-degree term and
 * rest(sum) the remainder — a disjoint partition mirroring the .m's
 * First/Rest in stored order. The %mr_linearQ branch fires BEFORE the
 * sum-split branch, so only NON-linear sum exponents reach the split,
 * and those are refused downstream (F^(A+B*X^2): the X^2 factor defeats
 * %mr_linearQ), so the stored-v difference between split orders is
 * unobservable in the pilot.
 *
 * TestAux fidelity (verified against the .m :4322-4350, 2026-08-28):
 *   * Branch A (:4331-4340) sets $expon$ from the STORED $expon$'s
 *     x-coefficient (:4336 Coefficient[$expon$,x,1]) and the sign flip
 *     (:4337) reads the same — the port uses %mr_coeff(v, x, 1) (v = the
 *     stored expon), correcting the brief (which used the NEW expon's
 *     coefficient). The stored coefficient is what keeps the integrand an
 *     integer power of the returned F^v. The flip's NegQ is the house
 *     %mr_negQ port (Rubi :642) — the documented strict-sign deviation
 *     applies here too.
 *   * Commensurable bases (fix round 1): the .m's :4328/:4331
 *     Log[base]/Log[$base$] factor reduces under FullSimplify to a
 *     rational for commensurable bases (Log[4]/Log[2] -> 2); ratsimp
 *     does NOT (see the FullSimplify note), so %mr_logRatio(base, F)
 *     supplies the exact rational r with base = F^r — 1 for base = F,
 *     false otherwise — for positive-integer bases whose prime
 *     supports match with proportional exponent vectors (factor
 *     prime-power decomposition, cross-multiplied in exact integers:
 *     e_b_i * e_F_1 = e_F_i * e_b_1). ifactor is UNBOUND in this build
 *     (describe: no exact match; MEASURED 2026-08-28 (5.50.0/SBCL)) —
 *     factor(n) is the decomposition (a prime with exponent 1 stays a
 *     bare atom; the factors come out in canonical order). Scope:
 *     positive integers only — positive rationals and other
 *     commensurable shapes remain declined at the RationalQ gate (flag
 *     for layer B / future classes; the class-2 corpus is
 *     integer-base in this pattern).
 *   * The .m's IGtQ base-swap (:4332-4335 / :4341-4344 — adopt the
 *     smaller of two positive-integer bases, then tmp = 1/tmp) is
 *     DROPPED per the brief. Pre fix round 1, commensurable numeric
 *     bases never reached the swap decision: the RationalQ gate
 *     declined them outright (the ratsimp gap above). Now
 *     %mr_logRatio supplies the ratio, so commensurable numeric bases
 *     DO reach the reconciliation; without the swap the port keeps the
 *     FIRST-SEEN base — 2^X*4^X -> 2^X here: args(2^X*4^X) =
 *     [2^X, 4^X] (MEASURED 2026-08-28 (5.50.0/SBCL)) so 2 is
 *     first-seen, and the .m's construction
 *     Coefficient[$expon$,x,1]*x/Denominator[tmp] with tmp = 2 keeps
 *     the stored expon (Denominator[2] = 1) — the same answer the .m
 *     gives for this factor order (its swap fires only on a LATER
 *     smaller base); where a larger base is stored first the .m would
 *     re-base onto the smaller one — a form-only difference, flagged
 *     for layer B (task-6 report).
 *
 * If/or safety (MEASURED 2026-08-28, 5.50.0/SBCL): an if whose condition
 * is `unknown` STAYS A NOUN in this build (it does not take the else
 * arm), so every if/or condition below is a structurally-decidable
 * boolean. is(u = x) is true/false only for structurally distinct forms
 * (is(x^2 = x) = is(a*x = x) = is(x+1 = x) = false; is(x = x) = true);
 * is(part(st, 2) = false) is likewise decidable; is(tmp < 0) sits under
 * the %mr_rationalQ(tmp) gate, so tmp is an explicit rational when it is
 * reached (decidable); the flag is always the literal true/false;
 * is(k # false) is decidable because k is the atom false or an explicit
 * rational (an atom), MEASURED 2026-08-28 (5.50.0/SBCL); the
 * %mr_logRatio guards (is(base = F), is(base > 0), ...) are evaluated
 * only after integerp(base) and integerp(F) short-circuit the and-chain,
 * so they see explicit integers.
 *
 * FunctionExpand: the .m calls it only on Gamma[p, z] with p a positive
 * integer; Maxima does not auto-expand gamma_incomplete(n, z) — for a
 * symbolic z the noun survives (op stays gamma_incomplete; expand and
 * diff leave it, MEASURED 2026-08-28 (5.50.0/SBCL)) — so the port
 * expands Gamma[n, z] = gamma(n) %e^-z sum_{k=0}^{n-1} z^k/k! via the
 * concretizing mr_sum (this file's line 53) and returns other shapes
 * unchanged (decline-safe).
 * FullSimplify: ratsimp — on the Aux shapes (quotients of
 * coefficients) ratsimp preserves the quotient and cancels common
 * factors; the unit test pins the denom behavior. ratsimp does NOT
 * reduce log identities (ratsimp(log(4)/log(2)) stays log(4)/log(2),
 * MEASURED 2026-08-28 (5.50.0/SBCL)) — that gap is what %mr_logRatio
 * covers for commensurable positive-integer bases (see above). */

%mr_fullSimplify(u) := ratsimp(u)$

/* One prime-power factor of factor output as [prime, exponent]. factor
   stores the same prime-power inconsistently (MEASURED 2026-08-28
   (5.50.0/SBCL): factor(4) is the power node 2^2, while the 2^2 factor
   of factor(12) is the ATOM 4, and factor(400)'s factors are the atoms
   16 and 25) — an atom factor is re-factored to recover the prime and
   exponent. A factor of factor(n) is always a single prime power, so
   the re-factoring yields an atom (a prime) or one power node. */
%mr_logFactorExp(p) := block([g],
  if %mr_powerQ(p) then [part(p, 1), part(p, 2)]
  else (
    g : factor(p),
    if atom(g) then [g, 1]
    else [part(g, 1), part(g, 2)]))$

/* The factor prime-power decomposition of an explicit integer n > 1 as
   [[prime, exponent], ...] (factor; ifactor is unbound in this build).
   The factors come out in canonical (ascending-prime) order, so
   position-wise comparison of two decompositions is sound. */
%mr_logFactorPairs(n) := block([g],
  g : factor(n),
  if atom(g) then [[g, 1]]
  else if %mr_powerQ(g) then [[part(g, 1), part(g, 2)]]
  else map(%mr_logFactorExp, args(g)))$

/* The exact rational r with base = F^r, for commensurable positive-
   integer bases; false otherwise (the .m's Log[base]/Log[$base$]
   factor at :4328/:4331, which FullSimplify reduces but ratsimp does
   not). base = F -> 1 before any factorization. The exponent vectors
   must be proportional: with r = e_b_1/e_F_1, cross-multiply in exact
   integers (e_b_i * e_F_1 = e_F_i * e_b_1 for every i). */
%mr_logRatio(base, F) := block([pb, pf, i, ok, r],
  if is(base = F) then 1
  else if not (integerp(base) and integerp(F)
              and is(base > 0) and is(F > 0)
              and not is(base = 1) and not is(F = 1)) then false
  else (
    pb : %mr_logFactorPairs(base),
    pf : %mr_logFactorPairs(F),
    if length(pb) # length(pf) then false
    else (
      ok : true,
      for i : 1 thru length(pb) do
        if not is(part(part(pb, i), 1) = part(part(pf, i), 1))
        then ok : false,
      if not ok then false
      else (
        r : part(part(pb, 1), 2)/part(part(pf, 1), 2),
        ok : true,
        for i : 1 thru length(pb) do
          if not is(part(part(pb, i), 2)*part(part(pf, 1), 2)
                    = part(part(pf, i), 2)*part(part(pb, 1), 2))
          then ok : false,
        if ok then r else false))))$

%mr_foE_test(u, x) := block([], %mr_foE_test2(u, x, [false, false, false]))$

%mr_foE_test2(u, x, st) := block([base, exn, r],
  if freeof(x, u) then st
  else if is(u = x) or %mr_calculusQ(u) then false
  else if %mr_powerQ(u) and freeof(x, part(u, 1))
        and %mr_linearQ(part(u, 2), x) then (
    base : part(u, 1), exn : part(u, 2),
    /* :4313 — $exponFlag$ = True: an explicit F^v power occurred. */
    r : %mr_foE_testAux(base, exn, x, [true, part(st, 2), part(st, 3)]),
    if atom(r) then false else r
  )
  else if %mr_hyperbolicQ(u) and %mr_linearQ(part(u, 1), x) then (
    /* :4315-4316 — hyperbolic of a linear form: base %e, flag
       UNCHANGED (no explicit power). */
    r : %mr_foE_testAux(%e, part(u, 1), x, st),
    if atom(r) then false else r
  )
  else if %mr_powerQ(u) and freeof(x, part(u, 1))
        and %mr_sumQ(part(u, 2)) then (
    /* :4317-4318 — F^(v1 + v2 + …) = F^v1 F^v2 …: test the split
       factors, thread the state. */
    r : %mr_foE_test2(part(u, 1)^part(part(u, 2), 1), x, st),
    if atom(r) then false
    else %mr_foE_test2(part(u, 1)^rest(part(u, 2)), x, r)
  )
  else (
    /* :4319 — Catch[Scan[If[Not[Test[#,x]],Throw[False]],u]; True:
       every subpart must test, state threaded through. `for p in` is the
       list idiom (`for p : lst` parses as `from`, MEASURED
       2026-08-28 (5.50.0/SBCL)). */
    block([st2, p],
      st2 : st,
      for p in args(u) while not atom(st2) do
        st2 : %mr_foE_test2(p, x, st2),
      st2)))$

%mr_foE_testAux(base, exn, x, st) := block([F, v, tmp, e, k],
  if is(part(st, 2) = false) then
    /* :4323-4326 — $base$ === Null: first base seen; take it, keep the
       full expon. */
    [part(st, 1), base, exn]
  else (
    F : part(st, 2), v : part(st, 3),
    /* :4328 — the Log[base]/Log[$base$] factor: the exact rational
       log-ratio for commensurable positive-integer bases, else the
       symbolic ratsimp quotient (which stays a log-quotient for
       distinct numeric bases — the RationalQ gate below declines it).
       is(k # false) is decidable: k is the atom false or an explicit
       rational (an atom), MEASURED 2026-08-28 (5.50.0/SBCL). */
    k : %mr_logRatio(base, F),
    if is(k # false) then
      tmp : k*%mr_coeff(exn, x, 1)/%mr_coeff(v, x, 1)
    else
      tmp : %mr_fullSimplify(log(base)*%mr_coeff(exn, x, 1)
                             /(log(F)*%mr_coeff(v, x, 1))),
    if not %mr_rationalQ(tmp) then false
    else if %mr_eqQ(%mr_coeff(v, x, 0), 0)
           /* :4331 — the constant-term ratio carries the same log
              factor, so the same exact-rational treatment. Its second
              operand divides by %mr_coeff(v, x, 0) — `or`
              short-circuits in this build (MEASURED 2026-08-28
              (5.50.0/SBCL)), so it is evaluated only when that
              coefficient is nonzero. */
           or %mr_neQ(tmp, if is(k # false) then
                          k*%mr_coeff(exn, x, 0)/%mr_coeff(v, x, 0)
                        else
                          %mr_fullSimplify(log(base)*%mr_coeff(exn, x, 0)
                                           /(log(F)*%mr_coeff(v, x, 0))))
    then (
      /* :4331-4340 — the constant-term ratio is inconsistent (or the
         stored expon has no constant term): the common expon is through
         the origin. :4336 reads the STORED expon's x-coefficient. */
      e : %mr_coeff(v, x, 1)*x/denom(tmp),
      /* :4337-4340 — sign flip: tmp < 0 and the stored coefficient < 0
         -> -v. */
      if is(tmp < 0) and %mr_negQ(%mr_coeff(v, x, 1))
      then [part(st, 1), F, -e]
      else [part(st, 1), F, e]
    )
    else (
      /* :4346-4349 — consistent: fold both expons to v/denom(tmp),
         then the :4347-4349 sign flip (tmp < 0 and the stored
         coefficient < 0 -> -v) — the SAME stored-value test as branch
         A (Maxima rational denominators are positive, so the folded
         value carries the stored coefficient's sign). The :4341-4344
         swap is dropped (see header). */
      if is(tmp < 0) and %mr_negQ(%mr_coeff(v, x, 1))
      then [part(st, 1), F, -(v/denom(tmp))]
      else [part(st, 1), F, v/denom(tmp)])))$

%mr_functionOfExponentialQ(u, x) := block([st],
  st : %mr_foE_test(u, x),
  is(not atom(st) and part(st, 1) = true))$

%mr_functionOfExponential(u, x) := block([st],
  if freeof(x, u) then u
  else (
    st : %mr_foE_test(u, x),
    if atom(st) then u
    else part(st, 2)^part(st, 3)))$

%mr_functionOfExponentialFunction(u, x) := block([st],
  if freeof(x, u) then u
  else (
    st : %mr_foE_test(u, x),
    if atom(st) then u
    else %mr_simp(%mr_foEFunctionAux(u, x, st), [x])))$

%mr_foEFunctionAux(u, x, st) := block([F, v, tmp],
  F : part(st, 2), v : part(st, 3),
  if atom(u) then u
  else if %mr_powerQ(u) and freeof(x, part(u, 1))
        and %mr_linearQ(part(u, 2), x) then (
    /* :4287-4290 — u = F0^(m x + c0) -> (F0/F)^… X^ratio: the .m's two
       branches on EqQ[Coefficient[$expon$, x, 0], 0]. */
    if %mr_eqQ(%mr_coeff(v, x, 0), 0) then
      part(u, 1)^%mr_coeff(part(u, 2), x, 0)
      *x^%mr_fullSimplify(log(part(u, 1))*%mr_coeff(part(u, 2), x, 1)
                          /(log(F)*%mr_coeff(v, x, 1)))
    else
      x^%mr_fullSimplify(log(part(u, 1))*%mr_coeff(part(u, 2), x, 1)
                         /(log(F)*%mr_coeff(v, x, 1)))
  )
  else if %mr_hyperbolicQ(u) and %mr_linearQ(part(u, 1), x) then (
    /* :4291-4300 — the .m's Switch on Head[u]: sinh/cosh/tanh/coth/sech/
       csch of the tmp = X^(coeff ratio) form. */
    tmp : x^%mr_fullSimplify(%mr_coeff(part(u, 1), x, 1)
                             /(log(F)*%mr_coeff(v, x, 1))),
    if is(op(u) = sinh) then tmp/2 - 1/(2*tmp)
    else if is(op(u) = cosh) then tmp/2 + 1/(2*tmp)
    else if is(op(u) = tanh) then (tmp - 1/tmp)/(tmp + 1/tmp)
    else if is(op(u) = coth) then (tmp + 1/tmp)/(tmp - 1/tmp)
    else if is(op(u) = sech) then 2/(tmp + 1/tmp)
    else if is(op(u) = csch) then 2/(tmp - 1/tmp)
    else u
  )
  else if %mr_powerQ(u) and freeof(x, part(u, 1))
        and %mr_sumQ(part(u, 2)) then (
    /* :4301-4302 — F^(v1+v2) -> the product of the rewrites. */
    %mr_foEFunctionAux(part(u, 1)^part(part(u, 2), 1), x, st)
    *%mr_foEFunctionAux(part(u, 1)^rest(part(u, 2)), x, st)
  )
  else (
    /* :4303 — Map[Function[FunctionOfExponentialFunctionAux[#,x]],u]
       preserves the head over ANY non-atomic head; op/args
       round-trips it (M1 precedent maxima_rubi_utils.mac:3811, the
       Task-5 FactorBase fix). The r104 call site is conditioned on
       FunctionOfExponentialQ[u,x] alone, so a Q-true input with any
       head must be rewritten — the old * / +-only arms returned u
       unchanged for other heads, a silent wrong rewrite (sin(F^(b x))
       must become sin(x)). Comma-form lambda (the colon form is a
       parse error) and no slash-star opener sequence inside a comment
       (Maxima nests comments), MEASURED 2026-08-28 (5.50.0/SBCL). */
    apply(op(u), map(lambda([p], %mr_foEFunctionAux(p, x, st)), args(u)))))$

%mr_functionExpand(u) := block([n, z],
  if not atom(u) and is(op(u) = gamma_incomplete)
     and length(args(u)) = 2 then (
    n : part(args(u), 1), z : part(args(u), 2),
    if integerp(n) and is(n > 0) then
      /* Gamma[n, z] = gamma(n) %e^-z sum_{k=0}^{n-1} z^k/k! — mr_sum
         concretizes the numeric bounds (this file's line 53). */
      gamma(n)*exp(-z)*mr_sum(z^k/factorial(k), 'k, 0, n - 1)
    else u
  )
  else u)$

mr_witness_utils() := true$
